+ "why_this_file_exists": "The 2026-11-17 brief makes measurement the first job: 'Turn on click and conversion measurement before writing anything else', with a privacy-preserving outbound-click counter, a self-hosted analytics endpoint, five defined events, and an empty but well-defined campaign ledger. The brief also states the rule the ledger is built around: figures may be added 'only when obtained from authorized first-party reports'. This file is the contract — the event definitions, the collector's posture, the ledger schema and the deployment boundary. It contains no traffic data, because none exists yet.",
+ "what_this_is_not": "This is not an analytics dashboard and it does not report a single measured reader. Every count on the measurement page is either zero, blank, or a number held in the reading device's own storage. Anyone who reads a number here as site-wide performance has misread the page, which is why the page says so in its first paragraph.",
+ "posture": {
+  "collector": "first-party, browser-local",
+  "what_runs": "A click counter in the page's own script. It increments a tally in this browser's localStorage when a reader follows an outbound product link, and it notes which page the click came from. The measurement page reads the same tally back on request.",
+  "what_does_not_run": "No cookie is set. No fetch(), beacon, pixel, XHR or third-party script is called by the collector, so nothing leaves the reader's browser. No fingerprinting, no user or session identifier, no full destination query string, no IP address, and nothing is written to Amazon's Program Content.",
+  "endpoint": null,
+  "endpoint_status": "not deployed — and it cannot be deployed on this host. GitHub Pages serves static files only, so there is nowhere for a browser to POST an event. The collector's endpoint constant is unset at build time, which is why the counter is local-only and why the ledger stays empty.",
+  "deployment_boundary": "Before any count on a reader's device can become a number about readers, four things have to exist: (1) a first-party endpoint on infrastructure the owner controls that accepts a POST and stores counts; (2) a retention and IP-handling policy for that endpoint, written down; (3) the privacy statement on this site updated from 'stored only on your device' to whatever the endpoint actually does, which is a Program Policies section 3(e) obligation and, for EU or UK readers, potentially a consent obligation; (4) a named person who reads the numbers. Until (1) exists, shipping the endpoint code would be a form with no mailbox.",
+  "why_not_just_third_party": "A hosted analytics script is the fast option and it is the one this project declines on its own evidence: section 3(e) requires an accurate statement of cookie and third-party technology use, the current notice says none are used, and a third-party script would make that sentence false the moment it loaded. The choice is not 'privacy vs measurement', it is 'which sentence the site has to rewrite'.",
+  "sources": [
+   {
+    "id": "S2",
+    "used_for": "Program Policies section 3(e) (cookie and third-party technology disclosure) and section 6(t)/2(b) (what Program Content may be used for)"
