-  <tbody><tr id="f1"><td class="num"><strong>F1</strong></td><td>ASIN → manufacturer model number: recorded for ten passes as not published by Amazon. Closed on 2026-11-16 for two of three products — see F49 and F68.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three</div></td><td class="small">HISTORY KEPT, BECAUSE THE PREMISE WAS THE DEFECT: this row said the model number could not be read from the listing, so each product&#x27;s model was published as a documented match with a caveat, and F49 recorded that a machine could not read it at all. On 2026-11-16 session arena/ba29c641 fetched all three /dp/ pages and each Product information block rendered. B0CDY277D3 states Model Number and Part Number RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W — the brand page&#x27;s and manual&#x27;s string, so that mapping is now sourced twice. B08DKYBTPH states Part Number RJ38-SQSS-8T (its Model Number row holds the marketing string &#x27;Large Air Fryer&#x27;). B09B7SB46R states 4-00675-02X-VN, one character away from the brand page&#x27;s 4-00675-02A-VN, and that conflict stays open (F70). Still open, unchanged by this pass: whether the supplied short links point where the account holder intended (F6, F7, F10), which is why the caveat on the *link* half of the mapping is not removed.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a></td></tr><tr id="f6"><td class="num"><strong>F6</strong></td><td>The Associates tag inside the three link.amazon short links is not observable.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three links</div></td><td class="small">The shortener hides the tag. Attribution cannot be verified from outside Associates Central, so &#x27;associates_tag_confirmed&#x27; is false for all three links. Needs the account holder to open Manage Tracking IDs.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a></td></tr><tr id="f7"><td class="num"><strong>F7</strong></td><td>Program Policies §6(w) (Content on your Site) prohibits link shortening that makes it unclear you are linking to an Amazon Site.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three links</div></td><td class="small">link.amazon keeps the Amazon name visible, so the risk is low, but the landing URL is generated and temporary. Recorded so the account holder can confirm current guidance before publishing.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f8"><td class="num"><strong>F8</strong></td><td>IP License §2(e) forbids aggregating, analysing or repurposing Program Content and training models on it.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Platform design</div></td><td class="small">Any AI research or trend scoring therefore runs only on first-party data and public manufacturer data. Documented as guardrail G6 in the blueprint.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f9"><td class="num"><strong>F9</strong></td><td>No Amazon price, star rating or review count is available to this build.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three</div></td><td class="small">Left blank everywhere by policy rather than by omission. Becomes fillable only if the operator secures Creators/PA API credentials.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GBRCD467W33NMWLD" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — New Product Advertising API (PA API) Registration" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S19</a></td></tr><tr id="f10"><td class="num"><strong>F10</strong></td><td>The short links are generated redirects with fallback parameters, not plain product links.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three links</div></td><td class="small">Logged redirect observations carried lv=shuf and plpRedirect=mhFallback. The candidate ASIN/title destinations were consistent across the recorded checks, but the btn_ref/srctok value changed between repeat or cache-busted requests (see F41 and the linked session evidence). These are observations of redirect responses, not confirmation that the candidate ASINs are the products intended by the account holder or that the links carry an Associates tag. Confirm both before publishing.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a></td></tr><tr id="f11"><td class="num"><strong>F11</strong></td><td>The site does not disclose cookies, pixels and third-party technologies as Participation Requirements §3(e) requires.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Whole site</div></td><td class="small">The site itself sets no cookies and runs no analytics, but §3(e) requires an accurate disclosure of cookie and third-party technology use, including that third parties may place or recognise cookies. Only a minimal notice is in the footer; a proper privacy/cookie statement is needed before launch. Legal review recommended.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f12"><td class="num"><strong>F12</strong></td><td>The automated platform functions in the brief are not running — by design, not by omission.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Platform</div></td><td class="small">Automated trend detection, competition scoring and conversion optimisation need an Associates account, Creators/PA API credentials and sales reports. PA API access is registered through Associates Central and credentials are tied to the primary account holder, with request rate scaled to account sales performance. None of that exists in this repository, and inventing the data to fill the gap would defeat the point of the project.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GBRCD467W33NMWLD" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — New Product Advertising API (PA API) Registration" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S19</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GMEDADBTCJ9KD8DQ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — About the Product Advertising API (topic index)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S20</a></td></tr><tr id="f13"><td class="num"><strong>F13</strong></td><td>Product listing titles are reproduced from Amazon; §6(a) restricts altering Program Content.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three</div></td><td class="small">Titles are shown verbatim and unedited. §6(a) permits truncation only where meaning is not materially altered, and whether a listing title counts as Program Content is not stated. Recorded so the operator can confirm before adding any paraphrase.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f14"><td class="num"><strong>F14</strong></td><td>The landing page overstated product resolution (corrected 2026-11-14).<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Root landing page</div></td><td class="small">The headline read &#x27;3 products resolved — all three short links followed to an ASIN&#x27;. The 2026-11-14 check found the short links reach Amazon fallback pages and the bare 9-character codes return &#x27;Page Not Found&#x27;. The headline now reads &#x27;0 verified product identities&#x27; and the ASINs are labelled as unconfirmed candidates. Evidence: reports/link-check-2026-11-14.md.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a></td></tr><tr id="f15"><td class="num"><strong>F15</strong></td><td>Plain ASIN URLs were also answered with Amazon&#x27;s fallback form on 2026-11-14.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three ASINs</div></td><td class="small">amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3, /dp/B09B7SB46R and /dp/B08DKYBTPH each returned a URL carrying lv=shuf and plpRedirect=mhFallback, so a fetch cannot show that the ASIN is the live product page. Confirm from a logged-in browser before any ASIN is treated as the supplied product.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a></td></tr><tr id="f16"><td class="num"><strong>F16</strong></td><td>link.amazon is not confirmed as an Amazon-documented short-link domain.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three links</div></td><td class="small">A web search on 2026-11-14 returned only third-party blogs naming amzn.to and a.co. No official Amazon page listing link.amazon was found. An official Associates help page is needed before the domain is described as Amazon&#x27;s.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a></td></tr><tr id="f17"><td class="num"><strong>F17</strong></td><td>Advisory link gate: table rows with no source link on inherited annex pages.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: dashboard/, site/, research/, session-3740f69d/ annexes</div></td><td class="small">Root pages generated by build.py were given a source badge, policy citation or flagged-page link in the arena/0c7445c7 pass (2026-11-14). Remaining advisory rows lived only in other sessions&#x27; annex builds; scripts/check_links.py reported them as non-blocking warnings. **CLOSED 2026-11-14 by sess-7111e2b6** along with F25: a row-source pass now runs inside the research/ and site/ generators, and scripts/source_rows.py applies it to the hand-authored annex builds. The advisory list is empty, and the gate&#x27;s own definition was corrected first, so that it can see single-quoted hrefs and every flagged-page filename.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f18"><td class="num"><strong>F18</strong></td><td>Committed s2 build stamp changes with the calendar date.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: s2/ (session zone1-s2 build)</div></td><td class="small">scripts/build_s2_site.py writes today&#x27;s date into the &#x27;built&#x27; stamp. Re-running on 2026-11-14 changes 13 files by that stamp alone (committed stamp: 2026-11-13). The CI gate regenerates before it checks, so it passes, but the committed copy is not pinned. Not changed in this pass because another session owns the file.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f19"><td class="num"><strong>F19</strong></td><td>Brand-site review counts move between days, so every rating on this site is a dated observation.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three</div></td><td class="small">Chefman&#x27;s own widget read 1,302 reviews on 2026-11-13 and 1,305 on 2026-11-14; the 8 Qt page read 715 then 716. A third read on 2026-11-16 moved them again — 1,307 and 717 — while both averages held at 4.6 for the third consecutive time. The averages did not move (4.6 on every read), but a published figure without its read date would silently become false. All three counts now carry all three dates in the dataset, and any copy generated from it must carry the date too.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a></td></tr><tr id="f20"><td class="num"><strong>F20</strong></td><td>The repository carries six parallel builds, and two of them were unreachable from the landing page until the 2026-11-14 integration pass.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Whole repo</div></td><td class="small">s2/, site/, dashboard/, research/, session-3740f69d/ and docs/ are independent session outputs with their own data, their own verifiers and their own verdicts; they disagree in places (for example on whether the commission rate can be stated, and on how to read the plpRedirect=mhFallback marker). The pass linked the two orphaned builds into the navigation and changed none of their content. The owner should decide which build is canonical before this points at a real domain — a live site with six home pages is a maintenance and trust problem, not a data problem.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/s2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">s2/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/site" target="_blank" rel="noopener">site/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/dashboard" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dashboard/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/research" target="_blank" rel="noopener">research/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/session-3740f69d" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-3740f69d/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">docs/ ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f23"><td class="num"><strong>F23</strong></td><td>One documented build command in the README no longer runs against the shared data — it is a pre-existing break, not a regression from this pass.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: scripts/build.mjs → docs/</div></td><td class="small">The Node builder that generated the retired docs/ build validates a schema (products with official_url / values, sources with a status field, compliance.rows, flags) that data/products.json, data/sources.json, data/compliance.json and data/flags.json no longer use, so &#x27;node scripts/build.mjs --check&#x27; throws ERR_INVALID_URL on undefined. Verified identical on the pre-change baseline, and no CI workflow calls it — pages.yml, ci.yml and verify.yml are Python-only. docs/ therefore stays frozen as committed and cannot be regenerated from current data. Not repaired here: re-authoring another session&#x27;s generator to a new schema would silently change what its published pages assert. Either retire docs/ and its README line, or point the builder at data/verified/. This pass added no dependency on it.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/scripts/build.mjs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">scripts/build.mjs ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f22"><td class="num"><strong>F22</strong></td><td>A committed merge-conflict marker was found in a published document and repaired by this pass.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md</div></td><td class="small">The file carried a bare &#x27;=======&#x27; line and a &#x27;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; origin/main&#x27; line on main, i.e. a previous integration resolved a conflict by leaving the markers in. Both sides are real work by different sessions, so this pass removed only the markers and kept both documents as separate sections. Any other file carrying markers should be treated the same way — delete the marker, never the work.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f21"><td class="num"><strong>F21</strong></td><td>Two verification passes on the same links reached different conclusions, and both are published.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: The three supplied links</div></td><td class="small">arena/1dcaaafc (reports/link-check-2026-11-14.md) reads the fallback marker plus the 9-character-token 404 as &#x27;product identity not confirmed&#x27; and removed the &#x27;products resolved&#x27; headline. sess-81495d2d (reverification.html) re-tested with a repeat request and a nonsense-token control: the target was identical across four sessions on two days, while an unrecognised token returns 403 instead of a product page, which is not what a pure not-found fallback would do. The reconciled position on this site: the *destination* of the links is reproducible and is documented; the *identity* of the product the operator meant, and the Associates tag attached to the link, are not confirmed and are not asserted. Confirm both in a logged-in browser and in Associates Central before publishing.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — Product-detail lookup for the supplied 9-character token (negative control)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S21</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/ZZZ9ZZZZZ" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon resolver, nonsense-token control request" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S22</a></td></tr><tr id="f24"><td class="num"><strong>F24</strong></td><td>PA-API 5 is officially deprecated (confirmed live 2026-11-14); the successor is Amazon&#x27;s Creators API.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Platform design</div></td><td class="small">The official docs URL now serves a deprecation notice: PA-API 5 calls return 403 AccessDeniedException and integrations are told to migrate to the Creators API. This supersedes the earlier board retraction of the cross-check&#x27;s F18 claim and means every automated feature in the blueprint must target the Creators API, not PA-API 5. The Program Policies phrase their data-use rules as &#x27;Creators API or PA API&#x27;, so the compliance quotes remain accurate. Independently noted in the dashboard annex as H-01.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/creatorsapi/docs/en-us/paapiv5-deprecation" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Creators API documentation — PA-API 5 Deprecation Notice" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S24</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GMEDADBTCJ9KD8DQ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — About the Product Advertising API (topic index)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S20</a></td></tr><tr id="f25"><td class="num"><strong>F25</strong></td><td>Table rows on inherited builds have no link to a registered official source.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: dashboard/, research/, site/, s2/, session-3740f69d/, annex-88769516/</div></td><td class="small">Audited 2026-11-14 with the project&#x27;s own official-host list (every host in data/sources.json, plus this repo on GitHub). Root pages generated by build.py now pass, and the build fails on any unsourced row. Inherited builds were not edited by that pass because other sessions own them. Count at 2026-11-14: 281 of 1,363 table rows in the repository have no sourced link (inherited builds: 281 of 1,057; root pages built by build.py: 0 of 306). **CLOSED 2026-11-14 by sess-7111e2b6.** The residue is now 0. Two things were wrong with the count itself: the link gate could not see single-quoted hrefs, and it recognised only one filename for the flagged page, so 33 rows that did carry a source link were being reported as unsourced. With the gate corrected, the true figure was 248 rows. A row-source pass was then added inside the research/ and site/ generators, and scripts/source_rows.py applies the same pass to the three hand-authored builds (dashboard/, annex-88769516/, session-3740f69d/). Rows that state a fact now link to that fact&#x27;s official document; rows that are judgements, blocked metrics or unverified values link to the flagged page, which is where the project puts unconfirmed values. The gate also now rejects rows that link only to a relative internal page.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/scripts/check_links.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">scripts/check_links.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/dashboard" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dashboard/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/research" target="_blank" rel="noopener">research/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/site" target="_blank" rel="noopener">site/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/s2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">s2/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/session-3740f69d" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-3740f69d/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/annex-88769516" target="_blank" rel="noopener">annex-88769516/ ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f26"><td class="num"><strong>F26</strong></td><td>Two platform KPIs are internal design targets; no official source states them.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: platform.html, KPI table: Verified-row coverage, Flag resolution time</div></td><td class="small">Shown with this flag, not with an invented source. They are operating targets for this project, not facts about Amazon, the FTC or any product, and they feed no published product value. The owner should decide whether to keep them as targets.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/platform.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/platform.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f27"><td class="num"><strong>F27</strong></td><td>Policy citations for the Participation Requirements &#x27;Content on your Site&#x27; items used section numbers that do not match the live page.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Root-site content (corrected in this pass); site/ annex pages still carry the old labels</div></td><td class="small">Live reads of affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies on 2026-11-14 (two independent renders; version dated April 14, 2026) place the customer-review/star-rating rule at Participation Requirements &#x27;6. Content on your Site&#x27; item (t), and items (g) incentives, (n) framing, (u) self-purchase, (v) cloaking, (w) link shortening and (y) price tracking in the same section. Repo content had cited these as §3(t), §3(g), §3(n), §3(u), §3(v), §2(w) and §3(y). Every quoted rule text was verified verbatim against the live page, so no published behaviour changes — only the citation labels were aligned to the live structure in build.py, data/compliance.json, data/products.json, data/sources.json, data/platform.json, data/marketing.json, README.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY.md and EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md. Spot-checked labels that were already correct: §2(b) prices/availability, §3(e) cookies, §6(a) alteration, IP License §2(e) aggregation/ML-training, Operating Agreement §5 disclosure. The site/ annex (session 1874bb64) still renders §3(t) from data/verified/; its owner session should align it (request logged in COORDINATION.md). Historical session records in COORDINATION.md were left as written.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f28"><td class="num"><strong>F28</strong></td><td>Three flag registers share overlapping ID spaces but index different issues, and two published documents cite the wrong one.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Cross-build consistency</div></td><td class="small">Confirmed 2026-11-14 by sess-91d0552e (renumbered F25 -&gt; F28 to avoid a collision with arena/6ecdb4e4&#x27;s F25). (a) research/build_research.py tells the reader to see &#x27;the root site&#x27;s Flagged page (shared register data/flags.json id F19)&#x27; for the product-titles/§6(a) issue and &#x27;id F20&#x27; for the cookie-disclosure issue. Those IDs ARE correct inside data/flags.json, but the root Flagged page is generated from build.py&#x27;s own PROGRAMME_FLAGS, where F19 is &#x27;brand-site review counts move between days&#x27; and F20 is &#x27;the repository carries six parallel builds&#x27;. A reader following that pointer lands on an unrelated row. (b) EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md, still served at the site root, asserts &#x27;Identity verified. All three short links resolve to Amazon.com product pages with different ASINs&#x27; and that &#x27;the official commission schedule redirected to a sign-in page&#x27;. Both are contradicted by this pass: the reconciled position published on this site is 0 verified product identities (F14-F16, F21), and the Standard Commission Income Statement was read in full, without any login, on 2026-11-14. Not repaired in this pass: data/flags.json is consumed by the s2 and docs generators and research/ is another session&#x27;s build, so re-keying either register would silently re-point another build&#x27;s citations. Decision needed: adopt one register as canonical and re-key the others, or relabel the pointers. Evidence: session-91d0552e/VERIFICATION-2026-11-14.md §5.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a></td></tr><tr id="f29"><td class="num"><strong>F29</strong></td><td>The Emeril preset count is stated five different ways across official sources, and two of them now say 24.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Emeril 26 QT French Door</div></td><td class="small">Re-read 2026-11-14 by sess-54c66535. Amazon&#x27;s listing title for ASIN B09B7SB46R says &#x27;24 cooking functions&#x27; and Emeril&#x27;s own description paragraph says &#x27;Equipped with 24 one-touch cooking presets&#x27;, while the same page&#x27;s specification block says &#x27;25 Cooking Pre-sets&#x27;, its feature heading says &#x27;10-In-1&#x27; and one of its carousel assets is named &#x27;11-in-1&#x27;. Two independent official sources now agreeing on 24 is new evidence: earlier passes recorded only 25 vs 10-in-1 vs 11-in-1 with no tiebreaker. The conflict is narrowed, not closed - the same brand page still contradicts itself - so no preset count is asserted anywhere on this site. Decision needed: confirm the figure against the unit&#x27;s own control panel before any copy states a number. &lt;strong&gt;Re-read 2026-11-15 by arena/88bb2db6, which changes that last sentence.&lt;/strong&gt; The owner&#x27;s manual for FAFO-001 was read row by row: its Preset Mode Chart and its Recommended Accessory Positions table each list exactly 24 presets by name, and the manual never states the numeral. The Amazon listing title independently says &#x27;24 One-Touch Preset Options&#x27;. The site now states 24 on the strength of the manual, names the one official line that disagrees (the brand page&#x27;s specification block, &#x27;25 Cooking Pre-sets&#x27;), and flags it. This row and F4 both stay open, but the site no longer asserts nothing. The 10-in-1 and 11-in-1 labels remain marketing wording and are not presented as specifications. Decision still needed: confirm the figure against the unit&#x27;s own control panel.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-54c66535/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-54c66535/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f30"><td class="num"><strong>F30</strong></td><td>Two published specification values are not stated by the official source the site cites for them.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket (preset count); Emeril 26 QT French Door (interior light)</div></td><td class="small">Re-read 2026-11-14 by sess-54c66535. The Chefman dual-window user guide RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W was read end to end and documents HI-FRY, +2 MIN, MUTE, SYNC BASKETS, SYNC FINISH, START/STOP, TIME/TEMP and SHAKE, but has no preset chart and never names the four food presets the site publishes; its control panel is artwork with no text layer. The Emeril brand page&#x27;s specification block lists Timer, 26-Quart Capacity, Dual-Speed Fan, 25 Cooking Pre-sets, dimensions, 24.6 Liters, Temperature Control and 120 volts, with no interior-light row. Both values were left as published rather than deleted, because deleting another session&#x27;s sourced row is not this pass&#x27;s call, but neither is currently traceable to an official statement. Decision needed: re-source both to the brand product pages, or blank them and let them appear here as unverified.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0576/5755/7158/files/RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W-UG-US.pdf" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry Touch Dual Window Air Fryer RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W User Guide (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S18</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-54c66535/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-54c66535/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-54c66535/pass6.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-54c66535/pass6.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f31"><td class="num"><strong>F31</strong></td><td>The two Chefman 6 Qt sister SKUs carry very different review counts on the brand&#x27;s own site.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket</div></td><td class="small">Re-read 2026-11-14 by sess-54c66535. The dual-basket page RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2 reports 4.6 stars from 194 reviews; the dual-window page RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W reports 4.6 stars from 1,305 reviews, on an identical list price and promo ladder. The 6.7x gap is a usable signal for deciding which SKU the supplied ASIN maps to, which is open flag F2. It is not an identification: a review count is not a product identifier. No SKU is asserted. Correction 2026-11-16 (session arena/ba29c641): this row also stated that &#x27;Amazon does not publish the model number behind an ASIN&#x27;, which is false — the listing&#x27;s Product information block publishes it (F49, F68) — so a review-count comparison is no longer the nearest thing to a route: the model number itself has been read, and it matches the -2W page this site maps to.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-6-qt-dual-basket-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Basket Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S11</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-54c66535/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-54c66535/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f32"><td class="num"><strong>F32</strong></td><td>Chefman sells three different 6-quart dual-basket SKUs; the site names two of them.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket</div></td><td class="small">Found live 2026-11-14 by arena/6b2eddeb. Chefman&#x27;s own store currently publishes RJ38-3DBW-V2 (&#x27;TurboFry 6 Qt. Double Basket Air Fryer with Easy View Windows&#x27;: 6 Qt / 5.68 L as two 3 Qt / 2.84 L baskets, 90–400 °F / 32–204 °C, 1700 W, five functions — air fry, bake, reheat, dehydrate, frozen — easy-view windows, interior lights, shake notifications, 4.5 stars from 30 reviews, $99.99 list), RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2 (dual-basket, 4.6 from 194, no specification block published) and RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W (dual-window, 200–450 °F, Hi-Fry, 4.6 from 1,305). The site records the -2W mapping because the Amazon title says &#x27;Hi-Fry&#x27; and &#x27;Easy View Windows&#x27;, and Hi-Fry is documented only on the -2W page — but Easy View windows appear on all three, so the title does not discriminate on its own. This is an identification risk, not an identification. Two other builds shipped in this repo (site/, research/) map the same ASIN to RJ38-3DBW-V2 with no caveat, so a reader can reach two different official-looking answers two clicks apart. Decision needed: open the listing, read the model plate or the &#x27;Product information&#x27; model number, and settle it once. **Addendum 2026-11-16 (session arena/ba29c641): the read was done, by fetch.** The listing&#x27;s Product information block states Model Number and Part Number RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W — the root site&#x27;s mapping, not the site/ and research/ mapping to RJ38-3DBW-V2. Those two builds now disagree with a document each of them can read; they are not edited here because those trees belong to other sessions, and the divergence is another reason to retire or re-key them (F44). See F68.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-6-qt-double-basket-air-fryer-with-easy-view-windows" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry 6 Qt. Double Basket Air Fryer with Easy View Windows (RJ38-3DBW-V2) — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S25</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-6-qt-dual-basket-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry® Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Basket Air Fryer (RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2) — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S27</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-6b2eddeb/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-6b2eddeb/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/site" target="_blank" rel="noopener">site/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/research" target="_blank" rel="noopener">research/ ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f33"><td class="num"><strong>F33</strong></td><td>The &#x27;Hi-Fry / 450 °F: None&#x27; row for the 8 Qt product is only true of the stainless SKU, and a second official SKU matches the Amazon title exactly.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry</div></td><td class="small">Found live 2026-11-14 by arena/6b2eddeb — narrows F3, does not resolve it. Chefman sells RJ38-SQSS-8T (TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel, max 400 °F / 205 °C, four food presets, stainless finish — the SKU the site currently maps) and RJ38-8T-BLACK (TurboFry 8 Qt Air Fryer w/ High Fry: Hi-Fry 450 °F / 232 °C for the final two minutes, and exactly four functions — air fry, bake, dehydrate, frozen — in a matte-black finish). The Amazon listing title for B08DKYBTPH reads &#x27;…4-in-1 with 450°F Hi-Fry Option… | Bake, Dehydrate, Frozen | … Stainless Steel&#x27;. The function list and the 450 °F Hi-Fry match RJ38-8T-BLACK word for word; the finish matches RJ38-SQSS-8T. So the title may describe a third variant, or may carry a finish that does not match the unit. The previous framing — &#x27;one of the two official sources is stale&#x27; — is now the less likely explanation. No value is changed and neither model is asserted; a human should read the model number printed in the listing&#x27;s Product information section. **Read 2026-11-16 by session arena/ba29c641, by fetch:** the listing states Part Number RJ38-SQSS-8T *and* Max Temperature Setting 400 °F — its identifier field points at the 400 °F model while its title describes the -BLACK&#x27;s Hi-Fry capability. That is a listing carrying a title from somewhere else, which is not the same as proof of which machine ships: because RJ38-8T-BLACK genuinely documents 450 °F (S26), the two-models explanation this row opened stays live and both readings are published.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-8-qt-air-fryer-w-high-fry" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry 8 Qt. Air Fryer w/ High Fry (RJ38-8T-BLACK) — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S26</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-6b2eddeb/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-6b2eddeb/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f34"><td class="num"><strong>F34</strong></td><td>The second executive summary at the repo root still asserts two things that are now known to be wrong.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-14 by arena/6b2eddeb. EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md (the older of the two root summaries) states in its Key findings: &#x27;Identity verified. All three short links resolve to Amazon.com product pages with different ASINs&#x27; and &#x27;the official commission schedule redirected to a sign-in page&#x27;. This session re-fetched both on 2026-11-14: the Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 and Bounty Events) was read in full with no login, and product identity is explicitly NOT verified anywhere on this site (F14–F16, F21, F32, F33). A dated correction banner was placed above that document by an earlier pass, but the contradicting sentences remain in the body and the file is still served from the site root by direct link. Decision needed: retire the duplicate document, or rewrite the two sentences. This pass did not edit it — the sentence is another session&#x27;s published work and rewriting it is a content decision, not a verification one.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f35"><td class="num"><strong>F35</strong></td><td>Nine separate HTML sites for the same three products are published in this repository, and they disagree with each other.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Whole repository</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-14 by arena/6b2eddeb. Alongside the canonical root build there are research/ (12 pages), session-3740f69d/ (11), s2/ (13), site/ (11), dashboard/ (14), docs/ (7) and annex-88769516/ (10), plus data-only trees in crosscheck/ and platform/. The root navigation links to six of them. They disagree on facts a reader would take as settled: the Chefman 6 Qt model (RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W on the root site, RJ38-3DBW-V2 in site/ and research/ — F32), and the policy section numbers for the review/star-rating and link-shortening rules (§6(t)/§6(w) on the root site after F27, §3(t)/§2(w) in site/ and session-3740f69d/). Each build is internally consistent and individually verified; the problem is that nothing tells a reader which one is canonical when they arrive from a search engine. Decision needed: keep one site and archive the rest behind a clearly labelled &#x27;previous verification passes&#x27; page, or make every non-canonical page carry a fixed banner naming the canonical build.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/site" target="_blank" rel="noopener">site/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/session-3740f69d" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-3740f69d/ ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f41"><td class="num"><strong>F41</strong></td><td>F10&#x27;s earlier wording incorrectly said the short links&#x27; srctok marker stays constant across repeat requests.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three links — historical wording correction</div></td><td class="small">Closed 2026-11-15 by arena/94a562a1-zone1: F10 now states that the recorded candidate ASIN/title destinations were consistent while btn_ref/srctok values changed across some repeat or cache-busted requests. The underlying records include srctok-2cef40d2904c29d8 on 2026-11-13; srctok-1e229870efde5acf changing to srctok-397b5ba66db39bbf on a cache-busted repeat (session-81495d2d evidence, row 4); srctok-704dc575b940a94c (session-54c66535); and srctok-0740a2e990c9bbb3 in two observations later that day. This closes only the wording mismatch. It does not prove that any candidate listing is the product intended by the account holder or confirm affiliate attribution; see F1, F6, F14-F16 and F21.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-81495d2d/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-81495d2d/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-ce4070ad/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-ce4070ad/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f37"><td class="num"><strong>F37</strong></td><td>A published value was cited to a source that does not state it.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Emeril 26 QT French Door — the &quot;Interior light: Yes&quot; row</div></td><td class="small">Found and fixed 2026-11-14 by sess-7111e2b6. The row cited the brand product page (S14), whose specification strip lists Timer, 26-Quart Capacity, Dual-Speed Fan, 25 Cooking Pre-sets, 16.9&quot;D x 15.8&quot;W x 14.8&quot;H, 24.6 Liters, Temperature Control and 120 volts — and no interior light. The value is nonetheless correct: the owner&#x27;s manual (S15) documents it in the control-panel section, &quot;F. LIGHT BUTTON: May be selected at any time during the cooking process to light the appliance&#x27;s interior.&quot; The row has been re-sourced from S14 to S15 and the manual&#x27;s wording recorded in the row note. The lesson is published rather than quietly fixed: a value can be true and still be mis-cited, and a citation that does not support its value is as much a defect as an invented number.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0687/4388/8948/files/FAFO-001_VN_32451_EF_IB_T22-5004282-C_1.pdf" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Owner&#x27;s Manual, model FAFO-001 (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S15</a></td></tr><tr id="f38"><td class="num"><strong>F38</strong></td><td>An Amazon listing title changed between the 2026-11-14 read and 2026-11-15, so a published value was stale.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Emeril 26 QT French Door — the listing-title row</div></td><td class="small">Found and fixed 2026-11-15 by arena/3ddfa50e. The stored title ended &#x27;| 26QT air fryer oven with French doors, 24 cooking functions, dual-speed fan and rotisserie for easy family meals.&#x27; and the live title on the same ASIN (B09B7SB46R, reached through https://link.amazon/B02NbDxp3) now ends &#x27;| Countertop Convection Cooker, 24 One-Touch Preset Options, Extra Large Interior, Six Cooking Accessories Included&#x27;. The preset count is unchanged at 24; the wording around it is not. The row now carries the 2026-11-15 string, keeps the superseded string in a visible field, and states the date of the read. This is the same class of volatility as F14 (brand-hosted review counts that moved in 24 hours) and the reason every volatile value on this site must carry its read date.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a></td></tr><tr id="f39"><td class="num"><strong>F39</strong></td><td>A source record was cited for a value it does not support: one registered URL stood in for three different product pages.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three products — the listing-title and ASIN rows</div></td><td class="small">Found and fixed 2026-11-15 by arena/3ddfa50e. Source S16 was registered with a single URL (https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3) but its title claimed to cover &#x27;ASINs B0CDY277D3, B09B7SB46R, B08DKYBTPH&#x27;, and it was cited as the source for all three products&#x27; listing titles and ASIN rows. For the Emeril and 8 Qt rows, the source link therefore took a reader to the Chefman 6 Qt listing. A citation that does not resolve to the page stating the value is the same defect class as F37. Fixed by registering each ASIN separately: S16 = B0CDY277D3, S28 = B09B7SB46R, S29 = B08DKYBTPH, and repointing every citation. The root build&#x27;s row-source gate did not catch this because it checks that a row links to *a* registered official host, not that the host page actually states the value.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a></td></tr><tr id="f40"><td class="num"><strong>F40</strong></td><td>Two different rows were published under the same flag number.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Register integrity — F36</div></td><td class="small">Found and fixed 2026-11-15 by arena/3ddfa50e. The programme-level row recording the srctok-marker finding (added by sess-ce4070ad, itself renumbered from F32) was published as F36, and a product-level row in data/products.json recording the Chefman 6 Qt preset-count mis-citation was also published as F36. Both rendered on the Flagged page, so the register showed 38 rows with 37 distinct numbers and any citation of &#x27;F36&#x27; was ambiguous. F28 records an earlier ID collision of the same kind, which means the rule was not enforceable. The programme-level row is renumbered F41; the product row keeps F36 because three session evidence files cite it by that number. A duplicate-ID build test now fails the build if this recurs.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f42"><td class="num"><strong>F42</strong></td><td>The operator&#x27;s location is stated two different ways on the same domain, and neither statement comes from an official source.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: questions.html (Q-06) and suggestions.html (SG-10) vs the s2/ annex register (its F08)</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by arena-b1e7b4bc. The 2026-11-15 pages say the owner is in Osaka, Japan; the s2 annex register (data/flags.json, row F08) records a Norway residence. Both were written from agent-session environment context, not from anything the owner supplied in the repository and not from any official page, so neither can be verified from here and at most one can be right. The Japan wording matters because Q-06 and SG-10 build a store-targeting decision on it. Decision needed: the owner states their actual country once, one register row cites it, and the other wording is corrected to match; until then no eligibility conclusion (Q-06, SG-10, or the s2 register&#x27;s F08) should be treated as settled.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/suggestions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">suggestions.html ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/questions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">questions.html ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/flags.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/flags.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/s2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">s2/ ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f44"><td class="num"><strong>F44</strong></td><td>The same site is published nine times from one repository, and eight of those copies are reachable at public URLs.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Whole repository — GitHub Pages deployment</div></td><td class="small">Measured 2026-11-15 by this pass: 106 HTML files are published from the repository root. The maintained site is the 27 sitemap URLs generated by build.py; the remaining pages are seven earlier or parallel builds (dashboard/, annex-88769516/, session-3740f69d/, research/, s2/, site/, docs/), each with its own homepage, its own near-duplicate comparison of the same three products, and in two cases a visible Amazon price. Amazon&#x27;s Program Policies §2(b) permit price display only when Amazon serves the link or the data comes from Creators/PA API, so a published copy that shows a price is a compliance exposure this project has otherwise been careful to avoid. Google&#x27;s own guidance (S30) states that the presumption is that individual pages match the overall quality of the domain, that a site reputation review weighs whether &#x27;the same identical or near-identical content appears on multiple other sites&#x27;, and that scaled content generated without added value for users should be excluded from search. Decision needed, and it is the owner&#x27;s: keep the older builds published for auditability and accept the duplicate-content and compliance risk, or remove the duplicate pages and keep only their evidence files. This pass did not delete or edit another session&#x27;s published output — that is not a unilateral decision to take. Full option set: SG-17 on the Suggestions page.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies" title="Google Search Central — Spam policies for Google web search" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S30</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/dashboard" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dashboard/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/annex-88769516" target="_blank" rel="noopener">annex-88769516/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/session-3740f69d" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-3740f69d/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/research" target="_blank" rel="noopener">research/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/s2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">s2/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/site" target="_blank" rel="noopener">site/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">docs/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/sitemap.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sitemap.xml ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f45"><td class="num"><strong>F45</strong></td><td>The site&#x27;s search-engine plumbing was incomplete: no page declared a canonical URL, and no page carried Open Graph or structured data.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Whole root site — every generated page</div></td><td class="small">Found and fixed 2026-11-15 by this pass, and measured before the fix: 0 of 27 generated pages had rel=canonical, 0 had og:* tags, 0 had a Twitter card, 0 had JSON-LD (a repository-wide count that also included the annex builds was 33 canonicals of 106 pages, from other sessions&#x27; templates). Two consequences: with nine copies of the same content on one origin, nothing told a search engine which copy is maintained; and a shared link rendered as a bare URL. Fixed in build.py&#x27;s layout() so every generated page now carries canonical, og:*, twitter:card and schema.org WebSite/Organization/BreadcrumbList/Article markup, and 404.html carries noindex and no canonical. Enforced by a new build guard (check_seo_metadata) so a later template edit cannot drop it. schema.org Product/Offer/AggregateRating is deliberately NOT emitted: the site publishes no Amazon price or star rating (§2(b), §6(t)), so product markup would either be empty or would assert a number this project refuses to publish.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies" title="Google Search Central — Spam policies for Google web search" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S30</a> <a class="src" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/qualify-outbound-links" title="Google Search Central — Qualify your outbound links to Google" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S31</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/sitemap.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sitemap.xml ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f43"><td class="num"><strong>F43</strong></td><td>Two wording advisories on the Suggestions and Questions pages (reader-facing precision, no data value affected).<div class="tiny muted">Scope: suggestions.html and questions.html (PR #58, X-633b115f)</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by arena-b1e7b4bc. (a) The Suggestions lede says &#x27;None of them has been implemented&#x27;, but for SG-03, SG-04, SG-05 and SG-11 option A is the current, already-implemented state (&#x27;Keep...&#x27;, &#x27;Keep blank and link to the live Amazon page (current, compliant)&#x27;), so those rows read more accurately as &#x27;confirm the current state&#x27; than as pending work. (b) Q-01&#x27;s &#x27;How to find out&#x27; says to check the owner&#x27;s Associates account for which tag the short links carry - the tag is not observable from the account&#x27;s tracking-ID page either (flag F6); only whoever created the short links knows the mapping. Decision needed: reword the lede and the Q-01 cell, or accept the current phrasing. Both rows&#x27; facts and sources check out; this flag is about wording only.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/suggestions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">suggestions.html ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/questions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">questions.html ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f47"><td class="num"><strong>F47</strong></td><td>There are two flag registers in this repository, they contradict each other, and both are readable from the live site.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: data/flags.json (F01-F27) vs flagged.html (F1-F43)</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-15 by arena/792c279e-zone1. data/flags.json still records F02 &#x27;The official Standard Commission Income schedule redirected to a sign-in page&#x27; and F01-F04 language asserting that the short links resolve &#x27;with different ASINs&#x27;. The re-verification performed in this same pass read the Standard Commission Income Statement in full without any login (Table 1: Kitchen 4.50%, All Other 4.00%) and re-confirmed all three destinations, so the older register&#x27;s two statements are contradicted by a live read. The rendered Flagged page says the opposite of data/flags.json on the commission question, so a reader who follows two links reaches two different answers. This pass did not edit data/flags.json: it is consumed by scripts/build_s2_site.py and scripts/build.mjs, and re-keying it would silently re-point another build&#x27;s citations. Decision needed: name one register canonical, then either re-key the other or replace its contradicting sentences with a dated pointer to this page. Evidence: the two files themselves, and reports/verification-2026-11-15.md section 1.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/flags.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/flags.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/sources.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/sources.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f48"><td class="num"><strong>F48</strong></td><td>The Amazon listing for the Emeril 26 QT says six cooking accessories; the site publishes seven.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Emeril 26 QT French Door — the Included accessories row</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-15 by arena/792c279e-zone1 while re-reading the live listing title. ASIN B09B7SB46R&#x27;s title now ends &#x27;... Extra Large Interior, Six Cooking Accessories Included&#x27;, while the site&#x27;s own row publishes 7, sourced to the brand product page and the owner&#x27;s manual. This is not necessarily a contradiction: a listing can count a rotisserie spit and its forks as one accessory, or bundle differently from the retail box, and the brand page and manual are the sources this site chose. The value was therefore NOT changed — altering it would mean replacing a manual-sourced number with a listing-title number on a row whose product identity is itself unconfirmed (F1, F2). Decision needed: count the accessories in the box against the manual&#x27;s accessory list, then publish one number with that source. Evidence: reports/verification-2026-11-15.md section 3 row V21.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0687/4388/8948/files/FAFO-001_VN_32451_EF_IB_T22-5004282-C_1.pdf" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Owner&#x27;s Manual, model FAFO-001 (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S15</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f49"><td class="num"><strong>F49</strong></td><td>The 2026-11-15 live pass could not retrieve the one fact that closes four high-severity flags, and the site now says why in the place a buyer will look.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three products — the model-number mapping</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by arena/792c279e-zone1. The three Amazon detail pages were fetched again and returned the buy box, the protection plans and the &#x27;Customers who viewed this item also viewed&#x27; blocks, but not the &#x27;Product information&#x27; table that carries the model number. That block is rendered client-side, so an automated read cannot see it; a human browser can, in about ten seconds per listing. This is recorded as its own row because seven sessions have now listed F1/F2/F32/F33 as &#x27;needs a human&#x27; without saying why the machine cannot do it, which invites an eighth session to try again. It is now also stated on picks.html, handover.html and hand-over item H1 rather than only in the register. Evidence: reports/verification-2026-11-15.md section 3 row V24. **TESTED AND FALSIFIED 2026-11-16 by session arena/ba29c641-zone1.** The same /dp/ URLs were fetched from the same kind of environment and the Product information block was present in the response body for all three ASINs, model rows included, so the block is not unreachable to an automated read; whether it renders depends on the render path, not on the existence of the data. The row is kept rather than deleted because the failure it documents is real and worth reading: a negative observation about a fetch was promoted to a rule (&#x27;only a human can do this&#x27;), that rule was copied forward by six later passes, and it then gated four flags, two questions and the top item of the decision queue. A &#x27;needs a human&#x27; claim is a claim about what a tool can reach, and it should be re-tested on the same schedule as any other checkable value. See F68.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/reports/verification-2026-11-15.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">reports/verification-2026-11-15.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f54"><td class="num"><strong>F54</strong></td><td>The Amazon department category is now read directly on all three listings; the rate itself still needs the Earnings report.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three products — commission rate</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by session 42ca97ad. The annex register’s row F-06 recorded that the browse-node path ‘Home &amp; Kitchen &gt; Kitchen &amp; Dining &gt; Small Appliances &gt; Fryers &gt; Air Fryers’ had been read directly on B09B7SB46R only, and named reading the other two listings as the confirmation step. This session fetched all three supplied link.amazon short links and read the identical path on B0CDY277D3 and B08DKYBTPH as well, so the evidence is now three for three rather than one for three. It still does not establish the category Amazon applies when a commission is settled: only the Associates Earnings report names the category on a shipped line, so the two candidate rows (Kitchen 4.50%, All Other 4.00%) remain the honest presentation until a sale exists. Decision needed: publish Kitchen 4.50% as the working rate with the breadcrumb cited (SG-10 path A), or keep both rows until the first Earnings report lands.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a></td></tr><tr id="f55"><td class="num"><strong>F55</strong></td><td>Eleven article sentences spliced a published display label into a mid-sentence position. Found, fixed and guarded.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: articles/ prose, and the build’s article gate</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by session 42ca97ad. The article prose in data/articles.json interpolates published values into sentences. Eleven of those interpolations put a display label — ‘Two 3-quart baskets’, ‘Designed for 4-6 people’, ‘Fries, Chicken, Fish, Meat (one-touch)’, ‘Limited 1-year warranty …’ — into a position where its capital is wrong, so the sentences read as machine output: ‘The dual-basket unit is 6 Qt / 5.67 L as Two 3-quart baskets’, ‘splits a 6 Qt / 5.67 L cavity into Two 3-quart baskets’. The defect was invisible to every earlier verification pass, because each value is genuinely published and correct in a table; it only appears when the page is read as prose, which is what a shopper does. It survived PR #71’s full rewrite of data/articles.json, which re-introduced the same ten sentences and added an eleventh in a new article. Fixed by adding a {{value::prose form}} syntax whose prose form may differ from the published value in letter case and nothing else, enforced by check_articles(), and by adding check_article_splices(), which fails the build when a capitalised value is spliced into a mid-sentence position. Both guards were proven to fire: the build failed on exactly these defects before the fix and passes after it. No published value changed and no source changed.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f56"><td class="num"><strong>F56</strong></td><td>The Application Review Process page was not re-read this pass; its figures are carried forward from an earlier fetch.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: S4 — the Associates application criteria</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by session 42ca97ad. This pass re-fetched the Program Policies, the Standard Commission Income Statement, both Chefman product pages, the Emeril product page, all three owner’s manuals, all three supplied short links, 16 CFR Part 255 and 16 CFR Part 465. It did not load the Application Review Process page (S4). The figures the repository carries for it — three qualifying sales within 180 days, roughly ten posts, content recent within about sixty days, a social follower floor of about five hundred — are carried forward from earlier logged fetches and are NOT re-confirmed by this pass. The sixty-day recency requirement is load-bearing for any content-refresh schedule the owner adopts, so it must be re-read before it is used as a scheduling rule rather than quoted from this repository. Note that a sibling pass on the same day did re-read this page and reported the same four figures, which corroborates them but does not change what this session itself fetched. What was and was not fetched is listed item by item in session-42ca97ad/EVIDENCE-2026-11-15.md section 6. **Re-read 2026-11-16 by arena/9fccce0c-zone1: all four figures are confirmed verbatim from the live page** — “we require at least three within the first 180 days”, “a good rule of thumb is at least 10 posts”, “Content on your website must be recent (generally within the last 60 days)” and “in most cases, at least 500” organic followers/likes — so the carried-forward part of this row is closed, and question Q-19 moves from *missing* to *known and sourced* with its answer on the row. Evidence: reports/verification-2026-11-16-9fccce0c.md row V3.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/G8TW5AE9XL2VX9VM" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Application Review Process" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S4</a></td></tr><tr id="f57"><td class="num"><strong>F57</strong></td><td>Three files under data/ are read by no build, and one of them is a stale copy of a register that is published.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: data/flagged.json, data/commission-rates.csv, data/products.csv</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by session 42ca97ad. A reference scan of every loader in the repository shows that scripts/build_site.py and scripts/verify_site.py read data/verified/, not data/. The root-level data/flagged.json is therefore an orphan: it holds a 13-row register whose live counterpart is data/verified/flagged.json (17 rows), and the two disagree across 85 lines — including the browse-node observation this pass has just updated, which would otherwise leave two registers saying different things about the same fact. data/commission-rates.csv and data/products.csv are read by nothing in the repository; the first is a CSV transcription of the Standard Commission Income Statement, the second the 2026-11-13 link-check ledger that records the bare-token 404 and the fallback redirect, and both are evidence worth keeping if they are labelled as evidence. Nothing published depends on any of the three, so this is a maintenance risk rather than a correctness bug, but a reader who opens data/flagged.json sees a register that no longer matches the site. This row is narrower than F47, which covers the same class of problem for data/flags.json; the two are kept separate because the files and the contradictions are different. Decision needed: delete the three orphans, or keep them explicitly as dated evidence with a header that says so.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f58"><td class="num"><strong>F58</strong></td><td>A register preamble repeated a sentence on the published page. Found on the deployed site, fixed, and guarded.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: questions.html — the preamble above the register</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-15 by session 42ca97ad, after PR #78 merged: the sentence ‘Questions in the ‘known and sourced’ state carry their answer on the row …’ appeared twice in the preamble above the questions register. The cause was the one-shot editor that adds the register rows (session-42ca97ad/apply_pass9_edits.py): it appended the sentence to the preamble unconditionally, so running it a second time appended it again. The build did not catch it, because the duplicate-ID guard sees IDs, the published-value guard sees numbers, and a repeated sentence is neither. Fixed by making the editor idempotent, by removing the duplicate from data/questions.json, and by adding check_register_preambles(), which fails the build if any register preamble repeats a sentence. The guard was proven to fire on an injected duplicate. The same edit also corrected the preamble&#x27;s own claim that the page ‘separates three things’ - it has always described four states, which is what the legend on the page lists.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f59"><td class="num"><strong>F59</strong></td><td>The repository serves six separate executive summaries, five of them stale and none labelled stale.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY.md, EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS8.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS9.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS9-42CA97AD.md, executive-summary.html</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/ad8105b3. A read of the repository root finds six files whose names all claim to be the executive summary: the generated executive-summary.html (current, built from data), EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY.md, and four earlier or parallel-session Markdown files (EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS8.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS9.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS9-42CA97AD.md). GitHub Pages publishes the repository root, so all six are reachable by URL. At least one, EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md, is already recorded in this register (F28) as asserting &#x27;Identity verified. All three short links resolve to Amazon.com product pages with different ASINs&#x27;, which this site&#x27;s reconciled position contradicts. This pass did not delete or rewrite any of them: they are other sessions&#x27; committed records, and deleting another session&#x27;s evidence is not a repair. The live site links only executive-summary.html. Decision needed: keep one Markdown briefing and move the rest under an archive directory with a one-line header saying what superseded them, or add a banner to each. Evidence: the six files themselves, and the root README. **Disposition recorded 2026-11-16 (session arena/ba29c641): the banner option was taken,** because deleting another session&#x27;s committed evidence is not a repair. Each non-canonical briefing now opens with a &#x27;Superseded, and kept&#x27; line naming its session and date, pointing at executive-summary.html, and stating the specific claim inside it that the repository has since reversed. Covered: EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md (whose 2026-11-14 banner also carried a stale count, refreshed here), EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS8.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS9.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS9-42CA97AD.md and EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS10-121929a5.md — six Markdown briefings beside the generated page. Nothing was deleted and no historical sentence was rewritten.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f60"><td class="num"><strong>F60</strong></td><td>The s2 annex&#x27;s requirements register cites two paths that do not exist, and its commission row rests on a premise the annex itself has since corrected.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: data/s2/requirements.json rows R-01 and R-08</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/ad8105b3 while building the root requirements register (requirements.html). Two defects in the annex register. (1) R-01 &#x27;Collect and organise a full list of requirements&#x27; gives its implementation path as &#x27;data/requirements.json rendered on Requirements page&#x27;. There is no data/requirements.json: the file is data/s2/requirements.json, and it renders at s2/pages/requirements.html. So the repository&#x27;s only requirements register pointed the reader at a different, non-existent file. This is the third occurrence of the same defect class (after F14 and F38 in this register), which is why the new root register has check_requirement_paths() failing the build on any cited path that does not exist, and that guard was proven to fire on an injected bad path. (2) R-08 &#x27;Analyse commission potential&#x27; is marked blocked with the reason &#x27;login-gated rate card (F-04)&#x27;. data/s2/flags.json row F-04 is itself titled &#x27;CORRECTED — a public commission table does exist&#x27; and records that the Standard Commission Income Statement is readable without a login, which this pass re-confirmed live on 2026-11-16: Table 1 renders &#x27;Physical Books, Kitchen, Automotive 4.50%&#x27;. The genuine remaining gap is narrower — Table 1 is keyed to categories, not ASINs — and the root register states it that way. Neither row was edited in the annex: data/s2/requirements.json is another session&#x27;s published output. Decision needed: re-point R-01 at the real path, and restate R-08&#x27;s blocking reason as the ASIN-to-category mapping rather than a login. Evidence: the two annex files and the commission page. **Repaired at source 2026-11-16 (session arena/ba29c641), with the correction shown as a dated line inside the annex row rather than applied silently:** R-01 names both registers and the paths that exist, R-08 states the ASIN-to-category mapping as the blocker and cites the public Standard Commission Income Statement, and R-03&#x27;s &#x27;no public demand metric exists&#x27; was corrected to what is actually true (a listing does carry a rank line; this repository declines to publish it under IP License §2(e), F8). The annex was regenerated with its own generator, the only supported way to change s2/**.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a></td></tr><tr id="f51"><td class="num"><strong>F51</strong></td><td>The brand&#x27;s own store reports a different product weight than the brand&#x27;s own specification block, and this site publishes the specification-block figure.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Chefman 6 Qt Dual-Window (RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W) and Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry (RJ38-SQSS-8T)</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by arena/63a2c638, round 9. The Chefman product pages print a Specifications block: the 6 Qt page says &#x27;Weight: 13.3 lbs / 6.03 kg&#x27; and the 8 Qt page says &#x27;Weight: (8 Qt Model) 12.8 lbs / 5.8 kg&#x27;. Those are the figures this site publishes, cited to S10 and S12. The same brand&#x27;s own commerce endpoint, read the same day, reports the variant weight as 14.51 lb / 6,582 g for RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W (S32) and 13.4 lb / 6,078 g for RJ38-SQSS-8T (S33). The endpoint figure is the weight Shopify carries for shipping, so the most likely reading is product weight versus packaged weight - but neither page says so, and the site must not choose between them by inference. No published value was changed: the specification block remains the cited source because that is the document the published row points at. Decision needed: state in each weight row whether the figure is the product or the packaged weight, or publish both.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer.json" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman product endpoint for RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W (6 Qt Dual-Window) — machine-readable record served by chefman.com" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S32</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1.json" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman product endpoint for RJ38-SQSS-8T / RJ38-SQSS-5T (8 Qt and 5 Qt TurboFry Touch)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S33</a></td></tr><tr id="f52"><td class="num"><strong>F52</strong></td><td>The open-questions register states that dimensions, weight and warranty are missing for all three products; the product dataset already publishes eight of those nine values from official sources.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: questions.html row Q-06 vs data/products.json</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by arena/63a2c638, round 9. Q-06 asks &#x27;What are the exterior dimensions, weight and warranty terms for each of the three products?&#x27; and is filed as &#x27;not_stated&#x27;. Checking the nine cells against the dataset this pass: exterior dimensions are published for all three (6 Qt from S10, Emeril from S14, 8 Qt from S12); warranty is published for all three (S10, S14, S13); weight is published for both Chefman models (S10, S12). One cell - the Emeril 26 QT product weight - is genuinely absent, because neither the brand page nor the owner&#x27;s manual states one. So the register overstates the gap by eight cells. This matters in both directions: a reader using Q-06 to decide what to research would redo work already done, and a reader trusting the product pages would think the register is stale. Q-06 was narrowed this pass to the single cell that is actually open, and is cross-linked to this row. No data value moved.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0576/5755/7158/files/RJ38-SQSS-8T-UG-US.pdf" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry Touch Air Fryer RJ38-SQSS-8T User Guide (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S13</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a></td></tr><tr id="f61"><td class="num"><strong>F61</strong></td><td>The canonical compliance register and platform blueprint had never carried the two clauses that govern an AI-agent affiliate platform, and three published pages pointed at a flag number that does not exist.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Whole site — compliance.html, platform.html, questions.html, suggestions.html, data.html</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/9fccce0c-zone1 during verification pass 10, and fixed in the same pass. Two independent defects. (a) Missing clauses. The Associates Program Policies page (S2, “Updated: April 14, 2026”) carries IP License §4 “Agents” (the Agent Terms) and Participation Requirements §5, both read verbatim live on 2026-11-16. Neither appeared anywhere in the canonical build: data/compliance.json held 0 occurrences of “Agent Terms” and data/platform.json held 0 occurrences of “Agent”, although the brief this project answers is explicitly an AI-powered affiliate platform and the root site ships a platform blueprint that proposes automated research. They are now requirements C17 and C18, with platform guardrail G11; the scope question they raise is published as Q-21 and the design decision as SG-22, and neither is answered by inference. (b) Dangling pointer. data/questions.json, data/suggestions.json and data/sources.json each told the reader to see the flag numbered 46 for the Application Review Process recency criterion. No such flag exists in the canonical register, which runs F1–F45 and F47–F58, and none exists in the legacy data/flags.json register either, which runs F01–F27. The three pointers now name F56, the row that actually covers it. A new build guard, check_register_cross_references(), fails the build if any generated page cites a flag id, source id, question id or suggestion id that is not in the register it points at, so the class of defect cannot recur silently. Evidence: reports/verification-2026-11-16-9fccce0c.md section E.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/G8TW5AE9XL2VX9VM" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Application Review Process" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S4</a></td></tr><tr id="f62"><td class="num"><strong>F62</strong></td><td>IP License §4 (the Agent Terms) was not read end to end, so the full list of agent-identification requirements is not yet on the record.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: S2 — the IP License section of the Program Policies page</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/9fccce0c-zone1. The Associates Program Policies page renders its IP License as one long table, and the markdown conversion used for that pass collapsed part of it. Everything quoted in requirement C17 was read directly in the response body, but the section was not read to its end: sub-part (a) refers to “the requirements in section 3 of these Agent Terms”, and the text of that section was not visible in the render. The Agent Terms are therefore published as the rules this pass could read, with the limit stated on the row, rather than as a complete checklist. Decision needed: read IP License §4 in a browser, in full, and extend requirement C17 if it adds anything — before the research agent is built (SG-22). Evidence: reports/verification-2026-11-16-9fccce0c.md, the limit stated under section A.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f53"><td class="num"><strong>F53</strong></td><td>The Amazon browse-node breadcrumb is now published for two ASINs, and it does not settle which commission rate applies.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three products - the &#x27;Amazon browse node&#x27; row and open question Q-11</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by arena/63a2c638, round 9, at the same time as the value itself was added, so the limit travels with the value. Both Chefman listings render the breadcrumb &#x27;Home &amp; Kitchen &gt; Kitchen &amp; Dining &gt; Small Appliances &gt; Fryers &gt; Air Fryers&#x27; (S16, S29). It is tempting to read &#x27;Kitchen&#x27; there and apply the 4.50% Kitchen rate from the Commission Income Statement (S3). That would be an inference, not a source: S3&#x27;s categories are commission categories defined by the Associates programme, Amazon&#x27;s browse nodes are a shopper-navigation taxonomy, and Amazon publishes no mapping between the two. The same breadcrumb begins &#x27;Home &amp; Kitchen&#x27;, and S3 also carries &#x27;Home&#x27; at 3.00%, so the breadcrumb alone cannot even tell you which of the two it resembles. The site therefore continues to show the commission rate as unconfirmed (Q-11) and now shows the browse node as a separate, sourced fact. The Emeril browse node is blank: three fetches of that ASIN on 2026-11-15 returned Amazon&#x27;s automated &#x27;Continue shopping&#x27; checkpoint instead of the listing page, and a value that was never rendered cannot be recorded. **Update 2026-11-16, from three reads on one day:** pass 10 (arena/121929a5) filled the Emeril row by reaching the listing through the supplied short link; arena/a746a9f2 re-read the breadcrumb directly and recorded that the checkpoint had cleared; session arena/ba29c641 fetched the /dp/ URL independently and found the same classification in the page&#x27;s Best Sellers Rank line (Kitchen &amp; Dining; Air Fryers node 17659096011). Three of three ASINs now carry a browse classification from three routes, and none of the three carries a commission category: the inference this row forbids is still forbidden, as arena/a746a9f2 put it.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a></td></tr><tr id="f46"><td class="num"><strong>F46</strong></td><td>Product photography rights are undecided: no row records which images, if any, the site publishes, whose permission covers them, or how Amazon Program Content image rights apply to these specific products. Amazon&#x27;s Operating Agreement and Program Policies govern Program Content imagery; the FTC treats imagery as part of an endorsement requiring clear disclosure of material connections. No product photography is published and Q-22 asks what may be published and whose permission covers it.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: SG-23, Q-22 and all three product pages</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/695f03b7-zone1. The design layer offers owner photos, licensed imagery and Amazon Program Content as image options, but none is decided; until Q-22 is answered, the site stays text-and-diagrams-only and the figures on figures.html are generated diagrams, not photographs. Evidence: data/suggestions.json (SG-23), data/questions.json (Q-22), build.py flags register.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f63"><td class="num"><strong>F63</strong></td><td>Fourth occurrence of the stale-count defect class: three registers asserted &#x27;eight articles&#x27; after the set grew to thirteen. Found and fixed.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: data/questions.json Q-10, data/suggestions.json SG-09, data/decision.json niche rows</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/a746a9f2 (X-a746a9f2). The brief&#x27;s line-by-line review grepped every generated page for register counts and found live assertions that had gone stale as the article set grew: questions.html (Q-10&#x27;s &#x27;what we know&#x27; cell and the page&#x27;s JSON-LD) and suggestions.html (SG-09&#x27;s title, problem, evidence label and recommendation) both said &#x27;eight articles&#x27;, and decisions.html carried &#x27;the site&#x27;s existing eight articles&#x27; plus a pill reading &#x27;Eight articles already exist in this shape&#x27; - while the articles index publishes thirteen. This is the same defect class as F14, F38 and F52: a register cell written as a snapshot and read as a live fact. Fixed per the F52 precedent: the two rows inside this session&#x27;s claimed scope were corrected in place, and the third (data/decision.json, created by a completed session) was corrected as an integration-scope wording fix, with before/after strings logged in COORDINATION.md. All three now carry a dated count (13, 2026-11-16) plus a pointer to the live articles index, so they read as dated snapshots rather than live claims. The same scan cleared the &#x27;parallel register&#x27; pages (decision-gate.html&#x27;s fourteen questions, marketing-directions.html&#x27;s twelve suggestions) as internally consistent — their ledes render their own frozen register&#x27;s length — and cleared the historical recounts in reverification.html and executive-summary.html, which describe already-fixed defects. No build guard can police free-prose counts without a whitelist of claim sentences; check_register_preambles covers repeated sentences only. Decision needed: adopt computed counts in register prose (SG-26 option A machinery) or accept dated snapshots as the house standard.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/questions.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/questions.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/suggestions.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/suggestions.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/decision.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/decision.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f64"><td class="num"><strong>F64</strong></td><td>Three registers carried duplicated content onto the published site. Found by line-by-line review, repaired, and guarded.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: questions.html row Q-11, the articles note, and one Chefman 8 Qt specification row</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/b23f12f0, round 11. A line-by-line read of data/ found three places where the same text was stored more than once and then published more than once: (1) the Q-11 row of the open-questions register carried three of its &#x27;what we know&#x27; entries seven times each - 18 entries collapsing to 6 - so the sentence &#x27;The same breadcrumb does NOT settle this question&#x27; appeared 14 times in the rendered questions.html; (2) the note field in data/articles.json repeated its first paragraph verbatim, joined by a pipe, so the editorial discipline statement was printed twice; (3) the &#x27;Hi-Fry / 450 F mode&#x27; row of the Chefman 8 Qt product cited source S29 three times in a row, which a reader sees as three source badges for one claim. None of the three is a wrong fact - each is the same fact printed repeatedly, which is precisely the pattern that makes a page read as machine-generated. All three were repaired with an order-preserving de-duplication that changed no wording. A build guard, check_register_duplicates(), now fails the build if any register list repeats an entry or any register string repeats a sentence, and the guard was proven to fire on an injected duplicate before it was committed.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/questions.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/questions.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/articles.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/articles.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f65"><td class="num"><strong>F65</strong></td><td>A recorded conclusion about Amazon’s bot checkpoint was wrong: the block is intermittent per request, not a property of the ASIN.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: data/sources.json source S28, data/products.json, and the &#x27;Amazon browse node&#x27; row of the Emeril 26 QT product</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/b23f12f0, round 11, because an earlier pass recorded a conclusion that this pass contradicts. On 2026-11-15 three fetches of ASIN B09B7SB46R were each answered by Amazon’s automated &#x27;Continue shopping&#x27; checkpoint, while the two Chefman ASINs fetched cleanly in the same window. The recorded conclusion was &#x27;this is a per-ASIN block, not a site-wide one&#x27;. Round 11 re-tested it: the Emeril ASIN loaded on the first attempt, and in the same session B0CDY277D3 - which had loaded cleanly the previous day - was served the checkpoint on its first attempt and returned the listing only on a retry. So the block is intermittent per request. The 2026-11-15 evidence could not have distinguished the two readings, because a per-ASIN pattern and an intermittent pattern look identical across three failures, but the distinction matters: a failed fetch is not evidence about the ASIN, and no future pass should record &#x27;blocked&#x27; as a property of a listing. Separately, this pass filled the Emeril browse-node cell and then found that session 121929a5 (pass 10) had filled the same cell independently on the same day, by the same route - the detail page and the supplied short link - reaching the identical string. Two independent reads agreeing is worth more than one, so both are recorded on the row and both sources cited (S28, S17). This pass also removed a stale &#x27;unverified&#x27; field that the earlier fill had left behind, which still read &#x27;Not read ... left blank&#x27; on a row that now carried a value; it did not render, but it would have contradicted the value if the render rule ever changed.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/sources.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/sources.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f66"><td class="num"><strong>F66</strong></td><td>The Emeril brand page now documents 24 presets and 25 presets on the same page, and states a different accessory count from its own Amazon listing title.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Emeril 26 QT French Door - the preset count and the included-accessories count</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/b23f12f0, round 11, from a source this project had not previously read: the brand storefront’s own machine-readable product record (S35), whose description text carries an updated_at stamp of 2026-11-16. It states &#x27;Equipped with 24 one-touch cooking presets&#x27; and &#x27;Included are 7 premium accessories&#x27;. Two consequences. On presets: the brand’s specification block still says &#x27;25 Cooking Pre-sets&#x27;, so the brand now contradicts itself within a single page rather than across two documents. The owner’s manual’s chart names 24 and the Amazon listing title says 24, so 24 now has three statements behind it and the site continues to publish 24 - but the conflict is recorded as live and unresolved, because the brand has not withdrawn the 25. On accessories: the site publishes 7 on the strength of the brand page, while the current Amazon listing title for the same ASIN reads &#x27;Six Cooking Accessories Included&#x27;. Both figures are quoted, neither was reconciled by inference, and the discrepancy is published rather than averaged. Neither published value changed in this pass.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360.json" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril product endpoint for the French Door AirFryer 360 (SKU 4-00675-02A-VN)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S35</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0687/4388/8948/files/FAFO-001_VN_32451_EF_IB_T22-5004282-C_1.pdf" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Owner&#x27;s Manual, model FAFO-001 (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S15</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/sources.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/sources.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f67"><td class="num"><strong>F67</strong></td><td>The flag duplicate-ID guard had gone blind: it matched zero rows and reported success, so it could not catch a same-day collision between two sessions.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: build.py - check_flag_ids_unique(), and the Flagged page it reads</div></td><td class="small">Found independently by two sessions on 2026-11-16 and repaired by the first of them. This pass (arena/b23f12f0, round 11) found the defect while renumbering its own flags and checking whether the guard would object; session arena/2e346d24 located and repaired it on main first, and additionally added check_flag_count_matches_page() for the related defect where the landing page was advertising zero open flags beside a 61-row register. The defect: check_flag_ids_unique() counted flag rows with a pattern that began with a bare opening table-row tag followed immediately by the number cell, while page_flagged() has emitted a row id attribute since the per-row anchors landed. The pattern therefore matched nothing, and a Counter over an empty list reports no duplicates - so the guard returned success on every run while two sessions published different rows under F61 and F62 on the same day. This repository has been bitten by flag-ID collisions at least three times before (F28, F40 and the preamble case F58), which is why the guard exists. It is recorded here rather than in the repairing session&#x27;s own words because the finding belongs on the register even though the repair predates this pass landing. Nothing in this row is a claim by this pass to have made the fix.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/flagged.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">flagged.html ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f68"><td class="num"><strong>F68</strong></td><td>The &#x27;only a human in a browser can read this&#x27; rule was untested for ten passes, and testing it took one fetch. Also: on one ASIN the model code is not in the field named Model Number.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: F1, F2, F32, F33, F49, SG-01, Q-01, Q-02, hand-over item H1, and every product page</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/ba29c641-zone1, as the consequence of F49. The register&#x27;s own explanation for why four flags needed a human was that the Product information table is rendered client-side and cannot be retrieved by a fetch. Three /dp/ URLs were fetched on 2026-11-16 and the block was present in each response, model rows included. Two lessons, both kept on the record. (1) A negative claim about tooling is an empirical claim and decays: &#x27;the fetch did not return it&#x27; became &#x27;a fetch cannot return it&#x27; without anyone re-testing, and six later passes copied the sentence forward, one of them into a hand-over sheet and a &#x27;three minutes of your time&#x27; callout on the landing page. (2) The method matters as much as the read: on B08DKYBTPH the field called Model Number holds the marketing string &#x27;Large Air Fryer&#x27; and the code sits in Part Number, so any procedure that reads &#x27;the Model Number row&#x27; — the exact procedure SG-01 told the owner to follow — returns a non-code for one of the three products. Both fields must be read, and the two are compared. What this pass did NOT do is convert a listing claim into a manufacturer specification: the 31 rows added to data/products.json are each labelled &#x27;as listed by Amazon&#x27; and sourced to S16/S28/S29, and product identity from the link side stays open (F6, F7, F10). The mechanism behind the earlier failed read is recorded separately and correctly by arena/b23f12f0 as F65 — the “Continue shopping” checkpoint is intermittent per request, not a property of the ASIN — so the 2026-11-15 observation that the table was missing was a real observation about three particular responses, and the error was generalising from them to a rule about all fetches. Both rows stand on their own reads.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0576/5755/7158/files/RJ38-SQSS-8T-UG-US.pdf" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry Touch Air Fryer RJ38-SQSS-8T User Guide (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S13</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0576/5755/7158/files/RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W-UG-US.pdf" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry Touch Dual Window Air Fryer RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W User Guide (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S18</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-8-qt-air-fryer-w-high-fry" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry 8 Qt. Air Fryer w/ High Fry (RJ38-8T-BLACK) — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S26</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/reports/asin-model-mapping-2026-11-16.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">reports/asin-model-mapping-2026-11-16.md ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f69"><td class="num"><strong>F69</strong></td><td>Two official documents disagree on the 8 Qt&#x27;s footprint, and one listing disagrees with itself about the 6 Qt&#x27;s capacity units.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: chefman-8qt-turbofry dimensions rows; chefman-6qt-dual-basket capacity rows</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/ba29c641-zone1, in the same read that closed F1. chefman.com publishes the 8 Qt (RJ38-SQSS-8T) as 15.4 in x 12.6 in x 13.5 in and separates the 5 Qt figures on the same page (S12); the listing for B08DKYBTPH publishes 12.75&quot;D x 13.5&quot;W x 10&quot;H (S29). Those are not two ways of writing one measurement: the depth differs by 2.65 in and the height by 3.5 in, so a buyer checking a cabinet clearance gets a different answer from each official source, and the weight agrees on both (12.8 lb) — which is what makes it a documentation problem rather than a transcription of a different product. Nothing was averaged: the brand figures stay the published row, the listing&#x27;s figures sit on their own dated row. The same class on the 6 Qt: its listing&#x27;s Capacity field reads &#x27;6 liters&#x27; while the same page&#x27;s title, its Size field and the brand page all read 6 quarts (5.67 L) — a unit label that moved. Any copy that turns a capacity or a footprint into advice (the capacity guide, the small-kitchen article) must carry the caveat until Chefman states which figures are the appliance and which are the carton. Decision needed: ask Chefman which number is the appliance footprint, or drop cabinet-clearance advice.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f70"><td class="num"><strong>F70</strong></td><td>Three official documents give three different model strings for the Emeril 26 QT, and the two newest differ by one character.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: emeril-26qt-french-door model_number and its listing rows</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/ba29c641-zone1. The owner&#x27;s manual is headed FAFO-001 (S15), the brand product page lists 4-00675-02A-VN (S14), and the listing for B09B7SB46R states 4-00675-02X-VN in both its Model Number and Part Number rows (S28). An &#x27;A&#x27; against an &#x27;X&#x27; in the tenth position is the whole difference, and it is the kind of difference that means either a variant suffix or a transcription error in one of three documents — no document in this repository says which. This is the one product where the live read did not settle the mapping the way it did for the two Chefman listings, so all three strings are quoted and none is asserted. The commercial reason it matters: a comparison page that prints the wrong SKU string is a page that can be accused of reviewing a different machine, on a site whose entire claim is traceability. Decision needed: Emeril Everyday support can state the SKU behind the ASIN in one reply; until then the product page shows three strings and the character in dispute.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0687/4388/8948/files/FAFO-001_VN_32451_EF_IB_T22-5004282-C_1.pdf" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Owner&#x27;s Manual, model FAFO-001 (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S15</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f71"><td class="num"><strong>F71</strong></td><td>The Emeril listing contradicts itself inside one table: 7 accessories in Configuration, six parts enumerated in Included Components, &#x27;Six&#x27; in the title. F48 records the six-vs-seven question; this row is about the self-contradiction.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: emeril-26qt-french-door &#x27;Included accessories&#x27;, &#x27;Configuration&#x27; and &#x27;Included components&#x27; rows</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/ba29c641-zone1, and it complicates F48 rather than repeating it. F48 was recorded 2026-11-15 from the listing *title* alone (&#x27;Six Cooking Accessories Included&#x27; against the site&#x27;s manual-sourced 7), and left open with the sensible observation that a listing may count a spit and its forks as one item. Reading the whole Product information block shows the page does not agree with itself: its Configuration field says &#x27;…with 24 Functions and 7 Accessories&#x27;, its Included Components row then enumerates six named parts (Rack, Bake Pan, Drip Tray, Crisper Basket, Rotisserie Spit, Fetch Tool), and its title says Six. So &#x27;the listing says six&#x27; is not a single fact to weigh against the manual — the listing says both, on one screen. The site&#x27;s published value stays 7 because the brand page and the manual state it (S14, S15); the Configuration field is a fourth document that agrees, and the enumeration is a second that does not. No averaging, no quiet correction. Decision needed: the packing list settles it. Note the same fetch also strengthened the other half of that row: &#x27;24 Functions&#x27; in the Configuration field is a third document agreeing with the manual&#x27;s 24 named presets against the brand page&#x27;s &#x27;25 Cooking Pre-sets&#x27; (F4). Distinct from F66, which records the brand page&#x27;s own 24/25 split and the title-vs-manual accessory disagreement: this row is the narrower observation that the two counts sit next to each other in one table on the listing.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0687/4388/8948/files/FAFO-001_VN_32451_EF_IB_T22-5004282-C_1.pdf" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Owner&#x27;s Manual, model FAFO-001 (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S15</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f72"><td class="num"><strong>F72</strong></td><td>Temperature floors published in degrees Celsius do not convert to the floors published in degrees Fahrenheit, on two of the three products.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: chefman-6qt-dual-basket and chefman-8qt-turbofry temperature rows; any copy quoting a low end</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/ba29c641-zone1. The 6 Qt brand page gives 200-450 °F / 95-235 °C (S10); the same product&#x27;s listing states &#x27;Min Temperature Setting: 93 °C&#x27; (S16), and 93 °C is 199.4 °F, not 95 °C&#x27;s 203 °F. The 8 Qt listing states &#x27;Min 94 °C&#x27; (S29) against the brand page&#x27;s 200 °F floor (S12), i.e. 201.2 °F. Two degrees, in both cases, in the same direction: 200 °F is 93.33 °C, so someone rounded the Celsius figure up in one document and left the Fahrenheit figure alone in the other. This is a rounding inconsistency, not a second specification, and it is recorded rather than repaired because the site does not get to rewrite a manufacturer&#x27;s numbers. What it forbids is specific: an article that says &#x27;the 6 Qt goes down to 95 °C&#x27; is quoting one page and contradicting another, so low-end claims must show both units with the source that uses them, or stay out of the copy.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f73"><td class="num"><strong>F73</strong></td><td>The requirements register promised computed counts in its own preamble and then typed eight of them into prose; four had already gone stale.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: data/requirements.json (rows A-02, A-04, A-06, B-01, B-05, B-06, B-08, C-02, D-05, G-01)</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/ba29c641-zone1 while updating the register for this pass&#x27;s rows. The file&#x27;s `counts_note` says: &#x27;Counts are computed from the rows at build time rather than typed, so this page cannot claim a total the register does not contain.&#x27; The status counts at the top of the page are indeed computed — every number inside the prose of individual rows was typed, and four had drifted within two days: &#x27;Forty-six spec rows&#x27; and &#x27;43 of 46 carry a value&#x27; (the dataset had moved to 46 and then to 77 as passes added sourced rows), &#x27;Twenty-one numbered suggestions (SG-01…SG-21)&#x27; against a register of 23, &#x27;Twenty questions&#x27; against 22, and &#x27;a generated sitemap of 37 URLs&#x27; against 46. None of these is a wrong fact about the world; each is a right fact about an older build, published as present tense, which is how a verification register quietly becomes a historical document. The fix is structural, not editorial: those rows now write named count tokens — n_spec_rows, n_verified_rows, n_blank_rows, n_suggestions, n_sg_last, n_questions, n_articles, n_topics, n_products, n_sources, n_banned, n_banned_distinct, n_tests, n_guards, n_stale_briefings — each wrapped in braces in the data and substituted by req_interp() from the same objects the pages render from. An unknown token raises at build time, and check_requirement_tokens() fails the build if a brace-delimited token survives into any generated page, so a row cannot ship half-interpolated. Both directions were proven: an injected bad token and an injected unresolved token each exited non-zero. The same class is why this register&#x27;s sibling in the s2 annex carries F60, and why the sitemap row&#x27;s number was removed rather than retyped: a count is only true at the moment it is written — including the counts typed into this register&#x27;s own log lines, one of which this pass had to correct in its own commit message two minutes after writing it (7,815 links became 7,826 when the guard rows were added). Process note for the next session: this pass&#x27;s rows were renumbered twice in one day to avoid same-day id collisions — first off F63 (arena/a746a9f2), then off F64-F67 (arena/b23f12f0, whose F67 is the duplicate-ID guard that has now caught both collisions). Renumbering is cheap while a PR is open and expensive after it merges.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/requirements.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/requirements.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f74"><td class="num"><strong>F74</strong></td><td>An article page existed on disk and on the live site that no data file or generator referenced: articles/sync-finish-two-buttons.html. Found independently twice on 2026-11-16; repaired the same day.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: articles/sync-finish-two-buttons.html vs data/articles.json (no matching slug); file removed 2026-11-16</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by session arena/19d92a81 during the line-by-line repo review, after the file arrived with a parallel pass&#x27;s merge. data/articles.json carries no &#x27;sync-finish-two-buttons&#x27; slug and no build step generates the page, so nothing will ever refresh or retire it - yet it is served publicly next to the generated articles and looks exactly like them, disclosure bar included, so a reader cannot tell it is unmanaged. A grep of data/ and build.py finds zero references. It is the same defect class as F57 (orphan registers), one layer up: a page outside the data-driven set. Independent discovery the same day by arena/5ac82b1d during the final integration pass: articles/ held 20 HTML files against 19 registered slugs; the orphan carried the same TP-09 subject as the registered article sync-finish-explained.html and appeared in no sitemap entry, no articles index and no internal link - an orphan a crawler could still reach by direct URL, publishing a second unregistered answer to the same question. Its own passing session&#x27;s completion record (X-121929a5) states this draft was discarded as a duplicate of round 11&#x27;s TP-09 article; the register entry was dropped but the HTML file survived the merge, and no gate compared the articles/ directory against the page set the build emits (verify.py checks links from files that exist, so it cannot see a file nothing links to). DECISION EXECUTED 2026-11-16 by arena/5ac82b1d: deleted rather than registered - the canonical TP-09 article (articles/sync-finish-explained.html, data/topics.json published_as) already covers the subject, so registering the duplicate would publish the same question twice. check_no_orphan_pages() now fails the build if any .html file in articles/, products/, guides/, categories/ or the repo root is not generated by this build, and the guard was proven to fire on an injected file before it was committed.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/articles.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/articles.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/topics.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/topics.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/reports/verification-2026-11-16-19d92a81.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">reports/verification-2026-11-16-19d92a81.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f75"><td class="num"><strong>F75</strong></td><td>The 404 page was published with relative links, but GitHub Pages serves it at any missing path — so every link on it broke under nested URLs.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: 404.html (all href/src values)</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/5ac82b1d while reading back the deleted orphan article&#x27;s URL on the live site: /articles/sync-finish-two-buttons.html correctly served the 404 page, but its nav, card and stylesheet links resolved against /articles/ (e.g. products/chefman-6qt-dual-basket.html became /articles/products/...), so the error page it served was itself unstyled and full of dead links — the one page whose whole job is to recover a lost reader. Root-relative links (/Zone1/...) were rejected as a fix because verify.py and scripts/check_links.py resolve internal hrefs against the file on disk, where they do not exist. Fixed instead by rewriting every relative href/src on 404.html to its absolute SITE_URL form in layout(), leaving fragment-only links (#anchor) alone since they address the displayed document. check_seo_metadata() now fails the build if any non-absolute, non-fragment URL survives on the 404 page. Note for later: this defect can only be observed by fetching a missing nested URL from the deployed site — no local gate sees the serving path.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/404.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">404.html ↗</a></td></tr></tbody>
+  <tbody><tr id="f1"><td class="num"><strong>F1</strong></td><td>ASIN → manufacturer model number: recorded for ten passes as not published by Amazon. Closed on 2026-11-16 for two of three products — see F49 and F68.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three</div></td><td class="small">HISTORY KEPT, BECAUSE THE PREMISE WAS THE DEFECT: this row said the model number could not be read from the listing, so each product&#x27;s model was published as a documented match with a caveat, and F49 recorded that a machine could not read it at all. On 2026-11-16 session arena/ba29c641 fetched all three /dp/ pages and each Product information block rendered. B0CDY277D3 states Model Number and Part Number RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W — the brand page&#x27;s and manual&#x27;s string, so that mapping is now sourced twice. B08DKYBTPH states Part Number RJ38-SQSS-8T (its Model Number row holds the marketing string &#x27;Large Air Fryer&#x27;). B09B7SB46R states 4-00675-02X-VN, one character away from the brand page&#x27;s 4-00675-02A-VN, and that conflict stays open (F70). Still open, unchanged by this pass: whether the supplied short links point where the account holder intended (F6, F7, F10), which is why the caveat on the *link* half of the mapping is not removed.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a></td></tr><tr id="f6"><td class="num"><strong>F6</strong></td><td>The Associates tag inside the three link.amazon short links is not observable.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three links</div></td><td class="small">The shortener hides the tag. Attribution cannot be verified from outside Associates Central, so &#x27;associates_tag_confirmed&#x27; is false for all three links. Needs the account holder to open Manage Tracking IDs.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a></td></tr><tr id="f7"><td class="num"><strong>F7</strong></td><td>Program Policies §6(w) (Content on your Site) prohibits link shortening that makes it unclear you are linking to an Amazon Site.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three links</div></td><td class="small">link.amazon keeps the Amazon name visible, so the risk is low, but the landing URL is generated and temporary. Recorded so the account holder can confirm current guidance before publishing.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f8"><td class="num"><strong>F8</strong></td><td>IP License §2(e) forbids aggregating, analysing or repurposing Program Content and training models on it.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Platform design</div></td><td class="small">Any AI research or trend scoring therefore runs only on first-party data and public manufacturer data. Documented as guardrail G6 in the blueprint.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f9"><td class="num"><strong>F9</strong></td><td>No Amazon price, star rating or review count is available to this build.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three</div></td><td class="small">Left blank everywhere by policy rather than by omission. Becomes fillable only if the operator secures Creators/PA API credentials.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GBRCD467W33NMWLD" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — New Product Advertising API (PA API) Registration" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S19</a></td></tr><tr id="f10"><td class="num"><strong>F10</strong></td><td>The short links are generated redirects with fallback parameters, not plain product links.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three links</div></td><td class="small">Logged redirect observations carried lv=shuf and plpRedirect=mhFallback. The candidate ASIN/title destinations were consistent across the recorded checks, but the btn_ref/srctok value changed between repeat or cache-busted requests (see F41 and the linked session evidence). These are observations of redirect responses, not confirmation that the candidate ASINs are the products intended by the account holder or that the links carry an Associates tag. Confirm both before publishing.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a></td></tr><tr id="f11"><td class="num"><strong>F11</strong></td><td>The site does not disclose cookies, pixels and third-party technologies as Participation Requirements §3(e) requires.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Whole site</div></td><td class="small">The site itself sets no cookies and runs no analytics, but §3(e) requires an accurate disclosure of cookie and third-party technology use, including that third parties may place or recognise cookies. Only a minimal notice is in the footer; a proper privacy/cookie statement is needed before launch. Legal review recommended. UPDATED 2026-11-17 (arena/58cbaaa2): the row&#x27;s 2026-11-13 premise — nothing is stored — is no longer the whole truth, so the footer statement was rewritten to say exactly what happens instead of left to rot: no cookie, no script, no network call, and one tally in the reader&#x27;s own localStorage written when a product link is followed, with an erase control in the same paragraph. The §3(e) obligation is now met by a statement that describes the technology rather than denying it exists; what remains open is the half this repository cannot close — whether the endpoint decision (F79) adds a third party, and whether a lawyer reads the sentence before a domain points here.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f12"><td class="num"><strong>F12</strong></td><td>The automated platform functions in the brief are not running — by design, not by omission.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Platform</div></td><td class="small">Automated trend detection, competition scoring and conversion optimisation need an Associates account, Creators/PA API credentials and sales reports. PA API access is registered through Associates Central and credentials are tied to the primary account holder, with request rate scaled to account sales performance. None of that exists in this repository, and inventing the data to fill the gap would defeat the point of the project.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GBRCD467W33NMWLD" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — New Product Advertising API (PA API) Registration" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S19</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GMEDADBTCJ9KD8DQ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — About the Product Advertising API (topic index)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S20</a></td></tr><tr id="f13"><td class="num"><strong>F13</strong></td><td>Product listing titles are reproduced from Amazon; §6(a) restricts altering Program Content.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three</div></td><td class="small">Titles are shown verbatim and unedited. §6(a) permits truncation only where meaning is not materially altered, and whether a listing title counts as Program Content is not stated. Recorded so the operator can confirm before adding any paraphrase.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f14"><td class="num"><strong>F14</strong></td><td>The landing page overstated product resolution (corrected 2026-11-14).<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Root landing page</div></td><td class="small">The headline read &#x27;3 products resolved — all three short links followed to an ASIN&#x27;. The 2026-11-14 check found the short links reach Amazon fallback pages and the bare 9-character codes return &#x27;Page Not Found&#x27;. The headline now reads &#x27;0 verified product identities&#x27; and the ASINs are labelled as unconfirmed candidates. Evidence: reports/link-check-2026-11-14.md.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a></td></tr><tr id="f15"><td class="num"><strong>F15</strong></td><td>Plain ASIN URLs were also answered with Amazon&#x27;s fallback form on 2026-11-14.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three ASINs</div></td><td class="small">amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3, /dp/B09B7SB46R and /dp/B08DKYBTPH each returned a URL carrying lv=shuf and plpRedirect=mhFallback, so a fetch cannot show that the ASIN is the live product page. Confirm from a logged-in browser before any ASIN is treated as the supplied product.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a></td></tr><tr id="f16"><td class="num"><strong>F16</strong></td><td>link.amazon is not confirmed as an Amazon-documented short-link domain.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three links</div></td><td class="small">A web search on 2026-11-14 returned only third-party blogs naming amzn.to and a.co. No official Amazon page listing link.amazon was found. An official Associates help page is needed before the domain is described as Amazon&#x27;s.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a></td></tr><tr id="f17"><td class="num"><strong>F17</strong></td><td>Advisory link gate: table rows with no source link on inherited annex pages.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: dashboard/, site/, research/, session-3740f69d/ annexes</div></td><td class="small">Root pages generated by build.py were given a source badge, policy citation or flagged-page link in the arena/0c7445c7 pass (2026-11-14). Remaining advisory rows lived only in other sessions&#x27; annex builds; scripts/check_links.py reported them as non-blocking warnings. **CLOSED 2026-11-14 by sess-7111e2b6** along with F25: a row-source pass now runs inside the research/ and site/ generators, and scripts/source_rows.py applies it to the hand-authored annex builds. The advisory list is empty, and the gate&#x27;s own definition was corrected first, so that it can see single-quoted hrefs and every flagged-page filename.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f18"><td class="num"><strong>F18</strong></td><td>Committed s2 build stamp changes with the calendar date.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: s2/ (session zone1-s2 build)</div></td><td class="small">scripts/build_s2_site.py writes today&#x27;s date into the &#x27;built&#x27; stamp. Re-running on 2026-11-14 changes 13 files by that stamp alone (committed stamp: 2026-11-13). The CI gate regenerates before it checks, so it passes, but the committed copy is not pinned. Not changed in this pass because another session owns the file.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f19"><td class="num"><strong>F19</strong></td><td>Brand-site review counts move between days, so every rating on this site is a dated observation.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three</div></td><td class="small">Chefman&#x27;s own widget read 1,302 reviews on 2026-11-13 and 1,305 on 2026-11-14; the 8 Qt page read 715 then 716. A third read on 2026-11-16 moved them again — 1,307 and 717 — while both averages held at 4.6 for the third consecutive time. The averages did not move (4.6 on every read), but a published figure without its read date would silently become false. All three counts now carry all three dates in the dataset, and any copy generated from it must carry the date too.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a></td></tr><tr id="f20"><td class="num"><strong>F20</strong></td><td>The repository carries six parallel builds, and two of them were unreachable from the landing page until the 2026-11-14 integration pass.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Whole repo</div></td><td class="small">s2/, site/, dashboard/, research/, session-3740f69d/ and docs/ are independent session outputs with their own data, their own verifiers and their own verdicts; they disagree in places (for example on whether the commission rate can be stated, and on how to read the plpRedirect=mhFallback marker). The pass linked the two orphaned builds into the navigation and changed none of their content. The owner should decide which build is canonical before this points at a real domain — a live site with six home pages is a maintenance and trust problem, not a data problem.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/s2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">s2/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/site" target="_blank" rel="noopener">site/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/dashboard" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dashboard/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/research" target="_blank" rel="noopener">research/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/session-3740f69d" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-3740f69d/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">docs/ ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f23"><td class="num"><strong>F23</strong></td><td>One documented build command in the README no longer runs against the shared data — it is a pre-existing break, not a regression from this pass.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: scripts/build.mjs → docs/</div></td><td class="small">The Node builder that generated the retired docs/ build validates a schema (products with official_url / values, sources with a status field, compliance.rows, flags) that data/products.json, data/sources.json, data/compliance.json and data/flags.json no longer use, so &#x27;node scripts/build.mjs --check&#x27; throws ERR_INVALID_URL on undefined. Verified identical on the pre-change baseline, and no CI workflow calls it — pages.yml, ci.yml and verify.yml are Python-only. docs/ therefore stays frozen as committed and cannot be regenerated from current data. Not repaired here: re-authoring another session&#x27;s generator to a new schema would silently change what its published pages assert. Either retire docs/ and its README line, or point the builder at data/verified/. This pass added no dependency on it.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/scripts/build.mjs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">scripts/build.mjs ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f22"><td class="num"><strong>F22</strong></td><td>A committed merge-conflict marker was found in a published document and repaired by this pass.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md</div></td><td class="small">The file carried a bare &#x27;=======&#x27; line and a &#x27;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; origin/main&#x27; line on main, i.e. a previous integration resolved a conflict by leaving the markers in. Both sides are real work by different sessions, so this pass removed only the markers and kept both documents as separate sections. Any other file carrying markers should be treated the same way — delete the marker, never the work.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f21"><td class="num"><strong>F21</strong></td><td>Two verification passes on the same links reached different conclusions, and both are published.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: The three supplied links</div></td><td class="small">arena/1dcaaafc (reports/link-check-2026-11-14.md) reads the fallback marker plus the 9-character-token 404 as &#x27;product identity not confirmed&#x27; and removed the &#x27;products resolved&#x27; headline. sess-81495d2d (reverification.html) re-tested with a repeat request and a nonsense-token control: the target was identical across four sessions on two days, while an unrecognised token returns 403 instead of a product page, which is not what a pure not-found fallback would do. The reconciled position on this site: the *destination* of the links is reproducible and is documented; the *identity* of the product the operator meant, and the Associates tag attached to the link, are not confirmed and are not asserted. Confirm both in a logged-in browser and in Associates Central before publishing.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — Product-detail lookup for the supplied 9-character token (negative control)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S21</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/ZZZ9ZZZZZ" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon resolver, nonsense-token control request" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S22</a></td></tr><tr id="f24"><td class="num"><strong>F24</strong></td><td>PA-API 5 is officially deprecated (confirmed live 2026-11-14); the successor is Amazon&#x27;s Creators API.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Platform design</div></td><td class="small">The official docs URL now serves a deprecation notice: PA-API 5 calls return 403 AccessDeniedException and integrations are told to migrate to the Creators API. This supersedes the earlier board retraction of the cross-check&#x27;s F18 claim and means every automated feature in the blueprint must target the Creators API, not PA-API 5. The Program Policies phrase their data-use rules as &#x27;Creators API or PA API&#x27;, so the compliance quotes remain accurate. Independently noted in the dashboard annex as H-01.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/creatorsapi/docs/en-us/paapiv5-deprecation" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Creators API documentation — PA-API 5 Deprecation Notice" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S24</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GMEDADBTCJ9KD8DQ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — About the Product Advertising API (topic index)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S20</a></td></tr><tr id="f25"><td class="num"><strong>F25</strong></td><td>Table rows on inherited builds have no link to a registered official source.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: dashboard/, research/, site/, s2/, session-3740f69d/, annex-88769516/</div></td><td class="small">Audited 2026-11-14 with the project&#x27;s own official-host list (every host in data/sources.json, plus this repo on GitHub). Root pages generated by build.py now pass, and the build fails on any unsourced row. Inherited builds were not edited by that pass because other sessions own them. Count at 2026-11-14: 281 of 1,363 table rows in the repository have no sourced link (inherited builds: 281 of 1,057; root pages built by build.py: 0 of 306). **CLOSED 2026-11-14 by sess-7111e2b6.** The residue is now 0. Two things were wrong with the count itself: the link gate could not see single-quoted hrefs, and it recognised only one filename for the flagged page, so 33 rows that did carry a source link were being reported as unsourced. With the gate corrected, the true figure was 248 rows. A row-source pass was then added inside the research/ and site/ generators, and scripts/source_rows.py applies the same pass to the three hand-authored builds (dashboard/, annex-88769516/, session-3740f69d/). Rows that state a fact now link to that fact&#x27;s official document; rows that are judgements, blocked metrics or unverified values link to the flagged page, which is where the project puts unconfirmed values. The gate also now rejects rows that link only to a relative internal page.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/scripts/check_links.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">scripts/check_links.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/dashboard" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dashboard/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/research" target="_blank" rel="noopener">research/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/site" target="_blank" rel="noopener">site/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/s2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">s2/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/session-3740f69d" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-3740f69d/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/annex-88769516" target="_blank" rel="noopener">annex-88769516/ ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f26"><td class="num"><strong>F26</strong></td><td>Two platform KPIs are internal design targets; no official source states them.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: platform.html, KPI table: Verified-row coverage, Flag resolution time</div></td><td class="small">Shown with this flag, not with an invented source. They are operating targets for this project, not facts about Amazon, the FTC or any product, and they feed no published product value. The owner should decide whether to keep them as targets.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/platform.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/platform.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f27"><td class="num"><strong>F27</strong></td><td>Policy citations for the Participation Requirements &#x27;Content on your Site&#x27; items used section numbers that do not match the live page.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Root-site content (corrected in this pass); site/ annex pages still carry the old labels</div></td><td class="small">Live reads of affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies on 2026-11-14 (two independent renders; version dated April 14, 2026) place the customer-review/star-rating rule at Participation Requirements &#x27;6. Content on your Site&#x27; item (t), and items (g) incentives, (n) framing, (u) self-purchase, (v) cloaking, (w) link shortening and (y) price tracking in the same section. Repo content had cited these as §3(t), §3(g), §3(n), §3(u), §3(v), §2(w) and §3(y). Every quoted rule text was verified verbatim against the live page, so no published behaviour changes — only the citation labels were aligned to the live structure in build.py, data/compliance.json, data/products.json, data/sources.json, data/platform.json, data/marketing.json, README.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY.md and EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md. Spot-checked labels that were already correct: §2(b) prices/availability, §3(e) cookies, §6(a) alteration, IP License §2(e) aggregation/ML-training, Operating Agreement §5 disclosure. The site/ annex (session 1874bb64) still renders §3(t) from data/verified/; its owner session should align it (request logged in COORDINATION.md). Historical session records in COORDINATION.md were left as written.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f28"><td class="num"><strong>F28</strong></td><td>Three flag registers share overlapping ID spaces but index different issues, and two published documents cite the wrong one.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Cross-build consistency</div></td><td class="small">Confirmed 2026-11-14 by sess-91d0552e (renumbered F25 -&gt; F28 to avoid a collision with arena/6ecdb4e4&#x27;s F25). (a) research/build_research.py tells the reader to see &#x27;the root site&#x27;s Flagged page (shared register data/flags.json id F19)&#x27; for the product-titles/§6(a) issue and &#x27;id F20&#x27; for the cookie-disclosure issue. Those IDs ARE correct inside data/flags.json, but the root Flagged page is generated from build.py&#x27;s own PROGRAMME_FLAGS, where F19 is &#x27;brand-site review counts move between days&#x27; and F20 is &#x27;the repository carries six parallel builds&#x27;. A reader following that pointer lands on an unrelated row. (b) EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md, still served at the site root, asserts &#x27;Identity verified. All three short links resolve to Amazon.com product pages with different ASINs&#x27; and that &#x27;the official commission schedule redirected to a sign-in page&#x27;. Both are contradicted by this pass: the reconciled position published on this site is 0 verified product identities (F14-F16, F21), and the Standard Commission Income Statement was read in full, without any login, on 2026-11-14. Not repaired in this pass: data/flags.json is consumed by the s2 and docs generators and research/ is another session&#x27;s build, so re-keying either register would silently re-point another build&#x27;s citations. Decision needed: adopt one register as canonical and re-key the others, or relabel the pointers. Evidence: session-91d0552e/VERIFICATION-2026-11-14.md §5.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a></td></tr><tr id="f29"><td class="num"><strong>F29</strong></td><td>The Emeril preset count is stated five different ways across official sources, and two of them now say 24.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Emeril 26 QT French Door</div></td><td class="small">Re-read 2026-11-14 by sess-54c66535. Amazon&#x27;s listing title for ASIN B09B7SB46R says &#x27;24 cooking functions&#x27; and Emeril&#x27;s own description paragraph says &#x27;Equipped with 24 one-touch cooking presets&#x27;, while the same page&#x27;s specification block says &#x27;25 Cooking Pre-sets&#x27;, its feature heading says &#x27;10-In-1&#x27; and one of its carousel assets is named &#x27;11-in-1&#x27;. Two independent official sources now agreeing on 24 is new evidence: earlier passes recorded only 25 vs 10-in-1 vs 11-in-1 with no tiebreaker. The conflict is narrowed, not closed - the same brand page still contradicts itself - so no preset count is asserted anywhere on this site. Decision needed: confirm the figure against the unit&#x27;s own control panel before any copy states a number. &lt;strong&gt;Re-read 2026-11-15 by arena/88bb2db6, which changes that last sentence.&lt;/strong&gt; The owner&#x27;s manual for FAFO-001 was read row by row: its Preset Mode Chart and its Recommended Accessory Positions table each list exactly 24 presets by name, and the manual never states the numeral. The Amazon listing title independently says &#x27;24 One-Touch Preset Options&#x27;. The site now states 24 on the strength of the manual, names the one official line that disagrees (the brand page&#x27;s specification block, &#x27;25 Cooking Pre-sets&#x27;), and flags it. This row and F4 both stay open, but the site no longer asserts nothing. The 10-in-1 and 11-in-1 labels remain marketing wording and are not presented as specifications. Decision still needed: confirm the figure against the unit&#x27;s own control panel.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-54c66535/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-54c66535/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f30"><td class="num"><strong>F30</strong></td><td>Two published specification values are not stated by the official source the site cites for them.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket (preset count); Emeril 26 QT French Door (interior light)</div></td><td class="small">Re-read 2026-11-14 by sess-54c66535. The Chefman dual-window user guide RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W was read end to end and documents HI-FRY, +2 MIN, MUTE, SYNC BASKETS, SYNC FINISH, START/STOP, TIME/TEMP and SHAKE, but has no preset chart and never names the four food presets the site publishes; its control panel is artwork with no text layer. The Emeril brand page&#x27;s specification block lists Timer, 26-Quart Capacity, Dual-Speed Fan, 25 Cooking Pre-sets, dimensions, 24.6 Liters, Temperature Control and 120 volts, with no interior-light row. Both values were left as published rather than deleted, because deleting another session&#x27;s sourced row is not this pass&#x27;s call, but neither is currently traceable to an official statement. Decision needed: re-source both to the brand product pages, or blank them and let them appear here as unverified.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0576/5755/7158/files/RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W-UG-US.pdf" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry Touch Dual Window Air Fryer RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W User Guide (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S18</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-54c66535/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-54c66535/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-54c66535/pass6.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-54c66535/pass6.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f31"><td class="num"><strong>F31</strong></td><td>The two Chefman 6 Qt sister SKUs carry very different review counts on the brand&#x27;s own site.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket</div></td><td class="small">Re-read 2026-11-14 by sess-54c66535. The dual-basket page RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2 reports 4.6 stars from 194 reviews; the dual-window page RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W reports 4.6 stars from 1,305 reviews, on an identical list price and promo ladder. The 6.7x gap is a usable signal for deciding which SKU the supplied ASIN maps to, which is open flag F2. It is not an identification: a review count is not a product identifier. No SKU is asserted. Correction 2026-11-16 (session arena/ba29c641): this row also stated that &#x27;Amazon does not publish the model number behind an ASIN&#x27;, which is false — the listing&#x27;s Product information block publishes it (F49, F68) — so a review-count comparison is no longer the nearest thing to a route: the model number itself has been read, and it matches the -2W page this site maps to.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-6-qt-dual-basket-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Basket Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S11</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-54c66535/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-54c66535/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f32"><td class="num"><strong>F32</strong></td><td>Chefman sells three different 6-quart dual-basket SKUs; the site names two of them.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket</div></td><td class="small">Found live 2026-11-14 by arena/6b2eddeb. Chefman&#x27;s own store currently publishes RJ38-3DBW-V2 (&#x27;TurboFry 6 Qt. Double Basket Air Fryer with Easy View Windows&#x27;: 6 Qt / 5.68 L as two 3 Qt / 2.84 L baskets, 90–400 °F / 32–204 °C, 1700 W, five functions — air fry, bake, reheat, dehydrate, frozen — easy-view windows, interior lights, shake notifications, 4.5 stars from 30 reviews, $99.99 list), RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2 (dual-basket, 4.6 from 194, no specification block published) and RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W (dual-window, 200–450 °F, Hi-Fry, 4.6 from 1,305). The site records the -2W mapping because the Amazon title says &#x27;Hi-Fry&#x27; and &#x27;Easy View Windows&#x27;, and Hi-Fry is documented only on the -2W page — but Easy View windows appear on all three, so the title does not discriminate on its own. This is an identification risk, not an identification. Two other builds shipped in this repo (site/, research/) map the same ASIN to RJ38-3DBW-V2 with no caveat, so a reader can reach two different official-looking answers two clicks apart. Decision needed: open the listing, read the model plate or the &#x27;Product information&#x27; model number, and settle it once. **Addendum 2026-11-16 (session arena/ba29c641): the read was done, by fetch.** The listing&#x27;s Product information block states Model Number and Part Number RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W — the root site&#x27;s mapping, not the site/ and research/ mapping to RJ38-3DBW-V2. Those two builds now disagree with a document each of them can read; they are not edited here because those trees belong to other sessions, and the divergence is another reason to retire or re-key them (F44). See F68.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-6-qt-double-basket-air-fryer-with-easy-view-windows" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry 6 Qt. Double Basket Air Fryer with Easy View Windows (RJ38-3DBW-V2) — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S25</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-6-qt-dual-basket-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry® Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Basket Air Fryer (RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2) — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S27</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-6b2eddeb/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-6b2eddeb/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/site" target="_blank" rel="noopener">site/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/research" target="_blank" rel="noopener">research/ ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f33"><td class="num"><strong>F33</strong></td><td>The &#x27;Hi-Fry / 450 °F: None&#x27; row for the 8 Qt product is only true of the stainless SKU, and a second official SKU matches the Amazon title exactly.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry</div></td><td class="small">Found live 2026-11-14 by arena/6b2eddeb — narrows F3, does not resolve it. Chefman sells RJ38-SQSS-8T (TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel, max 400 °F / 205 °C, four food presets, stainless finish — the SKU the site currently maps) and RJ38-8T-BLACK (TurboFry 8 Qt Air Fryer w/ High Fry: Hi-Fry 450 °F / 232 °C for the final two minutes, and exactly four functions — air fry, bake, dehydrate, frozen — in a matte-black finish). The Amazon listing title for B08DKYBTPH reads &#x27;…4-in-1 with 450°F Hi-Fry Option… | Bake, Dehydrate, Frozen | … Stainless Steel&#x27;. The function list and the 450 °F Hi-Fry match RJ38-8T-BLACK word for word; the finish matches RJ38-SQSS-8T. So the title may describe a third variant, or may carry a finish that does not match the unit. The previous framing — &#x27;one of the two official sources is stale&#x27; — is now the less likely explanation. No value is changed and neither model is asserted; a human should read the model number printed in the listing&#x27;s Product information section. **Read 2026-11-16 by session arena/ba29c641, by fetch:** the listing states Part Number RJ38-SQSS-8T *and* Max Temperature Setting 400 °F — its identifier field points at the 400 °F model while its title describes the -BLACK&#x27;s Hi-Fry capability. That is a listing carrying a title from somewhere else, which is not the same as proof of which machine ships: because RJ38-8T-BLACK genuinely documents 450 °F (S26), the two-models explanation this row opened stays live and both readings are published.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-8-qt-air-fryer-w-high-fry" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry 8 Qt. Air Fryer w/ High Fry (RJ38-8T-BLACK) — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S26</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-6b2eddeb/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-6b2eddeb/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f34"><td class="num"><strong>F34</strong></td><td>The second executive summary at the repo root still asserts two things that are now known to be wrong.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-14 by arena/6b2eddeb. EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md (the older of the two root summaries) states in its Key findings: &#x27;Identity verified. All three short links resolve to Amazon.com product pages with different ASINs&#x27; and &#x27;the official commission schedule redirected to a sign-in page&#x27;. This session re-fetched both on 2026-11-14: the Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 and Bounty Events) was read in full with no login, and product identity is explicitly NOT verified anywhere on this site (F14–F16, F21, F32, F33). A dated correction banner was placed above that document by an earlier pass, but the contradicting sentences remain in the body and the file is still served from the site root by direct link. Decision needed: retire the duplicate document, or rewrite the two sentences. This pass did not edit it — the sentence is another session&#x27;s published work and rewriting it is a content decision, not a verification one.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f35"><td class="num"><strong>F35</strong></td><td>Nine separate HTML sites for the same three products are published in this repository, and they disagree with each other.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Whole repository</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-14 by arena/6b2eddeb. Alongside the canonical root build there are research/ (12 pages), session-3740f69d/ (11), s2/ (13), site/ (11), dashboard/ (14), docs/ (7) and annex-88769516/ (10), plus data-only trees in crosscheck/ and platform/. The root navigation links to six of them. They disagree on facts a reader would take as settled: the Chefman 6 Qt model (RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W on the root site, RJ38-3DBW-V2 in site/ and research/ — F32), and the policy section numbers for the review/star-rating and link-shortening rules (§6(t)/§6(w) on the root site after F27, §3(t)/§2(w) in site/ and session-3740f69d/). Each build is internally consistent and individually verified; the problem is that nothing tells a reader which one is canonical when they arrive from a search engine. Decision needed: keep one site and archive the rest behind a clearly labelled &#x27;previous verification passes&#x27; page, or make every non-canonical page carry a fixed banner naming the canonical build.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/site" target="_blank" rel="noopener">site/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/session-3740f69d" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-3740f69d/ ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f41"><td class="num"><strong>F41</strong></td><td>F10&#x27;s earlier wording incorrectly said the short links&#x27; srctok marker stays constant across repeat requests.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three links — historical wording correction</div></td><td class="small">Closed 2026-11-15 by arena/94a562a1-zone1: F10 now states that the recorded candidate ASIN/title destinations were consistent while btn_ref/srctok values changed across some repeat or cache-busted requests. The underlying records include srctok-2cef40d2904c29d8 on 2026-11-13; srctok-1e229870efde5acf changing to srctok-397b5ba66db39bbf on a cache-busted repeat (session-81495d2d evidence, row 4); srctok-704dc575b940a94c (session-54c66535); and srctok-0740a2e990c9bbb3 in two observations later that day. This closes only the wording mismatch. It does not prove that any candidate listing is the product intended by the account holder or confirm affiliate attribution; see F1, F6, F14-F16 and F21.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-81495d2d/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-81495d2d/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/session-ce4070ad/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-ce4070ad/EVIDENCE-2026-11-14.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f37"><td class="num"><strong>F37</strong></td><td>A published value was cited to a source that does not state it.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Emeril 26 QT French Door — the &quot;Interior light: Yes&quot; row</div></td><td class="small">Found and fixed 2026-11-14 by sess-7111e2b6. The row cited the brand product page (S14), whose specification strip lists Timer, 26-Quart Capacity, Dual-Speed Fan, 25 Cooking Pre-sets, 16.9&quot;D x 15.8&quot;W x 14.8&quot;H, 24.6 Liters, Temperature Control and 120 volts — and no interior light. The value is nonetheless correct: the owner&#x27;s manual (S15) documents it in the control-panel section, &quot;F. LIGHT BUTTON: May be selected at any time during the cooking process to light the appliance&#x27;s interior.&quot; The row has been re-sourced from S14 to S15 and the manual&#x27;s wording recorded in the row note. The lesson is published rather than quietly fixed: a value can be true and still be mis-cited, and a citation that does not support its value is as much a defect as an invented number.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0687/4388/8948/files/FAFO-001_VN_32451_EF_IB_T22-5004282-C_1.pdf" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Owner&#x27;s Manual, model FAFO-001 (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S15</a></td></tr><tr id="f38"><td class="num"><strong>F38</strong></td><td>An Amazon listing title changed between the 2026-11-14 read and 2026-11-15, so a published value was stale.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Emeril 26 QT French Door — the listing-title row</div></td><td class="small">Found and fixed 2026-11-15 by arena/3ddfa50e. The stored title ended &#x27;| 26QT air fryer oven with French doors, 24 cooking functions, dual-speed fan and rotisserie for easy family meals.&#x27; and the live title on the same ASIN (B09B7SB46R, reached through https://link.amazon/B02NbDxp3) now ends &#x27;| Countertop Convection Cooker, 24 One-Touch Preset Options, Extra Large Interior, Six Cooking Accessories Included&#x27;. The preset count is unchanged at 24; the wording around it is not. The row now carries the 2026-11-15 string, keeps the superseded string in a visible field, and states the date of the read. This is the same class of volatility as F14 (brand-hosted review counts that moved in 24 hours) and the reason every volatile value on this site must carry its read date.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a></td></tr><tr id="f39"><td class="num"><strong>F39</strong></td><td>A source record was cited for a value it does not support: one registered URL stood in for three different product pages.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three products — the listing-title and ASIN rows</div></td><td class="small">Found and fixed 2026-11-15 by arena/3ddfa50e. Source S16 was registered with a single URL (https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3) but its title claimed to cover &#x27;ASINs B0CDY277D3, B09B7SB46R, B08DKYBTPH&#x27;, and it was cited as the source for all three products&#x27; listing titles and ASIN rows. For the Emeril and 8 Qt rows, the source link therefore took a reader to the Chefman 6 Qt listing. A citation that does not resolve to the page stating the value is the same defect class as F37. Fixed by registering each ASIN separately: S16 = B0CDY277D3, S28 = B09B7SB46R, S29 = B08DKYBTPH, and repointing every citation. The root build&#x27;s row-source gate did not catch this because it checks that a row links to *a* registered official host, not that the host page actually states the value.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a></td></tr><tr id="f40"><td class="num"><strong>F40</strong></td><td>Two different rows were published under the same flag number.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Register integrity — F36</div></td><td class="small">Found and fixed 2026-11-15 by arena/3ddfa50e. The programme-level row recording the srctok-marker finding (added by sess-ce4070ad, itself renumbered from F32) was published as F36, and a product-level row in data/products.json recording the Chefman 6 Qt preset-count mis-citation was also published as F36. Both rendered on the Flagged page, so the register showed 38 rows with 37 distinct numbers and any citation of &#x27;F36&#x27; was ambiguous. F28 records an earlier ID collision of the same kind, which means the rule was not enforceable. The programme-level row is renumbered F41; the product row keeps F36 because three session evidence files cite it by that number. A duplicate-ID build test now fails the build if this recurs.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f42"><td class="num"><strong>F42</strong></td><td>The operator&#x27;s location is stated two different ways on the same domain, and neither statement comes from an official source.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: questions.html (Q-06) and suggestions.html (SG-10) vs the s2/ annex register (its F08)</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by arena-b1e7b4bc. The 2026-11-15 pages say the owner is in Osaka, Japan; the s2 annex register (data/flags.json, row F08) records a Norway residence. Both were written from agent-session environment context, not from anything the owner supplied in the repository and not from any official page, so neither can be verified from here and at most one can be right. The Japan wording matters because Q-06 and SG-10 build a store-targeting decision on it. Decision needed: the owner states their actual country once, one register row cites it, and the other wording is corrected to match; until then no eligibility conclusion (Q-06, SG-10, or the s2 register&#x27;s F08) should be treated as settled.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/suggestions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">suggestions.html ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/questions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">questions.html ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/flags.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/flags.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/s2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">s2/ ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f44"><td class="num"><strong>F44</strong></td><td>The same site is published nine times from one repository, and eight of those copies are reachable at public URLs.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Whole repository — GitHub Pages deployment</div></td><td class="small">Measured 2026-11-15 by this pass: 106 HTML files are published from the repository root. The maintained site is the 27 sitemap URLs generated by build.py; the remaining pages are seven earlier or parallel builds (dashboard/, annex-88769516/, session-3740f69d/, research/, s2/, site/, docs/), each with its own homepage, its own near-duplicate comparison of the same three products, and in two cases a visible Amazon price. Amazon&#x27;s Program Policies §2(b) permit price display only when Amazon serves the link or the data comes from Creators/PA API, so a published copy that shows a price is a compliance exposure this project has otherwise been careful to avoid. Google&#x27;s own guidance (S30) states that the presumption is that individual pages match the overall quality of the domain, that a site reputation review weighs whether &#x27;the same identical or near-identical content appears on multiple other sites&#x27;, and that scaled content generated without added value for users should be excluded from search. Decision needed, and it is the owner&#x27;s: keep the older builds published for auditability and accept the duplicate-content and compliance risk, or remove the duplicate pages and keep only their evidence files. This pass did not delete or edit another session&#x27;s published output — that is not a unilateral decision to take. Full option set: SG-17 on the Suggestions page.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies" title="Google Search Central — Spam policies for Google web search" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S30</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/dashboard" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dashboard/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/annex-88769516" target="_blank" rel="noopener">annex-88769516/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/session-3740f69d" target="_blank" rel="noopener">session-3740f69d/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/research" target="_blank" rel="noopener">research/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/s2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">s2/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/site" target="_blank" rel="noopener">site/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/tree/main/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">docs/ ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/sitemap.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sitemap.xml ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f45"><td class="num"><strong>F45</strong></td><td>The site&#x27;s search-engine plumbing was incomplete: no page declared a canonical URL, and no page carried Open Graph or structured data.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Whole root site — every generated page</div></td><td class="small">Found and fixed 2026-11-15 by this pass, and measured before the fix: 0 of 27 generated pages had rel=canonical, 0 had og:* tags, 0 had a Twitter card, 0 had JSON-LD (a repository-wide count that also included the annex builds was 33 canonicals of 106 pages, from other sessions&#x27; templates). Two consequences: with nine copies of the same content on one origin, nothing told a search engine which copy is maintained; and a shared link rendered as a bare URL. Fixed in build.py&#x27;s layout() so every generated page now carries canonical, og:*, twitter:card and schema.org WebSite/Organization/BreadcrumbList/Article markup, and 404.html carries noindex and no canonical. Enforced by a new build guard (check_seo_metadata) so a later template edit cannot drop it. schema.org Product/Offer/AggregateRating is deliberately NOT emitted: the site publishes no Amazon price or star rating (§2(b), §6(t)), so product markup would either be empty or would assert a number this project refuses to publish.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies" title="Google Search Central — Spam policies for Google web search" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S30</a> <a class="src" href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/qualify-outbound-links" title="Google Search Central — Qualify your outbound links to Google" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S31</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/sitemap.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sitemap.xml ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f43"><td class="num"><strong>F43</strong></td><td>Two wording advisories on the Suggestions and Questions pages (reader-facing precision, no data value affected).<div class="tiny muted">Scope: suggestions.html and questions.html (PR #58, X-633b115f)</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by arena-b1e7b4bc. (a) The Suggestions lede says &#x27;None of them has been implemented&#x27;, but for SG-03, SG-04, SG-05 and SG-11 option A is the current, already-implemented state (&#x27;Keep...&#x27;, &#x27;Keep blank and link to the live Amazon page (current, compliant)&#x27;), so those rows read more accurately as &#x27;confirm the current state&#x27; than as pending work. (b) Q-01&#x27;s &#x27;How to find out&#x27; says to check the owner&#x27;s Associates account for which tag the short links carry - the tag is not observable from the account&#x27;s tracking-ID page either (flag F6); only whoever created the short links knows the mapping. Decision needed: reword the lede and the Q-01 cell, or accept the current phrasing. Both rows&#x27; facts and sources check out; this flag is about wording only.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/suggestions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">suggestions.html ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/questions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">questions.html ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f47"><td class="num"><strong>F47</strong></td><td>There are two flag registers in this repository, they contradict each other, and both are readable from the live site.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: data/flags.json (F01-F27) vs flagged.html (F1-F43)</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-15 by arena/792c279e-zone1. data/flags.json still records F02 &#x27;The official Standard Commission Income schedule redirected to a sign-in page&#x27; and F01-F04 language asserting that the short links resolve &#x27;with different ASINs&#x27;. The re-verification performed in this same pass read the Standard Commission Income Statement in full without any login (Table 1: Kitchen 4.50%, All Other 4.00%) and re-confirmed all three destinations, so the older register&#x27;s two statements are contradicted by a live read. The rendered Flagged page says the opposite of data/flags.json on the commission question, so a reader who follows two links reaches two different answers. This pass did not edit data/flags.json: it is consumed by scripts/build_s2_site.py and scripts/build.mjs, and re-keying it would silently re-point another build&#x27;s citations. Decision needed: name one register canonical, then either re-key the other or replace its contradicting sentences with a dated pointer to this page. Evidence: the two files themselves, and reports/verification-2026-11-15.md section 1.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/flags.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/flags.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/sources.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/sources.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f48"><td class="num"><strong>F48</strong></td><td>The Amazon listing for the Emeril 26 QT says six cooking accessories; the site publishes seven.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Emeril 26 QT French Door — the Included accessories row</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-15 by arena/792c279e-zone1 while re-reading the live listing title. ASIN B09B7SB46R&#x27;s title now ends &#x27;... Extra Large Interior, Six Cooking Accessories Included&#x27;, while the site&#x27;s own row publishes 7, sourced to the brand product page and the owner&#x27;s manual. This is not necessarily a contradiction: a listing can count a rotisserie spit and its forks as one accessory, or bundle differently from the retail box, and the brand page and manual are the sources this site chose. The value was therefore NOT changed — altering it would mean replacing a manual-sourced number with a listing-title number on a row whose product identity is itself unconfirmed (F1, F2). Decision needed: count the accessories in the box against the manual&#x27;s accessory list, then publish one number with that source. Evidence: reports/verification-2026-11-15.md section 3 row V21.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0687/4388/8948/files/FAFO-001_VN_32451_EF_IB_T22-5004282-C_1.pdf" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Owner&#x27;s Manual, model FAFO-001 (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S15</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f49"><td class="num"><strong>F49</strong></td><td>The 2026-11-15 live pass could not retrieve the one fact that closes four high-severity flags, and the site now says why in the place a buyer will look.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three products — the model-number mapping</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by arena/792c279e-zone1. The three Amazon detail pages were fetched again and returned the buy box, the protection plans and the &#x27;Customers who viewed this item also viewed&#x27; blocks, but not the &#x27;Product information&#x27; table that carries the model number. That block is rendered client-side, so an automated read cannot see it; a human browser can, in about ten seconds per listing. This is recorded as its own row because seven sessions have now listed F1/F2/F32/F33 as &#x27;needs a human&#x27; without saying why the machine cannot do it, which invites an eighth session to try again. It is now also stated on picks.html, handover.html and hand-over item H1 rather than only in the register. Evidence: reports/verification-2026-11-15.md section 3 row V24. **TESTED AND FALSIFIED 2026-11-16 by session arena/ba29c641-zone1.** The same /dp/ URLs were fetched from the same kind of environment and the Product information block was present in the response body for all three ASINs, model rows included, so the block is not unreachable to an automated read; whether it renders depends on the render path, not on the existence of the data. The row is kept rather than deleted because the failure it documents is real and worth reading: a negative observation about a fetch was promoted to a rule (&#x27;only a human can do this&#x27;), that rule was copied forward by six later passes, and it then gated four flags, two questions and the top item of the decision queue. A &#x27;needs a human&#x27; claim is a claim about what a tool can reach, and it should be re-tested on the same schedule as any other checkable value. See F68.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/reports/verification-2026-11-15.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">reports/verification-2026-11-15.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f54"><td class="num"><strong>F54</strong></td><td>The Amazon department category is now read directly on all three listings; the rate itself still needs the Earnings report.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three products — commission rate</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by session 42ca97ad. The annex register’s row F-06 recorded that the browse-node path ‘Home &amp; Kitchen &gt; Kitchen &amp; Dining &gt; Small Appliances &gt; Fryers &gt; Air Fryers’ had been read directly on B09B7SB46R only, and named reading the other two listings as the confirmation step. This session fetched all three supplied link.amazon short links and read the identical path on B0CDY277D3 and B08DKYBTPH as well, so the evidence is now three for three rather than one for three. It still does not establish the category Amazon applies when a commission is settled: only the Associates Earnings report names the category on a shipped line, so the two candidate rows (Kitchen 4.50%, All Other 4.00%) remain the honest presentation until a sale exists. Decision needed: publish Kitchen 4.50% as the working rate with the breadcrumb cited (SG-10 path A), or keep both rows until the first Earnings report lands.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a></td></tr><tr id="f55"><td class="num"><strong>F55</strong></td><td>Eleven article sentences spliced a published display label into a mid-sentence position. Found, fixed and guarded.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: articles/ prose, and the build’s article gate</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by session 42ca97ad. The article prose in data/articles.json interpolates published values into sentences. Eleven of those interpolations put a display label — ‘Two 3-quart baskets’, ‘Designed for 4-6 people’, ‘Fries, Chicken, Fish, Meat (one-touch)’, ‘Limited 1-year warranty …’ — into a position where its capital is wrong, so the sentences read as machine output: ‘The dual-basket unit is 6 Qt / 5.67 L as Two 3-quart baskets’, ‘splits a 6 Qt / 5.67 L cavity into Two 3-quart baskets’. The defect was invisible to every earlier verification pass, because each value is genuinely published and correct in a table; it only appears when the page is read as prose, which is what a shopper does. It survived PR #71’s full rewrite of data/articles.json, which re-introduced the same ten sentences and added an eleventh in a new article. Fixed by adding a {{value::prose form}} syntax whose prose form may differ from the published value in letter case and nothing else, enforced by check_articles(), and by adding check_article_splices(), which fails the build when a capitalised value is spliced into a mid-sentence position. Both guards were proven to fire: the build failed on exactly these defects before the fix and passes after it. No published value changed and no source changed.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f56"><td class="num"><strong>F56</strong></td><td>The Application Review Process page was not re-read this pass; its figures are carried forward from an earlier fetch.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: S4 — the Associates application criteria</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by session 42ca97ad. This pass re-fetched the Program Policies, the Standard Commission Income Statement, both Chefman product pages, the Emeril product page, all three owner’s manuals, all three supplied short links, 16 CFR Part 255 and 16 CFR Part 465. It did not load the Application Review Process page (S4). The figures the repository carries for it — three qualifying sales within 180 days, roughly ten posts, content recent within about sixty days, a social follower floor of about five hundred — are carried forward from earlier logged fetches and are NOT re-confirmed by this pass. The sixty-day recency requirement is load-bearing for any content-refresh schedule the owner adopts, so it must be re-read before it is used as a scheduling rule rather than quoted from this repository. Note that a sibling pass on the same day did re-read this page and reported the same four figures, which corroborates them but does not change what this session itself fetched. What was and was not fetched is listed item by item in session-42ca97ad/EVIDENCE-2026-11-15.md section 6. **Re-read 2026-11-16 by arena/9fccce0c-zone1: all four figures are confirmed verbatim from the live page** — “we require at least three within the first 180 days”, “a good rule of thumb is at least 10 posts”, “Content on your website must be recent (generally within the last 60 days)” and “in most cases, at least 500” organic followers/likes — so the carried-forward part of this row is closed, and question Q-19 moves from *missing* to *known and sourced* with its answer on the row. Evidence: reports/verification-2026-11-16-9fccce0c.md row V3.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/G8TW5AE9XL2VX9VM" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Application Review Process" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S4</a></td></tr><tr id="f57"><td class="num"><strong>F57</strong></td><td>Three files under data/ are read by no build, and one of them is a stale copy of a register that is published.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: data/flagged.json, data/commission-rates.csv, data/products.csv</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by session 42ca97ad. A reference scan of every loader in the repository shows that scripts/build_site.py and scripts/verify_site.py read data/verified/, not data/. The root-level data/flagged.json is therefore an orphan: it holds a 13-row register whose live counterpart is data/verified/flagged.json (17 rows), and the two disagree across 85 lines — including the browse-node observation this pass has just updated, which would otherwise leave two registers saying different things about the same fact. data/commission-rates.csv and data/products.csv are read by nothing in the repository; the first is a CSV transcription of the Standard Commission Income Statement, the second the 2026-11-13 link-check ledger that records the bare-token 404 and the fallback redirect, and both are evidence worth keeping if they are labelled as evidence. Nothing published depends on any of the three, so this is a maintenance risk rather than a correctness bug, but a reader who opens data/flagged.json sees a register that no longer matches the site. This row is narrower than F47, which covers the same class of problem for data/flags.json; the two are kept separate because the files and the contradictions are different. Decision needed: delete the three orphans, or keep them explicitly as dated evidence with a header that says so.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f58"><td class="num"><strong>F58</strong></td><td>A register preamble repeated a sentence on the published page. Found on the deployed site, fixed, and guarded.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: questions.html — the preamble above the register</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-15 by session 42ca97ad, after PR #78 merged: the sentence ‘Questions in the ‘known and sourced’ state carry their answer on the row …’ appeared twice in the preamble above the questions register. The cause was the one-shot editor that adds the register rows (session-42ca97ad/apply_pass9_edits.py): it appended the sentence to the preamble unconditionally, so running it a second time appended it again. The build did not catch it, because the duplicate-ID guard sees IDs, the published-value guard sees numbers, and a repeated sentence is neither. Fixed by making the editor idempotent, by removing the duplicate from data/questions.json, and by adding check_register_preambles(), which fails the build if any register preamble repeats a sentence. The guard was proven to fire on an injected duplicate. The same edit also corrected the preamble&#x27;s own claim that the page ‘separates three things’ - it has always described four states, which is what the legend on the page lists.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f59"><td class="num"><strong>F59</strong></td><td>The repository serves six separate executive summaries, five of them stale and none labelled stale.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY.md, EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS8.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS9.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS9-42CA97AD.md, executive-summary.html</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/ad8105b3. A read of the repository root finds six files whose names all claim to be the executive summary: the generated executive-summary.html (current, built from data), EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY.md, and four earlier or parallel-session Markdown files (EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS8.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS9.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS9-42CA97AD.md). GitHub Pages publishes the repository root, so all six are reachable by URL. At least one, EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md, is already recorded in this register (F28) as asserting &#x27;Identity verified. All three short links resolve to Amazon.com product pages with different ASINs&#x27;, which this site&#x27;s reconciled position contradicts. This pass did not delete or rewrite any of them: they are other sessions&#x27; committed records, and deleting another session&#x27;s evidence is not a repair. The live site links only executive-summary.html. Decision needed: keep one Markdown briefing and move the rest under an archive directory with a one-line header saying what superseded them, or add a banner to each. Evidence: the six files themselves, and the root README. **Disposition recorded 2026-11-16 (session arena/ba29c641): the banner option was taken,** because deleting another session&#x27;s committed evidence is not a repair. Each non-canonical briefing now opens with a &#x27;Superseded, and kept&#x27; line naming its session and date, pointing at executive-summary.html, and stating the specific claim inside it that the repository has since reversed. Covered: EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md (whose 2026-11-14 banner also carried a stale count, refreshed here), EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS8.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS9.md, EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS9-42CA97AD.md and EXECUTIVE-SUMMARY-PASS10-121929a5.md — six Markdown briefings beside the generated page. Nothing was deleted and no historical sentence was rewritten.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a></td></tr><tr id="f60"><td class="num"><strong>F60</strong></td><td>The s2 annex&#x27;s requirements register cites two paths that do not exist, and its commission row rests on a premise the annex itself has since corrected.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: data/s2/requirements.json rows R-01 and R-08</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/ad8105b3 while building the root requirements register (requirements.html). Two defects in the annex register. (1) R-01 &#x27;Collect and organise a full list of requirements&#x27; gives its implementation path as &#x27;data/requirements.json rendered on Requirements page&#x27;. There is no data/requirements.json: the file is data/s2/requirements.json, and it renders at s2/pages/requirements.html. So the repository&#x27;s only requirements register pointed the reader at a different, non-existent file. This is the third occurrence of the same defect class (after F14 and F38 in this register), which is why the new root register has check_requirement_paths() failing the build on any cited path that does not exist, and that guard was proven to fire on an injected bad path. (2) R-08 &#x27;Analyse commission potential&#x27; is marked blocked with the reason &#x27;login-gated rate card (F-04)&#x27;. data/s2/flags.json row F-04 is itself titled &#x27;CORRECTED — a public commission table does exist&#x27; and records that the Standard Commission Income Statement is readable without a login, which this pass re-confirmed live on 2026-11-16: Table 1 renders &#x27;Physical Books, Kitchen, Automotive 4.50%&#x27;. The genuine remaining gap is narrower — Table 1 is keyed to categories, not ASINs — and the root register states it that way. Neither row was edited in the annex: data/s2/requirements.json is another session&#x27;s published output. Decision needed: re-point R-01 at the real path, and restate R-08&#x27;s blocking reason as the ASIN-to-category mapping rather than a login. Evidence: the two annex files and the commission page. **Repaired at source 2026-11-16 (session arena/ba29c641), with the correction shown as a dated line inside the annex row rather than applied silently:** R-01 names both registers and the paths that exist, R-08 states the ASIN-to-category mapping as the blocker and cites the public Standard Commission Income Statement, and R-03&#x27;s &#x27;no public demand metric exists&#x27; was corrected to what is actually true (a listing does carry a rank line; this repository declines to publish it under IP License §2(e), F8). The annex was regenerated with its own generator, the only supported way to change s2/**.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a></td></tr><tr id="f51"><td class="num"><strong>F51</strong></td><td>The brand&#x27;s own store reports a different product weight than the brand&#x27;s own specification block, and this site publishes the specification-block figure.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Chefman 6 Qt Dual-Window (RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W) and Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry (RJ38-SQSS-8T)</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by arena/63a2c638, round 9. The Chefman product pages print a Specifications block: the 6 Qt page says &#x27;Weight: 13.3 lbs / 6.03 kg&#x27; and the 8 Qt page says &#x27;Weight: (8 Qt Model) 12.8 lbs / 5.8 kg&#x27;. Those are the figures this site publishes, cited to S10 and S12. The same brand&#x27;s own commerce endpoint, read the same day, reports the variant weight as 14.51 lb / 6,582 g for RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W (S32) and 13.4 lb / 6,078 g for RJ38-SQSS-8T (S33). The endpoint figure is the weight Shopify carries for shipping, so the most likely reading is product weight versus packaged weight - but neither page says so, and the site must not choose between them by inference. No published value was changed: the specification block remains the cited source because that is the document the published row points at. Decision needed: state in each weight row whether the figure is the product or the packaged weight, or publish both.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer.json" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman product endpoint for RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W (6 Qt Dual-Window) — machine-readable record served by chefman.com" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S32</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1.json" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman product endpoint for RJ38-SQSS-8T / RJ38-SQSS-5T (8 Qt and 5 Qt TurboFry Touch)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S33</a></td></tr><tr id="f52"><td class="num"><strong>F52</strong></td><td>The open-questions register states that dimensions, weight and warranty are missing for all three products; the product dataset already publishes eight of those nine values from official sources.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: questions.html row Q-06 vs data/products.json</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by arena/63a2c638, round 9. Q-06 asks &#x27;What are the exterior dimensions, weight and warranty terms for each of the three products?&#x27; and is filed as &#x27;not_stated&#x27;. Checking the nine cells against the dataset this pass: exterior dimensions are published for all three (6 Qt from S10, Emeril from S14, 8 Qt from S12); warranty is published for all three (S10, S14, S13); weight is published for both Chefman models (S10, S12). One cell - the Emeril 26 QT product weight - is genuinely absent, because neither the brand page nor the owner&#x27;s manual states one. So the register overstates the gap by eight cells. This matters in both directions: a reader using Q-06 to decide what to research would redo work already done, and a reader trusting the product pages would think the register is stale. Q-06 was narrowed this pass to the single cell that is actually open, and is cross-linked to this row. No data value moved.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0576/5755/7158/files/RJ38-SQSS-8T-UG-US.pdf" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry Touch Air Fryer RJ38-SQSS-8T User Guide (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S13</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a></td></tr><tr id="f61"><td class="num"><strong>F61</strong></td><td>The canonical compliance register and platform blueprint had never carried the two clauses that govern an AI-agent affiliate platform, and three published pages pointed at a flag number that does not exist.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Whole site — compliance.html, platform.html, questions.html, suggestions.html, data.html</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/9fccce0c-zone1 during verification pass 10, and fixed in the same pass. Two independent defects. (a) Missing clauses. The Associates Program Policies page (S2, “Updated: April 14, 2026”) carries IP License §4 “Agents” (the Agent Terms) and Participation Requirements §5, both read verbatim live on 2026-11-16. Neither appeared anywhere in the canonical build: data/compliance.json held 0 occurrences of “Agent Terms” and data/platform.json held 0 occurrences of “Agent”, although the brief this project answers is explicitly an AI-powered affiliate platform and the root site ships a platform blueprint that proposes automated research. They are now requirements C17 and C18, with platform guardrail G11; the scope question they raise is published as Q-21 and the design decision as SG-22, and neither is answered by inference. (b) Dangling pointer. data/questions.json, data/suggestions.json and data/sources.json each told the reader to see the flag numbered 46 for the Application Review Process recency criterion. No such flag exists in the canonical register, which runs F1–F45 and F47–F58, and none exists in the legacy data/flags.json register either, which runs F01–F27. The three pointers now name F56, the row that actually covers it. A new build guard, check_register_cross_references(), fails the build if any generated page cites a flag id, source id, question id or suggestion id that is not in the register it points at, so the class of defect cannot recur silently. Evidence: reports/verification-2026-11-16-9fccce0c.md section E.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/G8TW5AE9XL2VX9VM" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Application Review Process" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S4</a></td></tr><tr id="f62"><td class="num"><strong>F62</strong></td><td>IP License §4 (the Agent Terms) was not read end to end, so the full list of agent-identification requirements is not yet on the record.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: S2 — the IP License section of the Program Policies page</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/9fccce0c-zone1. The Associates Program Policies page renders its IP License as one long table, and the markdown conversion used for that pass collapsed part of it. Everything quoted in requirement C17 was read directly in the response body, but the section was not read to its end: sub-part (a) refers to “the requirements in section 3 of these Agent Terms”, and the text of that section was not visible in the render. The Agent Terms are therefore published as the rules this pass could read, with the limit stated on the row, rather than as a complete checklist. Decision needed: read IP License §4 in a browser, in full, and extend requirement C17 if it adds anything — before the research agent is built (SG-22). Evidence: reports/verification-2026-11-16-9fccce0c.md, the limit stated under section A.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f53"><td class="num"><strong>F53</strong></td><td>The Amazon browse-node breadcrumb is now published for two ASINs, and it does not settle which commission rate applies.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: All three products - the &#x27;Amazon browse node&#x27; row and open question Q-11</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-15 by arena/63a2c638, round 9, at the same time as the value itself was added, so the limit travels with the value. Both Chefman listings render the breadcrumb &#x27;Home &amp; Kitchen &gt; Kitchen &amp; Dining &gt; Small Appliances &gt; Fryers &gt; Air Fryers&#x27; (S16, S29). It is tempting to read &#x27;Kitchen&#x27; there and apply the 4.50% Kitchen rate from the Commission Income Statement (S3). That would be an inference, not a source: S3&#x27;s categories are commission categories defined by the Associates programme, Amazon&#x27;s browse nodes are a shopper-navigation taxonomy, and Amazon publishes no mapping between the two. The same breadcrumb begins &#x27;Home &amp; Kitchen&#x27;, and S3 also carries &#x27;Home&#x27; at 3.00%, so the breadcrumb alone cannot even tell you which of the two it resembles. The site therefore continues to show the commission rate as unconfirmed (Q-11) and now shows the browse node as a separate, sourced fact. The Emeril browse node is blank: three fetches of that ASIN on 2026-11-15 returned Amazon&#x27;s automated &#x27;Continue shopping&#x27; checkpoint instead of the listing page, and a value that was never rendered cannot be recorded. **Update 2026-11-16, from three reads on one day:** pass 10 (arena/121929a5) filled the Emeril row by reaching the listing through the supplied short link; arena/a746a9f2 re-read the breadcrumb directly and recorded that the checkpoint had cleared; session arena/ba29c641 fetched the /dp/ URL independently and found the same classification in the page&#x27;s Best Sellers Rank line (Kitchen &amp; Dining; Air Fryers node 17659096011). Three of three ASINs now carry a browse classification from three routes, and none of the three carries a commission category: the inference this row forbids is still forbidden, as arena/a746a9f2 put it.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a></td></tr><tr id="f46"><td class="num"><strong>F46</strong></td><td>Product photography rights are undecided: no row records which images, if any, the site publishes, whose permission covers them, or how Amazon Program Content image rights apply to these specific products. Amazon&#x27;s Operating Agreement and Program Policies govern Program Content imagery; the FTC treats imagery as part of an endorsement requiring clear disclosure of material connections. No product photography is published and Q-22 asks what may be published and whose permission covers it.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: SG-23, Q-22 and all three product pages</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/695f03b7-zone1. The design layer offers owner photos, licensed imagery and Amazon Program Content as image options, but none is decided; until Q-22 is answered, the site stays text-and-diagrams-only and the figures on figures.html are generated diagrams, not photographs. Evidence: data/suggestions.json (SG-23), data/questions.json (Q-22), build.py flags register.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a></td></tr><tr id="f63"><td class="num"><strong>F63</strong></td><td>Fourth occurrence of the stale-count defect class: three registers asserted &#x27;eight articles&#x27; after the set grew to thirteen. Found and fixed.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: data/questions.json Q-10, data/suggestions.json SG-09, data/decision.json niche rows</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/a746a9f2 (X-a746a9f2). The brief&#x27;s line-by-line review grepped every generated page for register counts and found live assertions that had gone stale as the article set grew: questions.html (Q-10&#x27;s &#x27;what we know&#x27; cell and the page&#x27;s JSON-LD) and suggestions.html (SG-09&#x27;s title, problem, evidence label and recommendation) both said &#x27;eight articles&#x27;, and decisions.html carried &#x27;the site&#x27;s existing eight articles&#x27; plus a pill reading &#x27;Eight articles already exist in this shape&#x27; - while the articles index publishes thirteen. This is the same defect class as F14, F38 and F52: a register cell written as a snapshot and read as a live fact. Fixed per the F52 precedent: the two rows inside this session&#x27;s claimed scope were corrected in place, and the third (data/decision.json, created by a completed session) was corrected as an integration-scope wording fix, with before/after strings logged in COORDINATION.md. All three now carry a dated count (13, 2026-11-16) plus a pointer to the live articles index, so they read as dated snapshots rather than live claims. The same scan cleared the &#x27;parallel register&#x27; pages (decision-gate.html&#x27;s fourteen questions, marketing-directions.html&#x27;s twelve suggestions) as internally consistent — their ledes render their own frozen register&#x27;s length — and cleared the historical recounts in reverification.html and executive-summary.html, which describe already-fixed defects. No build guard can police free-prose counts without a whitelist of claim sentences; check_register_preambles covers repeated sentences only. Decision needed: adopt computed counts in register prose (SG-26 option A machinery) or accept dated snapshots as the house standard.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/questions.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/questions.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/suggestions.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/suggestions.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/decision.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/decision.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f64"><td class="num"><strong>F64</strong></td><td>Three registers carried duplicated content onto the published site. Found by line-by-line review, repaired, and guarded.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: questions.html row Q-11, the articles note, and one Chefman 8 Qt specification row</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/b23f12f0, round 11. A line-by-line read of data/ found three places where the same text was stored more than once and then published more than once: (1) the Q-11 row of the open-questions register carried three of its &#x27;what we know&#x27; entries seven times each - 18 entries collapsing to 6 - so the sentence &#x27;The same breadcrumb does NOT settle this question&#x27; appeared 14 times in the rendered questions.html; (2) the note field in data/articles.json repeated its first paragraph verbatim, joined by a pipe, so the editorial discipline statement was printed twice; (3) the &#x27;Hi-Fry / 450 F mode&#x27; row of the Chefman 8 Qt product cited source S29 three times in a row, which a reader sees as three source badges for one claim. None of the three is a wrong fact - each is the same fact printed repeatedly, which is precisely the pattern that makes a page read as machine-generated. All three were repaired with an order-preserving de-duplication that changed no wording. A build guard, check_register_duplicates(), now fails the build if any register list repeats an entry or any register string repeats a sentence, and the guard was proven to fire on an injected duplicate before it was committed.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GRXPHT8U84RAYDXZ" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Standard Commission Income Statement (Table 1 &amp; Bounty Events)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S3</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/questions.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/questions.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/articles.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/articles.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f65"><td class="num"><strong>F65</strong></td><td>A recorded conclusion about Amazon’s bot checkpoint was wrong: the block is intermittent per request, not a property of the ASIN.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: data/sources.json source S28, data/products.json, and the &#x27;Amazon browse node&#x27; row of the Emeril 26 QT product</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/b23f12f0, round 11, because an earlier pass recorded a conclusion that this pass contradicts. On 2026-11-15 three fetches of ASIN B09B7SB46R were each answered by Amazon’s automated &#x27;Continue shopping&#x27; checkpoint, while the two Chefman ASINs fetched cleanly in the same window. The recorded conclusion was &#x27;this is a per-ASIN block, not a site-wide one&#x27;. Round 11 re-tested it: the Emeril ASIN loaded on the first attempt, and in the same session B0CDY277D3 - which had loaded cleanly the previous day - was served the checkpoint on its first attempt and returned the listing only on a retry. So the block is intermittent per request. The 2026-11-15 evidence could not have distinguished the two readings, because a per-ASIN pattern and an intermittent pattern look identical across three failures, but the distinction matters: a failed fetch is not evidence about the ASIN, and no future pass should record &#x27;blocked&#x27; as a property of a listing. Separately, this pass filled the Emeril browse-node cell and then found that session 121929a5 (pass 10) had filled the same cell independently on the same day, by the same route - the detail page and the supplied short link - reaching the identical string. Two independent reads agreeing is worth more than one, so both are recorded on the row and both sources cited (S28, S17). This pass also removed a stale &#x27;unverified&#x27; field that the earlier fill had left behind, which still read &#x27;Not read ... left blank&#x27; on a row that now carried a value; it did not render, but it would have contradicted the value if the render rule ever changed.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B04G1MEwY" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied by the account holder (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S17</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/sources.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/sources.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f66"><td class="num"><strong>F66</strong></td><td>The Emeril brand page now documents 24 presets and 25 presets on the same page, and states a different accessory count from its own Amazon listing title.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: Emeril 26 QT French Door - the preset count and the included-accessories count</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-16 by arena/b23f12f0, round 11, from a source this project had not previously read: the brand storefront’s own machine-readable product record (S35), whose description text carries an updated_at stamp of 2026-11-16. It states &#x27;Equipped with 24 one-touch cooking presets&#x27; and &#x27;Included are 7 premium accessories&#x27;. Two consequences. On presets: the brand’s specification block still says &#x27;25 Cooking Pre-sets&#x27;, so the brand now contradicts itself within a single page rather than across two documents. The owner’s manual’s chart names 24 and the Amazon listing title says 24, so 24 now has three statements behind it and the site continues to publish 24 - but the conflict is recorded as live and unresolved, because the brand has not withdrawn the 25. On accessories: the site publishes 7 on the strength of the brand page, while the current Amazon listing title for the same ASIN reads &#x27;Six Cooking Accessories Included&#x27;. Both figures are quoted, neither was reconciled by inference, and the discrepancy is published rather than averaged. Neither published value changed in this pass.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360.json" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril product endpoint for the French Door AirFryer 360 (SKU 4-00675-02A-VN)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S35</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0687/4388/8948/files/FAFO-001_VN_32451_EF_IB_T22-5004282-C_1.pdf" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Owner&#x27;s Manual, model FAFO-001 (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S15</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/sources.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/sources.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f67"><td class="num"><strong>F67</strong></td><td>The flag duplicate-ID guard had gone blind: it matched zero rows and reported success, so it could not catch a same-day collision between two sessions.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: build.py - check_flag_ids_unique(), and the Flagged page it reads</div></td><td class="small">Found independently by two sessions on 2026-11-16 and repaired by the first of them. This pass (arena/b23f12f0, round 11) found the defect while renumbering its own flags and checking whether the guard would object; session arena/2e346d24 located and repaired it on main first, and additionally added check_flag_count_matches_page() for the related defect where the landing page was advertising zero open flags beside a 61-row register. The defect: check_flag_ids_unique() counted flag rows with a pattern that began with a bare opening table-row tag followed immediately by the number cell, while page_flagged() has emitted a row id attribute since the per-row anchors landed. The pattern therefore matched nothing, and a Counter over an empty list reports no duplicates - so the guard returned success on every run while two sessions published different rows under F61 and F62 on the same day. This repository has been bitten by flag-ID collisions at least three times before (F28, F40 and the preamble case F58), which is why the guard exists. It is recorded here rather than in the repairing session&#x27;s own words because the finding belongs on the register even though the repair predates this pass landing. Nothing in this row is a claim by this pass to have made the fix.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/agreement" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Operating Agreement" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S1</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/flagged.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">flagged.html ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f68"><td class="num"><strong>F68</strong></td><td>The &#x27;only a human in a browser can read this&#x27; rule was untested for ten passes, and testing it took one fetch. Also: on one ASIN the model code is not in the field named Model Number.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: F1, F2, F32, F33, F49, SG-01, Q-01, Q-02, hand-over item H1, and every product page</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/ba29c641-zone1, as the consequence of F49. The register&#x27;s own explanation for why four flags needed a human was that the Product information table is rendered client-side and cannot be retrieved by a fetch. Three /dp/ URLs were fetched on 2026-11-16 and the block was present in each response, model rows included. Two lessons, both kept on the record. (1) A negative claim about tooling is an empirical claim and decays: &#x27;the fetch did not return it&#x27; became &#x27;a fetch cannot return it&#x27; without anyone re-testing, and six later passes copied the sentence forward, one of them into a hand-over sheet and a &#x27;three minutes of your time&#x27; callout on the landing page. (2) The method matters as much as the read: on B08DKYBTPH the field called Model Number holds the marketing string &#x27;Large Air Fryer&#x27; and the code sits in Part Number, so any procedure that reads &#x27;the Model Number row&#x27; — the exact procedure SG-01 told the owner to follow — returns a non-code for one of the three products. Both fields must be read, and the two are compared. What this pass did NOT do is convert a listing claim into a manufacturer specification: the 31 rows added to data/products.json are each labelled &#x27;as listed by Amazon&#x27; and sourced to S16/S28/S29, and product identity from the link side stays open (F6, F7, F10). The mechanism behind the earlier failed read is recorded separately and correctly by arena/b23f12f0 as F65 — the “Continue shopping” checkpoint is intermittent per request, not a property of the ASIN — so the 2026-11-15 observation that the table was missing was a real observation about three particular responses, and the error was generalising from them to a rule about all fetches. Both rows stand on their own reads.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0576/5755/7158/files/RJ38-SQSS-8T-UG-US.pdf" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry Touch Air Fryer RJ38-SQSS-8T User Guide (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S13</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0576/5755/7158/files/RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W-UG-US.pdf" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry Touch Dual Window Air Fryer RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W User Guide (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S18</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-8-qt-air-fryer-w-high-fry" title="Chefman (brand) — TurboFry 8 Qt. Air Fryer w/ High Fry (RJ38-8T-BLACK) — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S26</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/reports/asin-model-mapping-2026-11-16.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">reports/asin-model-mapping-2026-11-16.md ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f69"><td class="num"><strong>F69</strong></td><td>Two official documents disagree on the 8 Qt&#x27;s footprint, and one listing disagrees with itself about the 6 Qt&#x27;s capacity units.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: chefman-8qt-turbofry dimensions rows; chefman-6qt-dual-basket capacity rows</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/ba29c641-zone1, in the same read that closed F1. chefman.com publishes the 8 Qt (RJ38-SQSS-8T) as 15.4 in x 12.6 in x 13.5 in and separates the 5 Qt figures on the same page (S12); the listing for B08DKYBTPH publishes 12.75&quot;D x 13.5&quot;W x 10&quot;H (S29). Those are not two ways of writing one measurement: the depth differs by 2.65 in and the height by 3.5 in, so a buyer checking a cabinet clearance gets a different answer from each official source, and the weight agrees on both (12.8 lb) — which is what makes it a documentation problem rather than a transcription of a different product. Nothing was averaged: the brand figures stay the published row, the listing&#x27;s figures sit on their own dated row. The same class on the 6 Qt: its listing&#x27;s Capacity field reads &#x27;6 liters&#x27; while the same page&#x27;s title, its Size field and the brand page all read 6 quarts (5.67 L) — a unit label that moved. Any copy that turns a capacity or a footprint into advice (the capacity guide, the small-kitchen article) must carry the caveat until Chefman states which figures are the appliance and which are the carton. Decision needed: ask Chefman which number is the appliance footprint, or drop cabinet-clearance advice.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f70"><td class="num"><strong>F70</strong></td><td>Three official documents give three different model strings for the Emeril 26 QT, and the two newest differ by one character.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: emeril-26qt-french-door model_number and its listing rows</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/ba29c641-zone1. The owner&#x27;s manual is headed FAFO-001 (S15), the brand product page lists 4-00675-02A-VN (S14), and the listing for B09B7SB46R states 4-00675-02X-VN in both its Model Number and Part Number rows (S28). An &#x27;A&#x27; against an &#x27;X&#x27; in the tenth position is the whole difference, and it is the kind of difference that means either a variant suffix or a transcription error in one of three documents — no document in this repository says which. This is the one product where the live read did not settle the mapping the way it did for the two Chefman listings, so all three strings are quoted and none is asserted. The commercial reason it matters: a comparison page that prints the wrong SKU string is a page that can be accused of reviewing a different machine, on a site whose entire claim is traceability. Decision needed: Emeril Everyday support can state the SKU behind the ASIN in one reply; until then the product page shows three strings and the character in dispute.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0687/4388/8948/files/FAFO-001_VN_32451_EF_IB_T22-5004282-C_1.pdf" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Owner&#x27;s Manual, model FAFO-001 (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S15</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f71"><td class="num"><strong>F71</strong></td><td>The Emeril listing contradicts itself inside one table: 7 accessories in Configuration, six parts enumerated in Included Components, &#x27;Six&#x27; in the title. F48 records the six-vs-seven question; this row is about the self-contradiction.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: emeril-26qt-french-door &#x27;Included accessories&#x27;, &#x27;Configuration&#x27; and &#x27;Included components&#x27; rows</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/ba29c641-zone1, and it complicates F48 rather than repeating it. F48 was recorded 2026-11-15 from the listing *title* alone (&#x27;Six Cooking Accessories Included&#x27; against the site&#x27;s manual-sourced 7), and left open with the sensible observation that a listing may count a spit and its forks as one item. Reading the whole Product information block shows the page does not agree with itself: its Configuration field says &#x27;…with 24 Functions and 7 Accessories&#x27;, its Included Components row then enumerates six named parts (Rack, Bake Pan, Drip Tray, Crisper Basket, Rotisserie Spit, Fetch Tool), and its title says Six. So &#x27;the listing says six&#x27; is not a single fact to weigh against the manual — the listing says both, on one screen. The site&#x27;s published value stays 7 because the brand page and the manual state it (S14, S15); the Configuration field is a fourth document that agrees, and the enumeration is a second that does not. No averaging, no quiet correction. Decision needed: the packing list settles it. Note the same fetch also strengthened the other half of that row: &#x27;24 Functions&#x27; in the Configuration field is a third document agreeing with the manual&#x27;s 24 named presets against the brand page&#x27;s &#x27;25 Cooking Pre-sets&#x27; (F4). Distinct from F66, which records the brand page&#x27;s own 24/25 split and the title-vs-manual accessory disagreement: this row is the narrower observation that the two counts sit next to each other in one table on the listing.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://www.emerileveryday.com/products/emeril_lagasse_french_door_airfryer_360" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Toaster Oven Combo, 26 QT — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S14</a> <a class="src" href="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0687/4388/8948/files/FAFO-001_VN_32451_EF_IB_T22-5004282-C_1.pdf" title="Emeril Everyday (brand) — Emeril Lagasse French Door AirFryer 360 Owner&#x27;s Manual, model FAFO-001 (PDF)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S15</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B09B7SB46R" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B09B7SB46R (Emeril 26QT French Door)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S28</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f72"><td class="num"><strong>F72</strong></td><td>Temperature floors published in degrees Celsius do not convert to the floors published in degrees Fahrenheit, on two of the three products.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: chefman-6qt-dual-basket and chefman-8qt-turbofry temperature rows; any copy quoting a low end</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/ba29c641-zone1. The 6 Qt brand page gives 200-450 °F / 95-235 °C (S10); the same product&#x27;s listing states &#x27;Min Temperature Setting: 93 °C&#x27; (S16), and 93 °C is 199.4 °F, not 95 °C&#x27;s 203 °F. The 8 Qt listing states &#x27;Min 94 °C&#x27; (S29) against the brand page&#x27;s 200 °F floor (S12), i.e. 201.2 °F. Two degrees, in both cases, in the same direction: 200 °F is 93.33 °C, so someone rounded the Celsius figure up in one document and left the Fahrenheit figure alone in the other. This is a rounding inconsistency, not a second specification, and it is recorded rather than repaired because the site does not get to rewrite a manufacturer&#x27;s numbers. What it forbids is specific: an article that says &#x27;the 6 Qt goes down to 95 °C&#x27; is quoting one page and contradicting another, so low-end claims must show both units with the source that uses them, or stay out of the copy.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/products/turbofry-touch-dual-window-air-fryer" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman TurboFry Touch 6 Qt. Dual-Window Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S10</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a class="src" href="https://chefman.com/collections/air-fryers-1/products/turbofry-touch-air-fryer-1" title="Chefman (brand) — Chefman 8 Qt. TurboFry Touch Stainless-Steel Air Fryer — product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S12</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08DKYBTPH" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B08DKYBTPH (Chefman 8 Qt TurboFry)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S29</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/products.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/products.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f73"><td class="num"><strong>F73</strong></td><td>The requirements register promised computed counts in its own preamble and then typed eight of them into prose; four had already gone stale.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: data/requirements.json (rows A-02, A-04, A-06, B-01, B-05, B-06, B-08, C-02, D-05, G-01)</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/ba29c641-zone1 while updating the register for this pass&#x27;s rows. The file&#x27;s `counts_note` says: &#x27;Counts are computed from the rows at build time rather than typed, so this page cannot claim a total the register does not contain.&#x27; The status counts at the top of the page are indeed computed — every number inside the prose of individual rows was typed, and four had drifted within two days: &#x27;Forty-six spec rows&#x27; and &#x27;43 of 46 carry a value&#x27; (the dataset had moved to 46 and then to 77 as passes added sourced rows), &#x27;Twenty-one numbered suggestions (SG-01…SG-21)&#x27; against a register of 23, &#x27;Twenty questions&#x27; against 22, and &#x27;a generated sitemap of 37 URLs&#x27; against 46. None of these is a wrong fact about the world; each is a right fact about an older build, published as present tense, which is how a verification register quietly becomes a historical document. The fix is structural, not editorial: those rows now write named count tokens — n_spec_rows, n_verified_rows, n_blank_rows, n_suggestions, n_sg_last, n_questions, n_articles, n_topics, n_products, n_sources, n_banned, n_banned_distinct, n_tests, n_guards, n_stale_briefings — each wrapped in braces in the data and substituted by req_interp() from the same objects the pages render from. An unknown token raises at build time, and check_requirement_tokens() fails the build if a brace-delimited token survives into any generated page, so a row cannot ship half-interpolated. Both directions were proven: an injected bad token and an injected unresolved token each exited non-zero. The same class is why this register&#x27;s sibling in the s2 annex carries F60, and why the sitemap row&#x27;s number was removed rather than retyped: a count is only true at the moment it is written — including the counts typed into this register&#x27;s own log lines, one of which this pass had to correct in its own commit message two minutes after writing it (7,815 links became 7,826 when the guard rows were added). Process note for the next session: this pass&#x27;s rows were renumbered twice in one day to avoid same-day id collisions — first off F63 (arena/a746a9f2), then off F64-F67 (arena/b23f12f0, whose F67 is the duplicate-ID guard that has now caught both collisions). Renumbering is cheap while a PR is open and expensive after it merges.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/requirements.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/requirements.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f74"><td class="num"><strong>F74</strong></td><td>An article page existed on disk and on the live site that no data file or generator referenced: articles/sync-finish-two-buttons.html. Found independently twice on 2026-11-16; repaired the same day.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: articles/sync-finish-two-buttons.html vs data/articles.json (no matching slug); file removed 2026-11-16</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by session arena/19d92a81 during the line-by-line repo review, after the file arrived with a parallel pass&#x27;s merge. data/articles.json carries no &#x27;sync-finish-two-buttons&#x27; slug and no build step generates the page, so nothing will ever refresh or retire it - yet it is served publicly next to the generated articles and looks exactly like them, disclosure bar included, so a reader cannot tell it is unmanaged. A grep of data/ and build.py finds zero references. It is the same defect class as F57 (orphan registers), one layer up: a page outside the data-driven set. Independent discovery the same day by arena/5ac82b1d during the final integration pass: articles/ held 20 HTML files against 19 registered slugs; the orphan carried the same TP-09 subject as the registered article sync-finish-explained.html and appeared in no sitemap entry, no articles index and no internal link - an orphan a crawler could still reach by direct URL, publishing a second unregistered answer to the same question. Its own passing session&#x27;s completion record (X-121929a5) states this draft was discarded as a duplicate of round 11&#x27;s TP-09 article; the register entry was dropped but the HTML file survived the merge, and no gate compared the articles/ directory against the page set the build emits (verify.py checks links from files that exist, so it cannot see a file nothing links to). DECISION EXECUTED 2026-11-16 by arena/5ac82b1d: deleted rather than registered - the canonical TP-09 article (articles/sync-finish-explained.html, data/topics.json published_as) already covers the subject, so registering the duplicate would publish the same question twice. check_no_orphan_pages() now fails the build if any .html file in articles/, products/, guides/, categories/ or the repo root is not generated by this build, and the guard was proven to fire on an injected file before it was committed.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/articles.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/articles.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/topics.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/topics.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/reports/verification-2026-11-16-19d92a81.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">reports/verification-2026-11-16-19d92a81.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f76"><td class="num"><strong>F76</strong></td><td>The two links submitted on 2026-11-17 resolve to cooking thermometers, not air fryers: the submitted product set spans two categories while the site publishes one.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: PI-04 (B0c8moW59) and PI-05 (B0f3GKalj)</div></td><td class="small">Found by this pass while re-resolving all five submitted links on 2026-11-17 (observation_2026_11_17 on each record in data/product-intent-confirmations.json). The candidate destinations are a ThermoMaven instant-read thermometer and a CHEF iQ Sense Gen3 wireless thermometer, each filed by Amazon under Kitchen Utensils &amp; Gadgets &gt; Thermometers &amp; Timers &gt; Meat, whereas the first three links land under Small Appliances &gt; Fryers &gt; Air Fryers. Two consequences, and this pass acted on neither. (1) Scope: every product page, comparison row, buying guide and article on this site is about air fryers, and the masthead reads &#x27;Air-fryer field guide&#x27;. Adding two thermometer pages is a publishing decision (SG-36), not a data-entry task — it changes what the site is, and a second category built from two unconfirmed candidate destinations is how one thin affiliate footprint becomes two. (2) Money: the commission-category question was argued from the Air Fryers breadcrumb (Q-11, SG-10), and a thermometer listing sits in a different browse path, so the Kitchen 4.50% working rate cannot be extended to it by analogy. What this pass did instead: recorded both links with their observation fields filled and their confirmation fields blank, cross-read the two brands&#x27; own pages so the owner has something to compare intent against, and generated links.html so the divergence is on a page rather than buried in JSON. Decision needed: confirm which product each of the five links was made for, then choose path A (air fryers only, the thermometers stay in the register) or path B (a second category, sourced from brand documents before any page is written).</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefiq.com/products/iq-sense" title="CHEF iQ (brand) — CHEF iQ iQ Sense wireless meat thermometer — official product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S36</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.thermomaven.com/" title="ThermoMaven (brand) — ThermoMaven official site — instant-read and wireless thermometer line-up" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S37</a> <a class="src" href="https://link.amazon/B0c8moW59" title="Amazon.com — link.amazon short links supplied in the 2026-11-17 brief (B0c8moW59, B0f3GKalj)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S38</a> <a class="src" href="https://kdp.amazon.com/en_US/help/topic/G200652190" title="Amazon.com Kindle Direct Publishing (help centre) — Linking from Your Website (KDP Help G200652190)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S23</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/product-intent-confirmations.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/product-intent-confirmations.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/links.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">links.html ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/reports/verification-2026-11-17-58cbaaa2.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">reports/verification-2026-11-17-58cbaaa2.md ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f77"><td class="num"><strong>F77</strong></td><td>Neither newly submitted link&#x27;s candidate product can be resolved to a manufacturer model number from the brand&#x27;s own catalogue.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: PI-04 (ThermoMaven), PI-05 (CHEF iQ Sense)</div></td><td class="small">Recorded 2026-11-17 while checking whether the observed destinations could be promoted to product pages the way the three air fryers were. For the air fryers the mapping was closable: each listing&#x27;s own Product information block states RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W and RJ38-SQSS-8T, which are the brand documents&#x27; strings (F68). For PI-04 no such match exists: the observed listing title reads &#x27;1 Sec Professional&#x27; while the brand&#x27;s official site names its instant-read models F1 Pro and F1 Turbo and describes the F1 Turbo with a half-second read (S37), so the brand page neither confirms nor denies which model the listing is. PI-05 is closer — the brand&#x27;s iQ Sense page states model CQ60-3C-Z3-E, five sensors and a 1,000 °F ambient rating, consistent with the &#x27;Gen3&#x27; in the observed title (S36) — but &#x27;consistent with&#x27; is an inference about a listing reached through a fallback redirect, and this project does not publish inferences about the destination it is paid on. No spec row, comparison cell or article was created from either candidate. Decision needed: the account holder states which product each link was created to point at; only then does the F68 route (read the listing&#x27;s model line) become meaningful.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://chefiq.com/products/iq-sense" title="CHEF iQ (brand) — CHEF iQ iQ Sense wireless meat thermometer — official product page" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S36</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.thermomaven.com/" title="ThermoMaven (brand) — ThermoMaven official site — instant-read and wireless thermometer line-up" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S37</a> <a class="src" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CDY277D3" title="Amazon.com (product listings) — Amazon detail page for ASIN B0CDY277D3 (Chefman 6 Qt Dual Basket)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S16</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/product-intent-confirmations.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/product-intent-confirmations.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/links.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">links.html ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f78"><td class="num"><strong>F78</strong></td><td>The tracking ID selected by the account holder matches the shape Amazon documents, and cannot be confirmed to exist on the account — so no published link carries it.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: shopnow047c-20 — all five submitted links</div></td><td class="small">The 2026-11-17 brief supplied &#x27;Selected Tracking ID: shopnow047c-20&#x27;, which answers half of Q-04 (which ID is intended) and none of the other half (whether it exists, and whether it is attached to the links). Checked against the only official page that describes what a tracking ID is, read verbatim today (S6): the help node&#x27;s worked example is &#x27;storeid-20&#x27;, with sub-tag IDs of the form &#x27;storeid-1-20&#x27;; the supplied value matches that shape. The node states no validation rule and publishes no list of IDs per account, so a shape match is not an existence check — and existence is the only thing that earns. The build therefore keeps every outbound link exactly as supplied: a tag that does not exist on the account cannot attribute a sale, and a destination rewritten by the publisher would be worse than an ambiguous one, because it would look settled. The gate is now data rather than convention — selected_tracking_id.tagging_gate.state in data/product-intent-confirmations.json — enforced by check_special_link_gate(), which fails the build if a tagged anchor appears while the gate is closed, or if the gate is opened without a confirmation date and source. Read the same day, two further official nodes change how this flag should be argued, and the change is recorded here rather than quietly edited out of the earlier passes: Amazon states that &#x27;it is not necessary for your unique ID to appear in order for us to track sales made after the initial click through&#x27;, because the session is tagged on its side (S39) — so the missing tag= parameter on a landing URL is not evidence that a link failed to attribute, and the site&#x27;s earlier reliance on that absence was an over-reading of a null observation. What remains unprovable from outside the account is the positive: that the ID is attached and that it is listed. That is a report question, not a URL question (S40 names the Tracking ID Summary Report, and a limit of 100 tracking IDs per account, which is what makes per-channel sub-tags cheap enough to recommend). Decision needed: five minutes at Associates Central &gt; Manage your Tracking ID, then either open the gate or write back that the ID is not listed.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GK5TZZ4AWML2QSLA" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — What are tracking IDs, and how does the new site help Associates use them?" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S6</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GF4RJEQNXSMR7SP3" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Why doesn&#x27;t my tracking ID appear in your URL after a visitor clicks through to Amazon from my Web site?" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S39</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GJDYPQZK6E37RLPU" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — How do I request multiple tracking IDs?" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S40</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/product-intent-confirmations.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/product-intent-confirmations.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/links.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">links.html ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f79"><td class="num"><strong>F79</strong></td><td>The shipped click counter counts one device, not an audience: with no collector deployed its numbers cannot be aggregated, and the campaign ledger stays empty by design.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: measurement.html, data/measurement.json, assets/app.js</div></td><td class="small">The 2026-11-17 brief asks for a privacy-preserving outbound-click counter, a self-hosted analytics endpoint, five defined events, and an empty but well-defined campaign ledger — and it puts that ahead of writing anything else. The pass that ran the same day read the same instruction and declined to ship a counter at all, on the ground that a browser counter storing counts on each visitor&#x27;s device &#x27;would not give the publisher usable aggregate analytics&#x27; and that inventing an endpoint would be misleading (arena/02b4e9c7&#x27;s completion record). Both readings are partly right, and the reconciliation is published rather than hidden. What shipped: the five events defined in a machine-readable register; a collector that stores counts in the reader&#x27;s own browser and sends nothing; and a ledger whose eight columns each carry the rule for what may fill them. What did not ship and cannot: the endpoint — GitHub Pages serves static files, there is no server here to accept a POST, and a collector URL written into code with nobody to run it is a promise, not a feature. So the counter is a per-device diagnostic and is labelled that way on every surface that shows a number: the instrument, wired, waiting for the day an endpoint exists. The option that needs no infrastructure at all is Amazon&#x27;s own — extra tracking IDs per channel, which report clicks and orders separately with no script on this site (the sub-tag plan on the measurement page, sourced to S6). Decision needed: deploy a first-party collector and rewrite the privacy statement to match it; or adopt sub-tags and leave the counter as a diagnostic; or remove the counter. All three are one edit, and SG-38 lays them out.</td><td><a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Associates Program Policies (Commission Income Statement, Participation Requirements, Products Statement, Mobile Application Policy, Trademark Guidelines, IP License)" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S2</a> <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GK5TZZ4AWML2QSLA" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — What are tracking IDs, and how does the new site help Associates use them?" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S6</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/measurement.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/measurement.json ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/measurement.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">measurement.html ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/assets/app.js" target="_blank" rel="noopener">assets/app.js ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f80"><td class="num"><strong>F80</strong></td><td>Ten passes typed &#x27;the three supplied links&#x27; into present-tense prose, and the sentence went false the day a fourth and fifth link arrived.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: build.py (footer, landing note), data/decision.json, EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY.md</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-17 in the line-by-line review, minutes after the brief itself demonstrated the defect by supplying two more links. The number of submitted links is a fact that changes when the owner acts, and it had been typed into sentences in several files rather than read from the register — so the footer and the landing page both still described a submission of three while the brief described five. Same defect class as F73 (counts typed into prose) and F14 (a headline that outlived its premise), one step up: those passes were checking numbers against data; nobody was checking the *narrative* against data, and every number inside each of those sentences was individually fine. Repaired where the sentence is present tense: the footer, the landing-page link-status note and the new register page take their counts from len(records) in data/product-intent-confirmations.json, and check_submitted_link_count() fails the build if a generated page asserts a different number in its own words. Not repaired, deliberately: the dated rows that record what an earlier pass observed while three links existed (F14, F21, F28, F32 and the historical prose in data/decision.json) are statements about a past state, and rewriting them would destroy the audit trail that makes them evidence. Process note for the next session: when the owner submits another link, add the record — never the prose.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/product-intent-confirmations.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/product-intent-confirmations.json ↗</a></td></tr><tr id="f75"><td class="num"><strong>F75</strong></td><td>The 404 page was published with relative links, but GitHub Pages serves it at any missing path — so every link on it broke under nested URLs.<div class="tiny muted">Scope: 404.html (all href/src values)</div></td><td class="small">Found 2026-11-16 by arena/5ac82b1d while reading back the deleted orphan article&#x27;s URL on the live site: /articles/sync-finish-two-buttons.html correctly served the 404 page, but its nav, card and stylesheet links resolved against /articles/ (e.g. products/chefman-6qt-dual-basket.html became /articles/products/...), so the error page it served was itself unstyled and full of dead links — the one page whose whole job is to recover a lost reader. Root-relative links (/Zone1/...) were rejected as a fix because verify.py and scripts/check_links.py resolve internal hrefs against the file on disk, where they do not exist. Fixed instead by rewriting every relative href/src on 404.html to its absolute SITE_URL form in layout(), leaving fragment-only links (#anchor) alone since they address the displayed document. check_seo_metadata() now fails the build if any non-absolute, non-fragment URL survives on the 404 page. Note for later: this defect can only be observed by fetching a missing nested URL from the deployed site — no local gate sees the serving path.</td><td><a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/build.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">build.py ↗</a> <a href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/404.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">404.html ↗</a></td></tr></tbody>
