+<p class="small"><strong>Why the gate exists.</strong> While the ID is unconfirmed, every outbound product link on this site stays exactly as supplied and carries no tag parameter. A tag that does not exist on the account cannot earn, and a link rewritten to a destination nobody confirmed would be a commercial link invented by the publisher. The gate opens by data, not by editing a template.</p>
+<div class="callout note">
+  <strong>A correction to how this register argued its own case, dated 2026-11-17.</strong>
+  Earlier passes treated the absence of a <code>tag=</code> parameter on a landing URL as part of the reason to keep the
+  gate closed. Amazon's own help node says the opposite about what a URL can show: “it is not necessary for your unique
+  ID to appear in order for us to track sales made after the initial click through,” because the shopping session is
+  tagged on Amazon's side <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GF4RJEQNXSMR7SP3" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — Why doesn&#x27;t my tracking ID appear in your URL after a visitor clicks through to Amazon from my Web site?" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S39</a>. So a clean landing URL is a null observation, not a negative one — it
+  neither confirms nor refutes attribution, and this page does not use it that way. What the register can and does say is
+  that the positive is unprovable from outside the account: whether <code>shopnow047c-20</code> is listed, and whether a click
+  shows up in its summary report <a class="src" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GJDYPQZK6E37RLPU" title="Amazon.com Associates Central — How do I request multiple tracking IDs?" rel="noopener" target="_blank">S40</a>. That report row is the confirmation, and the gate stays shut until
+  somebody with the login reads it. The same node records a limit of 100 tracking IDs per account, which is why the
+  per-channel sub-tag plan on the <a href="measurement.html">measurement page</a> is the cheap option rather than a
+  exotic one.
+</div>
+<p class="small"><strong>What opening it takes</strong> — four edits in order, and no template change:</p>
+<ol class="tight small"><li>Set intended_product.status to &#x27;confirmed&#x27; on the record whose listing the owner meant to promote, with official_listing_url and confirmation_source filled.</li><li>Set selected_tracking_id.associates_central_confirmation_date to the date the ID was read on the Manage your Tracking ID screen, and add the screen&#x27;s own URL as source.</li><li>Set tagging_gate.state to &#x27;open&#x27; and rebuild. build.py then emits canonical /dp/&lt;confirmed ASIN&gt;?tag=&lt;value&gt; links for confirmed records only.</li><li>The build fails if the gate is open with no confirmation evidence (check_special_link_gate), and if any tagged link appears for a record whose identity is unconfirmed.</li></ol>
