-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/questions.html#faq","url":"https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/questions.html","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Which exact manufacturer model number does each of the three supplied Amazon links point to?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: All three link.amazon short links resolve to the same three ASINs on every recorded fetch: B0CDY277D3, B09B7SB46R and B08DKYBTPH (7 observation rounds, 2026-11-13 to 2026-11-15). The three destination pages carry the listing titles recorded in data/products.json, except the Emeril title, which changed between 2026-11-14 and 2026-11-15 (flag F38). The bare 9-character codes in the short links (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV) are not ASINs and return 'Page Not Found' when used as /dp/ identifiers. Re-tested 2026-11-15. What is missing: Nothing for the model step. For the link attribution: a statement from the account holder, or the Associates Central link builder showing which ASIN each short link was created for. Neither is reachable from this repository. How to find it out: Done 2026-11-16 by session ba29c641: the Product information block was read off all three listing pages and published on the product pages and in reports/asin-model-mapping-2026-11-16.md. The residual part - link-to-ASIN intent - can only be settled by whoever created the short links."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is the Chefman 8 Qt listing the RJ38-SQSS-8T the site maps it to, or the RJ38-8T-BLACK that documents the 450 F Hi-Fry?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The Amazon listing title contains '450 F Hi-Fry Option' and 'Bake, Dehydrate, Frozen'. The chefman.com page for RJ38-SQSS-8T states a maximum temperature of 400 F. Chefman publishes a separate RJ38-8T-BLACK that documents 450 F Hi-Fry and exactly those four functions (flag F33). What is missing: A statement from Chefman or the seller about which SKU sits behind ASIN B08DKYBTPH, or the model plate on a unit bought against it. The site will not pick between its own documents without one. How to find it out: Done 2026-11-16 by session arena/ba29c641 (Part Number and Max Temperature rows read from the listing's Product information block; see reports/asin-model-mapping-2026-11-16.md). What a script cannot do: open the box."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is the Amazon Associates account approved, in review, or closed - and when did the current review window start?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The Standard Commission Income Statement can be read without signing in, so the rate table is public. The site is built as though the account is live, but nothing in the repository records its status. What is missing: The account status and the date the window began. Both are inside Associates Central. How to find it out: Associates Central home page shows status directly. Record the status and, if in review, the date the application was submitted."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which Associates tag is inside the three supplied short links, and does it belong to this account?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The links are link.amazon short links, so the redirect target is observable but the embedded tag is not. The redirect target carries Amazon's own parameters (lv=shuf, btn_ref=srctok-..., plpRedirect=mhFallback) but no tag= parameter is visible in the landing URL. What is missing: The full tagged URL. It is inside the short link and is only visible from the account that created it, or by reading the click report. How to find it out: Option 1: open each link while signed in to Associates Central and confirm the click appears against your tracking ID. Option 2: rebuild each link as a full amazon.com URL with your tag and use that instead."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you have, or can you get, Creators API access?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The rate table and all program documents are readable without signing in. The site currently displays no Amazon price, rating or review count on any page, and that is correct rather than an omission. What is missing: Whether the account qualifies for Creators API, and whether any credentials exist. How to find it out: Associates Central > Tools > Creators API (or PA API). Access requires qualifying sales. Record approved / not approved."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the product weight of the Emeril 26 QT French Door, and is the weight published for the two Chefman models the product weight or the packaged weight?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Exterior dimensions are published for all three products from official sources: the Chefman 6 Qt from its product page (S10), the Chefman 8 Qt from its product page (S12), and the Emeril 26 QT from its product page (S14). Warranty terms are published for all three: 1-year Chefman assurance (S10), limited 1-year from the owner's manual (S13), and 1-year warranty (S14). Product weight is published for both Chefman models from their specification blocks: 13.3 lb / 6.03 kg for the 6 Qt (S10) and 12.8 lb / 5.8 kg for the 8 Qt (S12). The same brand's own commerce endpoint, read on 2026-11-15, reports 14.51 lb / 6,582 g for RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W (S32) and 13.4 lb / 6,078 g for RJ38-SQSS-8T (S33) - both higher, and neither page says which figure is the product and which is the package (flag F51). NARROWED 2026-11-15 (arena/63a2c638, flag F52): this row previously asked for dimensions, weight and warranty for all three products and was filed as 'not_stated'. Eight of those nine cells were already published from official sources in data/products.json. Only one cell is genuinely open. NEW EVIDENCE 2026-11-16 (arena/b23f12f0, round 11): the brand storefront's own machine-readable product record (S35) reports a variant weight of 27.04 lb / 12,265 g for SKU 4-00675-02A-VN. This is recorded as a candidate, not published as the Emeril's product weight, because the endpoint does not say whether its weight field is the product or the packaged weight - the same ambiguity flag F51 records for the two Chefman models, whose specification-block weights (13.3 lb and 12.8 lb) are both lower than their own endpoint weights (14.51 lb and 13.4 lb). Converting a shipping figure into a product specification is exactly the inference this project refuses to make. What is missing: Two things, and only two. (1) The Emeril 26 QT product weight - neither the brand product page (S14) nor the owner's manual (S15) states one. (2) A statement from Chefman, on either page, saying whether its published weight is the product or the packaged weight. How to find it out: For the Emeril: read the 'Product information' or specification table on the brand page and the specifications page of the owner's manual; if neither states a weight, leave the cell blank and say so rather than converting a shipping weight. For the Chefman labels: quote the sentence next to the weight in each Specifications block, and quote the endpoint field, then decide which one the site publishes - or publish both with their labels."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Should this be one public site, and should it live on a domain you own?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: GitHub Pages is configured to build from main at the repository root, legacy build type, HTTPS enforced. Eight additional directories carry their own index.html and are served under the same origin. The repository already generates robots.txt and a sitemap.xml for the root build only (23 URLs). What is missing: Whether the annexes are meant to be public, and whether a custom domain is acceptable. How to find it out: A decision, not a lookup: keep the annexes public as an audit trail, or publish only the root and keep the rest in the repository. Record the answer here."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What may this site measure, and whose consent does that require?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The site is fully static and runs no client-side tracking of any kind. Amazon reports clicks and orders per tracking ID without any on-site script. Section 2(b) permits additional sub-tag IDs to separate traffic sources. What is missing: Whether third-party analytics are wanted at all, and if so which lawful basis applies for EU visitors. How to find it out: Decide between Associates sub-tags alone (no disclosure change) and a cookieless analytics script (small disclosure update). The choice is in the Suggestions page, SG-08."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who is the named author, and what may the site honestly claim about how content is produced?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: No page on the site names an author or describes the production method. The footer states no hands-on testing is claimed anywhere on the site. 16 CFR 255.5 requires disclosure of material connections that a significant minority of the audience would not expect. What is missing: The name to publish, whether an editorial human reviews each page before publication, and whether AI assistance will be disclosed. How to find it out: A decision. If nothing else, publishing the method is enough: 'every number is quoted from a manufacturer document or the CFR, drafted with AI assistance and checked against the source'. That sentence is true today and is not currently on the site."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which search intent does each existing article own, and who is allowed to write the next one?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Every published article is source-linked and none claims hands-on testing (live count on the articles index). One earlier pass already deleted three orphaned drafts for exactly this reason and preserved their text in session-7111e2b6/articles-superseded.json. What is missing: A written assignment of one primary intent per article, and a rule that a new article may not target an intent already claimed. How to find it out: Thirty minutes with the article index. Assign each one intent, publish the map, and make a duplicate a build failure."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which category has Amazon actually assigned to each ASIN, and therefore which commission rate applies?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The published Table 1 lists Physical Books, Kitchen and Automotive at 4.50%, and All Other Categories at 4.00%. Amazon does not publish an ASIN-to-category mapping, which is why the site shows both candidate rows. All three listings display the same breadcrumb: Home & Kitchen > Kitchen & Dining > Small Appliances > Fryers > Air Fryers. The same breadcrumb does NOT settle this question. The Commission Income Statement's categories (S3) are commission categories defined by the Associates programme; browse nodes are a shopper-navigation taxonomy; Amazon publishes no mapping between them. The breadcrumb begins 'Home & Kitchen' while S3 carries both 'Kitchen' at 4.50% and 'Home' at 3.00%, so it cannot even indicate which of the two it resembles. See flag F53. RESOLVED 2026-11-16 (arena/b23f12f0, round 11, flag F65): the Emeril browse node is no longer unknown. ASIN B09B7SB46R was fetched successfully on 2026-11-16 and rendered the breadcrumb 'Home & Kitchen > Kitchen & Dining > Small Appliances > Fryers > Air Fryers' (S28) - identical to the two Chefman paths - and the same breadcrumb was reached independently through the supplied short link in the same session (S17). The 2026-11-15 reading that called the block 'per-ASIN' was wrong: on 2026-11-16 the same ASIN fetched cleanly on the first attempt while one of the two ASINs that had worked the previous day was served the checkpoint on its first attempt and returned the listing only on a retry. The checkpoint is intermittent per request, so a failed fetch is not evidence about the ASIN. NEW 2026-11-15 (arena/63a2c638): the two Chefman listings each render an Amazon browse-node breadcrumb reading 'Home & Kitchen > Kitchen & Dining > Small Appliances > Fryers > Air Fryers' (S16 for B0CDY277D3, S29 for B08DKYBTPH). That breadcrumb is now published as a sourced fact on each product page. UPDATED 2026-11-16 (arena/b23f12f0): all three listings now have a confirmed browse node, and all three are the same node. That closes the last blank cell in the commission question's evidence, but it does not close the question - see the entry above on why a browse path is not a commission category. Re-read again the same day by arena/ba29c641 fetching the /dp/ URL directly: its Best Sellers Rank line reads '#214 in Kitchen & Dining' and '#6 in Air Fryers' (node 17659096011), agreeing with both earlier reads. Three independent routes, one classification — and still no commission category. What is missing: Confirmation of the category Amazon applies at commission time. The breadcrumb is strong evidence; the Earnings report is proof. How to find it out: The rate is not discoverable from any public page - it is only observable after the fact, in the Associates reports, where each qualifying purchase shows the rate actually applied. Read the category column on the first few real orders and the question answers itself. Publishing a rate before then means publishing a guess about how Amazon classified the product."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is there any published statement on this site that a live official source contradicts today?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: This pass fetched all three supplied links, the Commission Income Statement, the Program Policies page, 16 CFR 255.5, 16 CFR Part 465 and both manufacturers' product pages on 2026-11-15. One published value was found stale and is corrected in this pass: the Emeril listing title, which now reads '24 One-Touch Preset Options' where the stored value read '24 cooking functions' (flag F38). One citation defect was found and corrected: the source record S16 is registered as the B0CDY277D3 URL but was cited for all three listing titles, so two of the three citations pointed at a different product's page (flag F39). One register-integrity defect was found and corrected: two different rows were both numbered F36 (flag F40). What is missing: A standing re-read schedule. Review counts and listing titles change without notice, and no page on the site currently records when a value was last read except the re-verification ledger. How to find it out: Re-read every value on a fixed cadence and publish the date next to each volatile figure. The re-verification ledger is the right home for this; it needs a schedule rather than a one-off pass."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What exactly does the current application-review process require, in its own words?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The page existed at the recorded URL and was quoted by an earlier pass with a logged fetch. This pass attempted the page and did not get content back, so it is flagged rather than repeated as verified. What is missing: A current read of the review-process page, which requires loading it in a browser. How to find it out: Open the source link and read the qualifying-criteria section. Record what it says today, with the date."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the actual conversion rate for these three products, and what would one sale be worth?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Manufacturer list prices are $119.99, $199.99 and $119.99 USD, each sourced to the brand page. At the Kitchen rate of 4.50%, a sale at list price is worth roughly $5.40, $9.00 and $5.40 respectively before returns. No sales data exists in this repository. What is missing: Real click and order counts. These live in Associates Central and cannot be obtained by research. How to find it out: Associates Central > Reports. Until there is history, label any projection as a scenario with its assumed conversion rate stated."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What commission rate does Amazon's published table give for kitchen appliances, and does that rate apply to these three listings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The published Table 1 lists Physical Books, Kitchen and Automotive at 4.50%, and All Other Categories at 4.00%. Amazon does not publish an ASIN-to-category mapping, which is why the site shows both candidate rows. All three listings display the same breadcrumb: Home & Kitchen > Kitchen & Dining > Small Appliances > Fryers > Air Fryers. Re-read 2026-11-15: the breadcrumb was read directly on all three ASINs - B0CDY277D3, B09B7SB46R and B08DKYBTPH - not only on the Emeril listing, which was the limit of the earlier observation recorded in the annex register's F-06. What is missing: Confirmation of the category Amazon applies at commission time. No public source states it for an ASIN; the Earnings report names it only on a shipped line. How to find it out: Associates Central > Reports > Earnings shows the category applied to each shipped item. Before any sale exists, the breadcrumb is the best available evidence and is recorded as evidence rather than as confirmation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"May this site display Amazon prices, star ratings or review counts?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Program Policies 2(b), read verbatim 2026-11-15: \"Product prices and availability may vary from time to time. ... your Site may only show prices and availability if: (a) we serve the link in which that price and availability data are displayed, or (b) you obtain Product pricing and availability data via Creators API or PA API and you comply with the requirements regarding use of Creators API or PA API in the License.\" Program Policies 6(t), read verbatim 2026-11-15: \"You will not display or otherwise use any of our customer reviews or star ratings, in part or in whole, on your Site unless you have obtained a link to that customer review or star rating through Creators API or PA API and you comply with the requirements for Creators API and PA API described in the License.\" The build has an executable guard for this: a build check refuses to emit any price tracking or price alerting element, and the site carries no price or rating values at all. What is missing: Nothing about the rule itself - it is a settled reading of the current policy text. What is missing is whether the account will ever hold the API access that would unlock the display. How to find it out: Not applicable to the rule. If the account later obtains Creators API or PA API access, sections 2(b) and 6(t) must be re-read against that API's own licence terms before any value is displayed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is this site's product coverage actually based on?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Every populated specification row in data/products.json names at least one official source, and build.py fails the build if a row carries a value with no source. All three owner's manuals were read end to end on 2026-11-15 and every value attributed to them was found in them. The site's own footer, every article header and the three product pages each state that nothing was tested. What is missing: Nothing about the coverage. What the site cannot answer is how any of these machines actually cooks, because nobody here has used one - and that gap is the honest limit of every comparison on the site. How to find it out: Only by buying and testing each unit and recording the test. The conflicts that a reader is most likely to hit - which Chefman 8-quart model the listing actually is, what the Emeril's preset count really says - can only be settled by reading the model plate on the unit, or by the owner's own test."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can the brief's AI agent read and analyse Amazon's own product data?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: IP License section 1, read verbatim 2026-11-15: \"This license does not include any downloading, copying or other use of Program Content for the benefit of any third party, or any use of data mining, robots, or similar data gathering and extraction tools.\" IP License section 1, final paragraph, read verbatim 2026-11-15: \"will not allow any third party to, use Program Content to, directly or indirectly, develop or improve large language or multimodal models, machine learning models or related technology.\" IP License section 2(e), read verbatim 2026-11-15: \"You will not, without our express prior written approval, access or use Creators API, PA API or Data Feeds for the purpose of aggregating, analyzing, extracting, or repurposing any Product Advertising Content or in connection with any software or other application intended for use by persons or entities that offer products on an Amazon Site, or in the direct training or fine-tuning of a machine learning and/or foundational model.\" The repository already runs its automation on first-party and manufacturer data only; flag F8 records the same constraint as a platform guardrail. What is missing: Nothing in the rule. What is missing is Amazon's prior written approval, which section 2(e) names as the only route to the restricted use and which does not exist today. How to find it out: Only Amazon can grant it. Until approval exists in writing, the constraint stands and the platform must run on manufacturer and first-party data."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How often should this site's published values be re-read?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Every rating figure the site publishes already carries its read date, and flag F19 records why. Re-read 2026-11-16: the Chefman 6 Qt counter read 1,307 (from 1,305) and the 8 Qt read 717 (from 716), while both averages held at 4.6. The two rows the site publishes were refreshed to this reading. The 2026-11-15 pass re-read every manufacturer page and every owner's manual and found no published value to be wrong. Application Review Process, re-read live 2026-11-16: the 180-day / three-sale rule, the ~10-post rule, the 60-day content-recency rule and the ~500-follower social threshold are all confirmed verbatim. That closes the 'not re-read' part of flag F56. The 2026-11-16 pass re-read the Standard Commission Income Statement, the Program Policies page, 16 CFR Part 255, both Chefman product pages, the Emeril product page and all three supplied short links, and found no published value wrong. What is missing: The owner's chosen refresh interval for each class of value - Amazon states the review-side recency window, not a maintenance schedule for a documentation site. Nothing on the site has gone stale: the most recent read of every published value is 2026-11-16. How to find it out: Set an interval per value class and let the build report anything past it - which is SG-18. The Application Review page no longer needs re-reading for its own sake, only when Amazon changes it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which country and marketplace is this site actually promoting, and who states it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: All three supplied link.amazon short links resolve to amazon.com, the US marketplace, and all three listings sit under the same US browse-node path. Participation Requirements 1, read verbatim 2026-11-15: \"If you are a non-US person participating in the Associates Program, you agree that unless you have otherwise notified us in your tax information you will perform all services under the Agreement outside the United States.\" The commission table the site publishes is the US Standard Commission Income Statement. What is missing: The operator's actual country of residence, stated once by the operator, and the marketplace the site is intended to promote. The two statements already in the repository are agent-session observations, not owner statements. How to find it out: The owner states their country once, one register row cites it, and the other wording is corrected to match. The Associates application itself, which asks for tax information and a bank account, is the authority on whether a non-US applicant is accepted."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"If the research agent is automated, what is it allowed to touch, and what must it call itself?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: IP License §4, read verbatim 2026-11-16: \"The terms in this section (\"Agent Terms\") apply if you use, allow, enable, or cause the deployment of an Agent to access, use, or interact with the Program Content. For the purposes of these Agent Terms, \"Agent\" means any software or service that takes autonomous or semi-autonomous action on behalf of, or at the instruction of, any person or entity.\" Same section, verbatim: \"In all HTTP/HTTPS requests, identify that the request is from an Agent and disclose the name of the Agent by including the following in the request's user agent string: \"Agent/[agent name]\" (e.g., Agent/AmazonAgent)\"; \"Not conceal or obfuscate that any access, use, or interactions are from an Agent…\"; \"Respond truthfully to any question or prompt seeking to determine if interactions are coming from a human or a computer.\" Same section, verbatim: \"At our sole discretion, we may limit, including by technical measures, whether and how any Agent accesses, uses, and interacts with Program Content.\" Participation Requirements §5, read verbatim 2026-11-16: \"You will not, without express prior written approval, use, or allow any third party to use, any Special Links or Program Content to develop machine learning models or related technology.\" IP License §1, read verbatim 2026-11-16: \"We will not allow any third party to, use Program Content to, directly or indirectly, develop or improve large language or multimodal models, machine learning models or related technology.\" The published site consumes no Program Content at all, so no Agent Term is triggered by anything it renders today. The clauses matter for the proposed research agent, not for the pages. What is missing: Two things, and neither can be settled from a public page. First, whether a public Amazon product page fetched by an automated tool counts as \"Program Content\" — the IP License defines Program Content around Creators API, PA API and Data Feeds, and the policies page does not say whether an ordinary listing page is inside that definition. Second, the agent's declared name. A third point is only partly legible: IP License §4(a) refers to \"the requirements in section 3 of these Agent Terms\", and section 3 of the Agent Terms was not readable in the render used for this pass (flag F62), so the full list of identification requirements is not yet on the record. How to find it out: Read IP License §4 in a browser, in full, including the section 3 cross-reference. Then ask Amazon one question in writing — through Associates Central support — and keep the answer: \"Does fetching a public amazon.com product detail page with an automated tool count as accessing Program Content under IP License §4, and is a User-Agent of Agent/zone1-research acceptable?\" Until Amazon answers, the conservative design is the one in SG-22: identify as an agent, never defeat a bot check, and keep the research stage off Program Content entirely."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which images may this site publish, and whose permission covers them?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Amazon's Operating Agreement licenses Program Content (including images) to Associates under the Agreement's rules. The FTC requires clear disclosure of material connections, which applies to imagery as well as text. What is missing: A recorded decision on which image source the site uses, and the rights statement covering each image. How to find it out: Read Amazon's Program Content/IP provisions in the Operating Agreement and Program Policies, then decide between owner photos, licensed imagery, Program Content images, or text-and-diagrams only."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who decides the publishing calendar — including seasonal pieces such as the gift guide — and what is each piece's deadline?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The Standard Commission Income Statement, read live 2026-11-16, pays 4.50% on Kitchen and 4.00% on All Other Categories. The topic register now holds three seasonal rows: TP-15 (gift guide, published 2026-11-16), TP-16 (hosting capacity) and TP-17 (deal-season checks), both proposed. The application review requires site content to be recent — 'generally within the last 60 days' — and looks for about 10 posts. Nothing in the repository records any owner statement about seasonal content, deadlines or sign-off. What is missing: A one-line owner decision: which seasonal window(s) to target, how many pieces per window, who approves a publish, and the date each piece must be ready by. 'No seasonal content' is also a usable answer — it would retire TP-16 and TP-17 instead of leaving them pending. How to find it out: The owner states the calendar once — in COORDINATION.md, in a dated reply, or in the handover sheet — and the editorial queue's blocked_by rows are cleared against that statement. The search window itself is not in dispute; only the owner's appetite and sign-off are missing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who signs off on the suggestion queue, and on what cadence?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Every row on the suggestions page is explicitly unimplemented and carries options, a recommendation, effort and risk. Several earlier flags were dispositioned as 'the owner's call' (for example F59), which is this same gap surfacing elsewhere. Decision path P5 (session arena/19d92a81) groups the reader-facing polish suggestions so one 'yes' covers eight rows. What is missing: A named decider, a review cadence, and what 'approved' means - approved to build, or approved to test? How to find it out: One message from the owner: a cadence (for example '30 minutes monthly, I mark each row approve/reject/defer') and what approval means. The page can render an approval-state column as soon as the data carries it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is 'Zone1 Kitchen' the final brand name for this site?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The root site's titles and organization schema use 'Zone1 Kitchen' with the tagline 'Air-fryer decisions, backed by sources'. The /s2 annex publishes under 'Zone1 - Amazon Associates platform'. The README titles the project 'Zone1 - verified Amazon affiliate product intelligence'. What is missing: The owner's decision on the one name reader-facing surfaces should carry. How to find it out: The owner states the name in one line; a single commit then aligns titles, og tags, the annex header and the README. SG-28 carries the implementation options."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which API will the automation platform call, now that PA-API 5 has a deprecation notice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Amazon publishes a PA-API 5 Deprecation Notice and a Product Advertising API registration topic (both linked on this row). The platform blueprint's verification stages are API-agnostic; only the acquisition layer is interface-specific (SG-34). What is missing: The replacement interface's eligibility terms, rate limits and timeline as they apply to this account - and which interface the owner wants the platform built against. How to find it out: Read the deprecation notice's replacement guidance (linked), check the account's eligibility for the successor API, then record the decision in data/platform.json and update the blueprint page."}}]},{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","@id":"https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/#breadcrumb","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Questions and gaps","item":"https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/questions.html"}]}]}</script>
+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/questions.html#faq","url":"https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/questions.html","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Which exact manufacturer model number does each of the three supplied Amazon links point to?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: All three link.amazon short links resolve to the same three ASINs on every recorded fetch: B0CDY277D3, B09B7SB46R and B08DKYBTPH (7 observation rounds, 2026-11-13 to 2026-11-15). The three destination pages carry the listing titles recorded in data/products.json, except the Emeril title, which changed between 2026-11-14 and 2026-11-15 (flag F38). The bare 9-character codes in the short links (B04G1MEwY, B02NbDxp3, B00hJ57DV) are not ASINs and return 'Page Not Found' when used as /dp/ identifiers. Re-tested 2026-11-15. What is missing: Nothing for the model step. For the link attribution: a statement from the account holder, or the Associates Central link builder showing which ASIN each short link was created for. Neither is reachable from this repository. How to find it out: Done 2026-11-16 by session ba29c641: the Product information block was read off all three listing pages and published on the product pages and in reports/asin-model-mapping-2026-11-16.md. The residual part - link-to-ASIN intent - can only be settled by whoever created the short links."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is the Chefman 8 Qt listing the RJ38-SQSS-8T the site maps it to, or the RJ38-8T-BLACK that documents the 450 F Hi-Fry?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The Amazon listing title contains '450 F Hi-Fry Option' and 'Bake, Dehydrate, Frozen'. The chefman.com page for RJ38-SQSS-8T states a maximum temperature of 400 F. Chefman publishes a separate RJ38-8T-BLACK that documents 450 F Hi-Fry and exactly those four functions (flag F33). What is missing: A statement from Chefman or the seller about which SKU sits behind ASIN B08DKYBTPH, or the model plate on a unit bought against it. The site will not pick between its own documents without one. How to find it out: Done 2026-11-16 by session arena/ba29c641 (Part Number and Max Temperature rows read from the listing's Product information block; see reports/asin-model-mapping-2026-11-16.md). What a script cannot do: open the box."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is the Amazon Associates account approved, in review, or closed - and when did the current review window start?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The Standard Commission Income Statement can be read without signing in, so the rate table is public. The site is built as though the account is live, but nothing in the repository records its status. What is missing: The account status and the date the window began. Both are inside Associates Central. How to find it out: Associates Central home page shows status directly. Record the status and, if in review, the date the application was submitted."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which Associates tag is inside the three supplied short links, and does it belong to this account?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The links are link.amazon short links, so the redirect target is observable but the embedded tag is not. The redirect target carries Amazon's own parameters (lv=shuf, btn_ref=srctok-..., plpRedirect=mhFallback) but no tag= parameter is visible in the landing URL. What is missing: The full tagged URL. It is inside the short link and is only visible from the account that created it, or by reading the click report. How to find it out: Option 1: open each link while signed in to Associates Central and confirm the click appears against your tracking ID. Option 2: rebuild each link as a full amazon.com URL with your tag and use that instead."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you have, or can you get, Creators API access?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The rate table and all program documents are readable without signing in. The site currently displays no Amazon price, rating or review count on any page, and that is correct rather than an omission. What is missing: Whether the account qualifies for Creators API, and whether any credentials exist. How to find it out: Associates Central > Tools > Creators API (or PA API). Access requires qualifying sales. Record approved / not approved."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the product weight of the Emeril 26 QT French Door, and is the weight published for the two Chefman models the product weight or the packaged weight?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Exterior dimensions are published for all three products from official sources: the Chefman 6 Qt from its product page (S10), the Chefman 8 Qt from its product page (S12), and the Emeril 26 QT from its product page (S14). Warranty terms are published for all three: 1-year Chefman assurance (S10), limited 1-year from the owner's manual (S13), and 1-year warranty (S14). Product weight is published for both Chefman models from their specification blocks: 13.3 lb / 6.03 kg for the 6 Qt (S10) and 12.8 lb / 5.8 kg for the 8 Qt (S12). The same brand's own commerce endpoint, read on 2026-11-15, reports 14.51 lb / 6,582 g for RJ38-SQPF-3TDB-2W (S32) and 13.4 lb / 6,078 g for RJ38-SQSS-8T (S33) - both higher, and neither page says which figure is the product and which is the package (flag F51). NARROWED 2026-11-15 (arena/63a2c638, flag F52): this row previously asked for dimensions, weight and warranty for all three products and was filed as 'not_stated'. Eight of those nine cells were already published from official sources in data/products.json. Only one cell is genuinely open. NEW EVIDENCE 2026-11-16 (arena/b23f12f0, round 11): the brand storefront's own machine-readable product record (S35) reports a variant weight of 27.04 lb / 12,265 g for SKU 4-00675-02A-VN. This is recorded as a candidate, not published as the Emeril's product weight, because the endpoint does not say whether its weight field is the product or the packaged weight - the same ambiguity flag F51 records for the two Chefman models, whose specification-block weights (13.3 lb and 12.8 lb) are both lower than their own endpoint weights (14.51 lb and 13.4 lb). Converting a shipping figure into a product specification is exactly the inference this project refuses to make. What is missing: Two things, and only two. (1) The Emeril 26 QT product weight - neither the brand product page (S14) nor the owner's manual (S15) states one. (2) A statement from Chefman, on either page, saying whether its published weight is the product or the packaged weight. How to find it out: For the Emeril: read the 'Product information' or specification table on the brand page and the specifications page of the owner's manual; if neither states a weight, leave the cell blank and say so rather than converting a shipping weight. For the Chefman labels: quote the sentence next to the weight in each Specifications block, and quote the endpoint field, then decide which one the site publishes - or publish both with their labels."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Should this be one public site, and should it live on a domain you own?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: GitHub Pages is configured to build from main at the repository root, legacy build type, HTTPS enforced. Eight additional directories carry their own index.html and are served under the same origin. The repository already generates robots.txt and a sitemap.xml for the root build only (23 URLs). What is missing: Whether the annexes are meant to be public, and whether a custom domain is acceptable. How to find it out: A decision, not a lookup: keep the annexes public as an audit trail, or publish only the root and keep the rest in the repository. Record the answer here."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What may this site measure, and whose consent does that require?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The site is fully static and runs no client-side tracking of any kind. Amazon reports clicks and orders per tracking ID without any on-site script. Section 2(b) permits additional sub-tag IDs to separate traffic sources. What is missing: Whether third-party analytics are wanted at all, and if so which lawful basis applies for EU visitors. How to find it out: Decide between Associates sub-tags alone (no disclosure change) and a cookieless analytics script (small disclosure update). The choice is in the Suggestions page, SG-08."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who is the named author, and what may the site honestly claim about how content is produced?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: No page on the site names an author or describes the production method. The footer states no hands-on testing is claimed anywhere on the site. 16 CFR 255.5 requires disclosure of material connections that a significant minority of the audience would not expect. What is missing: The name to publish, whether an editorial human reviews each page before publication, and whether AI assistance will be disclosed. How to find it out: A decision. If nothing else, publishing the method is enough: 'every number is quoted from a manufacturer document or the CFR, drafted with AI assistance and checked against the source'. That sentence is true today and is not currently on the site."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which search intent does each existing article own, and who is allowed to write the next one?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Every published article is source-linked and none claims hands-on testing (live count on the articles index). One earlier pass already deleted three orphaned drafts for exactly this reason and preserved their text in session-7111e2b6/articles-superseded.json. What is missing: A written assignment of one primary intent per article, and a rule that a new article may not target an intent already claimed. How to find it out: Thirty minutes with the article index. Assign each one intent, publish the map, and make a duplicate a build failure."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which category has Amazon actually assigned to each ASIN, and therefore which commission rate applies?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The published Table 1 lists Physical Books, Kitchen and Automotive at 4.50%, and All Other Categories at 4.00%. Amazon does not publish an ASIN-to-category mapping, which is why the site shows both candidate rows. All three listings display the same breadcrumb: Home & Kitchen > Kitchen & Dining > Small Appliances > Fryers > Air Fryers. The same breadcrumb does NOT settle this question. The Commission Income Statement's categories (S3) are commission categories defined by the Associates programme; browse nodes are a shopper-navigation taxonomy; Amazon publishes no mapping between them. The breadcrumb begins 'Home & Kitchen' while S3 carries both 'Kitchen' at 4.50% and 'Home' at 3.00%, so it cannot even indicate which of the two it resembles. See flag F53. RESOLVED 2026-11-16 (arena/b23f12f0, round 11, flag F65): the Emeril browse node is no longer unknown. ASIN B09B7SB46R was fetched successfully on 2026-11-16 and rendered the breadcrumb 'Home & Kitchen > Kitchen & Dining > Small Appliances > Fryers > Air Fryers' (S28) - identical to the two Chefman paths - and the same breadcrumb was reached independently through the supplied short link in the same session (S17). The 2026-11-15 reading that called the block 'per-ASIN' was wrong: on 2026-11-16 the same ASIN fetched cleanly on the first attempt while one of the two ASINs that had worked the previous day was served the checkpoint on its first attempt and returned the listing only on a retry. The checkpoint is intermittent per request, so a failed fetch is not evidence about the ASIN. NEW 2026-11-15 (arena/63a2c638): the two Chefman listings each render an Amazon browse-node breadcrumb reading 'Home & Kitchen > Kitchen & Dining > Small Appliances > Fryers > Air Fryers' (S16 for B0CDY277D3, S29 for B08DKYBTPH). That breadcrumb is now published as a sourced fact on each product page. UPDATED 2026-11-16 (arena/b23f12f0): all three listings now have a confirmed browse node, and all three are the same node. That closes the last blank cell in the commission question's evidence, but it does not close the question - see the entry above on why a browse path is not a commission category. Re-read again the same day by arena/ba29c641 fetching the /dp/ URL directly: its Best Sellers Rank line reads '#214 in Kitchen & Dining' and '#6 in Air Fryers' (node 17659096011), agreeing with both earlier reads. Three independent routes, one classification — and still no commission category. What is missing: Confirmation of the category Amazon applies at commission time. The breadcrumb is strong evidence; the Earnings report is proof. How to find it out: The rate is not discoverable from any public page - it is only observable after the fact, in the Associates reports, where each qualifying purchase shows the rate actually applied. Read the category column on the first few real orders and the question answers itself. Publishing a rate before then means publishing a guess about how Amazon classified the product."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is there any published statement on this site that a live official source contradicts today?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: This pass fetched all three supplied links, the Commission Income Statement, the Program Policies page, 16 CFR 255.5, 16 CFR Part 465 and both manufacturers' product pages on 2026-11-15. One published value was found stale and is corrected in this pass: the Emeril listing title, which now reads '24 One-Touch Preset Options' where the stored value read '24 cooking functions' (flag F38). One citation defect was found and corrected: the source record S16 is registered as the B0CDY277D3 URL but was cited for all three listing titles, so two of the three citations pointed at a different product's page (flag F39). One register-integrity defect was found and corrected: two different rows were both numbered F36 (flag F40). What is missing: A standing re-read schedule. Review counts and listing titles change without notice, and no page on the site currently records when a value was last read except the re-verification ledger. How to find it out: Re-read every value on a fixed cadence and publish the date next to each volatile figure. The re-verification ledger is the right home for this; it needs a schedule rather than a one-off pass."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What exactly does the current application-review process require, in its own words?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The page existed at the recorded URL and was quoted by an earlier pass with a logged fetch. This pass attempted the page and did not get content back, so it is flagged rather than repeated as verified. What is missing: A current read of the review-process page, which requires loading it in a browser. How to find it out: Open the source link and read the qualifying-criteria section. Record what it says today, with the date."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the actual conversion rate for these three products, and what would one sale be worth?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Manufacturer list prices are $119.99, $199.99 and $119.99 USD, each sourced to the brand page. At the Kitchen rate of 4.50%, a sale at list price is worth roughly $5.40, $9.00 and $5.40 respectively before returns. No sales data exists in this repository. What is missing: Real click and order counts. These live in Associates Central and cannot be obtained by research. How to find it out: Associates Central > Reports. Until there is history, label any projection as a scenario with its assumed conversion rate stated."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What commission rate does Amazon's published table give for kitchen appliances, and does that rate apply to these three listings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The published Table 1 lists Physical Books, Kitchen and Automotive at 4.50%, and All Other Categories at 4.00%. Amazon does not publish an ASIN-to-category mapping, which is why the site shows both candidate rows. All three listings display the same breadcrumb: Home & Kitchen > Kitchen & Dining > Small Appliances > Fryers > Air Fryers. Re-read 2026-11-15: the breadcrumb was read directly on all three ASINs - B0CDY277D3, B09B7SB46R and B08DKYBTPH - not only on the Emeril listing, which was the limit of the earlier observation recorded in the annex register's F-06. What is missing: Confirmation of the category Amazon applies at commission time. No public source states it for an ASIN; the Earnings report names it only on a shipped line. How to find it out: Associates Central > Reports > Earnings shows the category applied to each shipped item. Before any sale exists, the breadcrumb is the best available evidence and is recorded as evidence rather than as confirmation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"May this site display Amazon prices, star ratings or review counts?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Program Policies 2(b), read verbatim 2026-11-15: \"Product prices and availability may vary from time to time. ... your Site may only show prices and availability if: (a) we serve the link in which that price and availability data are displayed, or (b) you obtain Product pricing and availability data via Creators API or PA API and you comply with the requirements regarding use of Creators API or PA API in the License.\" Program Policies 6(t), read verbatim 2026-11-15: \"You will not display or otherwise use any of our customer reviews or star ratings, in part or in whole, on your Site unless you have obtained a link to that customer review or star rating through Creators API or PA API and you comply with the requirements for Creators API and PA API described in the License.\" The build has an executable guard for this: a build check refuses to emit any price tracking or price alerting element, and the site carries no price or rating values at all. What is missing: Nothing about the rule itself - it is a settled reading of the current policy text. What is missing is whether the account will ever hold the API access that would unlock the display. How to find it out: Not applicable to the rule. If the account later obtains Creators API or PA API access, sections 2(b) and 6(t) must be re-read against that API's own licence terms before any value is displayed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is this site's product coverage actually based on?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Every populated specification row in data/products.json names at least one official source, and build.py fails the build if a row carries a value with no source. All three owner's manuals were read end to end on 2026-11-15 and every value attributed to them was found in them. The site's own footer, every article header and the three product pages each state that nothing was tested. What is missing: Nothing about the coverage. What the site cannot answer is how any of these machines actually cooks, because nobody here has used one - and that gap is the honest limit of every comparison on the site. How to find it out: Only by buying and testing each unit and recording the test. The conflicts that a reader is most likely to hit - which Chefman 8-quart model the listing actually is, what the Emeril's preset count really says - can only be settled by reading the model plate on the unit, or by the owner's own test."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can the brief's AI agent read and analyse Amazon's own product data?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: IP License section 1, read verbatim 2026-11-15: \"This license does not include any downloading, copying or other use of Program Content for the benefit of any third party, or any use of data mining, robots, or similar data gathering and extraction tools.\" IP License section 1, final paragraph, read verbatim 2026-11-15: \"will not allow any third party to, use Program Content to, directly or indirectly, develop or improve large language or multimodal models, machine learning models or related technology.\" IP License section 2(e), read verbatim 2026-11-15: \"You will not, without our express prior written approval, access or use Creators API, PA API or Data Feeds for the purpose of aggregating, analyzing, extracting, or repurposing any Product Advertising Content or in connection with any software or other application intended for use by persons or entities that offer products on an Amazon Site, or in the direct training or fine-tuning of a machine learning and/or foundational model.\" The repository already runs its automation on first-party and manufacturer data only; flag F8 records the same constraint as a platform guardrail. What is missing: Nothing in the rule. What is missing is Amazon's prior written approval, which section 2(e) names as the only route to the restricted use and which does not exist today. How to find it out: Only Amazon can grant it. Until approval exists in writing, the constraint stands and the platform must run on manufacturer and first-party data."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How often should this site's published values be re-read?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Every rating figure the site publishes already carries its read date, and flag F19 records why. Re-read 2026-11-16: the Chefman 6 Qt counter read 1,307 (from 1,305) and the 8 Qt read 717 (from 716), while both averages held at 4.6. The two rows the site publishes were refreshed to this reading. The 2026-11-15 pass re-read every manufacturer page and every owner's manual and found no published value to be wrong. Application Review Process, re-read live 2026-11-16: the 180-day / three-sale rule, the ~10-post rule, the 60-day content-recency rule and the ~500-follower social threshold are all confirmed verbatim. That closes the 'not re-read' part of flag F56. The 2026-11-16 pass re-read the Standard Commission Income Statement, the Program Policies page, 16 CFR Part 255, both Chefman product pages, the Emeril product page and all three supplied short links, and found no published value wrong. What is missing: The owner's chosen refresh interval for each class of value - Amazon states the review-side recency window, not a maintenance schedule for a documentation site. Nothing on the site has gone stale: the most recent read of every published value is 2026-11-16. How to find it out: Set an interval per value class and let the build report anything past it - which is SG-18. The Application Review page no longer needs re-reading for its own sake, only when Amazon changes it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which country and marketplace is this site actually promoting, and who states it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: All three supplied link.amazon short links resolve to amazon.com, the US marketplace, and all three listings sit under the same US browse-node path. Participation Requirements 1, read verbatim 2026-11-15: \"If you are a non-US person participating in the Associates Program, you agree that unless you have otherwise notified us in your tax information you will perform all services under the Agreement outside the United States.\" The commission table the site publishes is the US Standard Commission Income Statement. What is missing: The operator's actual country of residence, stated once by the operator, and the marketplace the site is intended to promote. The two statements already in the repository are agent-session observations, not owner statements. How to find it out: The owner states their country once, one register row cites it, and the other wording is corrected to match. The Associates application itself, which asks for tax information and a bank account, is the authority on whether a non-US applicant is accepted."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"If the research agent is automated, what is it allowed to touch, and what must it call itself?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: IP License §4, read verbatim 2026-11-16: \"The terms in this section (\"Agent Terms\") apply if you use, allow, enable, or cause the deployment of an Agent to access, use, or interact with the Program Content. For the purposes of these Agent Terms, \"Agent\" means any software or service that takes autonomous or semi-autonomous action on behalf of, or at the instruction of, any person or entity.\" Same section, verbatim: \"In all HTTP/HTTPS requests, identify that the request is from an Agent and disclose the name of the Agent by including the following in the request's user agent string: \"Agent/[agent name]\" (e.g., Agent/AmazonAgent)\"; \"Not conceal or obfuscate that any access, use, or interactions are from an Agent…\"; \"Respond truthfully to any question or prompt seeking to determine if interactions are coming from a human or a computer.\" Same section, verbatim: \"At our sole discretion, we may limit, including by technical measures, whether and how any Agent accesses, uses, and interacts with Program Content.\" Participation Requirements §5, read verbatim 2026-11-16: \"You will not, without express prior written approval, use, or allow any third party to use, any Special Links or Program Content to develop machine learning models or related technology.\" IP License §1, read verbatim 2026-11-16: \"We will not allow any third party to, use Program Content to, directly or indirectly, develop or improve large language or multimodal models, machine learning models or related technology.\" The published site consumes no Program Content at all, so no Agent Term is triggered by anything it renders today. The clauses matter for the proposed research agent, not for the pages. What is missing: Two things, and neither can be settled from a public page. First, whether a public Amazon product page fetched by an automated tool counts as \"Program Content\" — the IP License defines Program Content around Creators API, PA API and Data Feeds, and the policies page does not say whether an ordinary listing page is inside that definition. Second, the agent's declared name. A third point is only partly legible: IP License §4(a) refers to \"the requirements in section 3 of these Agent Terms\", and section 3 of the Agent Terms was not readable in the render used for this pass (flag F62), so the full list of identification requirements is not yet on the record. How to find it out: Read IP License §4 in a browser, in full, including the section 3 cross-reference. Then ask Amazon one question in writing — through Associates Central support — and keep the answer: \"Does fetching a public amazon.com product detail page with an automated tool count as accessing Program Content under IP License §4, and is a User-Agent of Agent/zone1-research acceptable?\" Until Amazon answers, the conservative design is the one in SG-22: identify as an agent, never defeat a bot check, and keep the research stage off Program Content entirely."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which images may this site publish, and whose permission covers them?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Amazon's Operating Agreement licenses Program Content (including images) to Associates under the Agreement's rules. The FTC requires clear disclosure of material connections, which applies to imagery as well as text. What is missing: A recorded decision on which image source the site uses, and the rights statement covering each image. How to find it out: Read Amazon's Program Content/IP provisions in the Operating Agreement and Program Policies, then decide between owner photos, licensed imagery, Program Content images, or text-and-diagrams only."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who decides the publishing calendar — including seasonal pieces such as the gift guide — and what is each piece's deadline?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The Standard Commission Income Statement, read live 2026-11-16, pays 4.50% on Kitchen and 4.00% on All Other Categories. The topic register now holds three seasonal rows: TP-15 (gift guide, published 2026-11-16), TP-16 (hosting capacity) and TP-17 (deal-season checks), both proposed. The application review requires site content to be recent — 'generally within the last 60 days' — and looks for about 10 posts. Nothing in the repository records any owner statement about seasonal content, deadlines or sign-off. What is missing: A one-line owner decision: which seasonal window(s) to target, how many pieces per window, who approves a publish, and the date each piece must be ready by. 'No seasonal content' is also a usable answer — it would retire TP-16 and TP-17 instead of leaving them pending. How to find it out: The owner states the calendar once — in COORDINATION.md, in a dated reply, or in the handover sheet — and the editorial queue's blocked_by rows are cleared against that statement. The search window itself is not in dispute; only the owner's appetite and sign-off are missing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who signs off on the suggestion queue, and on what cadence?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Every row on the suggestions page is explicitly unimplemented and carries options, a recommendation, effort and risk. Several earlier flags were dispositioned as 'the owner's call' (for example F59), which is this same gap surfacing elsewhere. Decision path P5 (session arena/19d92a81) groups the reader-facing polish suggestions so one 'yes' covers eight rows. What is missing: A named decider, a review cadence, and what 'approved' means - approved to build, or approved to test? How to find it out: One message from the owner: a cadence (for example '30 minutes monthly, I mark each row approve/reject/defer') and what approval means. The page can render an approval-state column as soon as the data carries it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is 'Zone1 Kitchen' the final brand name for this site?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The root site's titles and organization schema use 'Zone1 Kitchen' with the tagline 'Air-fryer decisions, backed by sources'. The /s2 annex publishes under 'Zone1 - Amazon Associates platform'. The README titles the project 'Zone1 - verified Amazon affiliate product intelligence'. What is missing: The owner's decision on the one name reader-facing surfaces should carry. How to find it out: The owner states the name in one line; a single commit then aligns titles, og tags, the annex header and the README. SG-28 carries the implementation options."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which API will the automation platform call, now that PA-API 5 has a deprecation notice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Amazon publishes a PA-API 5 Deprecation Notice and a Product Advertising API registration topic (both linked on this row). The platform blueprint's verification stages are API-agnostic; only the acquisition layer is interface-specific (SG-34). What is missing: The replacement interface's eligibility terms, rate limits and timeline as they apply to this account - and which interface the owner wants the platform built against. How to find it out: Read the deprecation notice's replacement guidance (linked), check the account's eligibility for the successor API, then record the decision in data/platform.json and update the blueprint page."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which product were the two links submitted on 2026-11-17 created to send a reader to, and does this site publish them at all?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: Both links were re-read live on 2026-11-17: PI-04 lands on /dp/B0DHZTCNR5 and PI-05 on /dp/B0DZFD6R9Z, each carrying the same lv=shuf / plpRedirect=mhFallback fallback markers recorded for the original three links. The served listing titles name a ThermoMaven instant-read thermometer and a CHEF iQ Sense Gen3 wireless thermometer; Amazon files both under Kitchen Utensils & Gadgets > Thermometers & Timers > Meat, not under Fryers > Air Fryers. The brands' own pages were read the same day: CHEF iQ states model CQ60-3C-Z3-E with five sensors and a 1,000 °F ambient rating for the iQ Sense; ThermoMaven's site names its instant-read models F1 Pro and F1 Turbo and does not use the '1 Sec' label the observed listing carries. The intended-product fields on both records are blank by design, and the observed destinations are stored in a separate field so the two can never be merged. What is missing: A statement from the account holder of which product each link was made for — ideally the official /dp/ URL written into the record, with the date it was checked in the account holder's own browser. How to find it out: Open each submitted link in the browser signed in to the Amazon account, read the listing title and the model line in Product information, and compare it with the intended product. If it matches, fill the four intended_product fields in data/product-intent-confirmations.json and rebuild; if it does not, write down what was meant and the register will show both."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the selected tracking ID shopnow047c-20 exist on the Associates account, and is it the ID attached to the published links?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The ID was supplied by the account holder on 2026-11-17 and recorded in the register as user-provided and unvalidated. Its shape matches the only example Amazon publishes for a tracking ID — storeid-20, with sub-tag IDs of the form storeid-1-20 — read verbatim from the help node on 2026-11-17. The help node states no validation rule and publishes no list of IDs per account, so a shape match cannot confirm existence. The build now enforces the consequence: while the gate reads closed, every outbound link ships exactly as supplied and a tagged anchor fails the build. Read the same day: Amazon's help node states that a tracking ID does not need to appear in the click-through URL for the sale to be attributed, because the session is tagged on Amazon's side (S39). The clean landing URLs recorded against these links therefore prove nothing either way, and an earlier pass's reliance on that absence is corrected on the register page rather than edited out of history. What is missing: Two observations only the account holder can make: the ID listed at Associates Central > Manage your Tracking ID, and a click-through appearing under that ID in the report. How to find it out: Open Manage your Tracking ID (the screen S6 and S40 both name) and check for shopnow047c-20. Then click one published product link and confirm the click appears in that ID's Tracking ID Summary Report — the URL you land on is not the evidence, per S39. Write the date into associates_central_confirmation_date, cite the screen as the source, and set tagging_gate.state to open."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do the five defined events' counts go, and who is allowed to read them?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The event dictionary, the collector's posture and the ledger schema are in data/measurement.json and rendered on the measurement page. The collector sets no cookie, makes no request, and stores no reader identifier; the footer statement was rewritten on 2026-11-17 to say exactly that. GitHub Pages serves static files, so there is no endpoint for the browser to post to, and the register records the endpoint as null rather than inventing one. Amazon's own per-tracking-ID reports answer the channel question with no code on this site at all. What is missing: A hosting decision (does the owner want a first-party collector, and where), a written retention and IP-handling policy, and a named reader of the numbers. How to find it out: Answer Q-28 first, because a tracking-ID report answers most of what an endpoint would, for free. If an endpoint is still wanted, choose any first-party destination the owner controls, write the four items in the deployment boundary before it accepts a single event, and rebuild so the privacy statement describes what now happens."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which authorized first-party report will fill the campaign ledger, and when does its reporting window open?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The eight columns and their filling rules are defined in data/measurement.json and published on the measurement page. The ledger has zero rows, and the reason is published next to it rather than hidden. Reports are read in Associates Central by the account holder; no public page exposes them. The browser counter is never summed into the ledger, and the build rejects a row that tries. What is missing: Access to Associates Central > Reports, an approved account, and at least one reporting window with activity under a confirmed tracking ID. How to find it out: After Q-28 and Q-03 are answered, download the Earnings report filtered to the confirmed tracking ID and transcribe one dated row into the register: report date, report name, tracking ID, sessions, clicks, items shipped, orders, and who transcribed it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which commission category does Amazon actually apply to the two newly submitted listings, and does the site's Kitchen argument extend to them?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What we know: The Standard Commission Income Statement was read in full without a login and is registered as a source; it publishes rates by category. The three air-fryer candidate destinations were observed under Small Appliances > Fryers > Air Fryers; the two new ones under Kitchen Utensils & Gadgets > Thermometers & Timers > Meat. Amazon does not publish the category it assigns to an ASIN for commission purposes, and no public page maps a browse path to a rate line. The site therefore keeps its per-product rate cells as candidates with the table cited, and does not extend them. What is missing: The category named on a shipped line in the owner's own Earnings report — the only document that states, for a real transaction, which rate applied. How to find it out: After the first attributed sale under a confirmed tracking ID, read the category named on that report line and record it against the ASIN in the register. Until then the honest cell stays a candidate with its source, not a number."}}]},{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","@id":"https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/#breadcrumb","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Questions and gaps","item":"https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/questions.html"}]}]}</script>
