+  <p>Turn the two things the owner supplied on 2026-11-17 — two more product links and a selected tracking ID — into a site whose commercial links are attributable and whose product set is a decision rather than an accident.</p>
+  <p class="small"><strong>Contains.</strong> <a class="src" href="#sg-36">SG-36</a> <a class="src" href="#sg-37">SG-37</a> <a class="src" href="#sg-38">SG-38</a> <a class="src" href="#sg-39">SG-39</a> <a class="src" href="#sg-40">SG-40</a></p>
+  <p class="small"><strong>Effort.</strong> S (two of the five rows are a browser and five minutes; the other three are reading and choosing)</p>
+  <p class="small"><strong>Must be true first.</strong> Nothing outside the owner&#x27;s own screens. The register, the gate and the measurement layer are already built and enforced by the build, so this path is answering questions, not waiting on code.</p>
+  <p class="small"><strong>What you get.</strong> Every submitted link either confirmed or explicitly parked; tagged canonical links for the confirmed ones, emitted by the build and not by hand; a five-event measurement dictionary with a working local counter and an empty ledger whose rules make a fabricated row impossible; and a topic queue that says what it is waiting for.</p>
+  <p class="small"><strong>What it does not fix.</strong> It will not produce a single conversion figure. Orders come from a report this repository cannot read, and the ledger stays empty until a human with account access writes its first row — which is the point of the design, not a shortfall.</p>
+  <p class="small"><strong>Why it sits here in the order.</strong> It is the only path where the owner&#x27;s own action unblocks the largest number of other rows: SG-37 gates every revenue claim the site could ever make, and SG-36 decides what the other five paths are written for.</p>
+  <p class="rec"><strong>Pick this if.</strong> You intend to earn from this site rather than merely publish it.</p>
