+<article class="sug" id="sg-37">
+  <div class="sug-head">
+    <div>
+      <div class="tiny muted">SG-37 · Attribution</div>
+      <h3>Confirm the selected tracking ID in Associates Central, then open the gate — the build already refuses to move before you do</h3>
+    </div>
+    <div class="score" title="impact 5 x confidence 5 - this pass's judgement, not a measurement">
+      <strong>25</strong><span>priority</span>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+  <p><strong>The problem.</strong> The account holder selected a tracking ID, shopnow047c-20, on 2026-11-17. Ten passes could only record that the tag inside the short links was unobservable; now there is a named ID, and the remaining unknown is much smaller and much easier: does that ID exist on the account, and does it appear in that ID&#x27;s report after a click. The site cannot check either, and until it is checked every published link stays untagged — which is the correct behaviour, not an omission: a tag that does not exist attributes nothing, and a destination the publisher invented looks settled while earning nothing. What is new in this pass is that the refusal is now machinery: a field in data/product-intent-confirmations.json plus check_special_link_gate(), which fails the build if a tagged anchor appears while the gate reads closed, or if the gate is opened without a confirmation date and source. Two help nodes read on the same day also narrow what this register may claim from a URL: Amazon states that the ID need not appear in the click-through URL for attribution to work (S39), so the clean landing URLs are not evidence against the links — the confirmation is a report row, read in the Tracking ID Summary Report (S40).</p>
+  <div class="evidence"><strong>Evidence.</strong> <a class="src-link" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GK5TZZ4AWML2QSLA" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon — what tracking IDs are, with the storeid-20 example (re-read 2026-11-17) ↗</a> <a class="src-link" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GK5TZZ4AWML2QSLA" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon — where tracking IDs are created and managed (the help node names the screen; the console itself is behind the account login) ↗</a> <a class="src-link" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/operating/policies" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Program Policies — links must carry the Associate&#x27;s tag ↗</a> <a class="src-link" href="https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/links.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The register page carrying the gate state ↗</a> <a class="src-link" href="https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/flagged.html#f78" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Flag F78 ↗</a> <a class="src-link" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GF4RJEQNXSMR7SP3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon — a tracking ID need not appear in the URL to attribute (S39) ↗</a> <a class="src-link" href="https://affiliate-program.amazon.com/help/node/topic/GJDYPQZK6E37RLPU" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon — multiple tracking IDs, the 100-ID limit, the summary report (S40) ↗</a></div>
+  <p class="label">Paths you could take</p>
+  <ol class="paths"><li><strong>A. Five minutes in Associates Central, then open the gate for the records whose product you confirmed</strong><div class="tiny" style="margin:.15rem 0 .35rem">Effort S · risk low · reversible</div><div class="small">Confirm shopnow047c-20 is listed; write the confirmation date into the register; set tagging_gate.state to open; rebuild. The build emits canonical /dp/&lt;confirmed ASIN&gt;?tag=… CTAs for confirmed rows only, and refuses everything else.</div></li><li><strong>B. Confirm the ID, and keep the supplied short links as-is</strong><div class="tiny" style="margin:.15rem 0 .35rem">Effort S · risk low · reversible</div><div class="small">If the short links already carry this ID inside the shortener, nothing needs to change on the site — verify by clicking one and checking the report row. Cheaper than a rewrite, but the ambiguity stays: the destination is still whatever the linkener decides at click time.</div></li><li><strong>C. Leave everything as it is</strong><div class="tiny" style="margin:.15rem 0 .35rem">Effort 0 · risk medium · reversible</div><div class="small">Status quo: untagged links, no attribution, no evidence the site can ever earn. Fine for a publication whose value is its sources; not fine for an affiliate site.</div></li></ol>
+  <p class="rec"><strong>This pass would pick:</strong> A, then test one link against the report before the other four are rebuilt on the same assumption.</p>
+  <p class="small"><strong>Why.</strong> This is the one item on the queue where a five-minute check by the only person with account access converts a whole site from &#x27;content that might earn&#x27; to &#x27;content that is attributed&#x27;. The build has already done the engineering half.</p>
+  <p class="small"><strong>Cost of not doing it.</strong> High in the narrow sense that matters: unattributed clicks are the same as no clicks.</p>
+  <p class="small"><strong>Blocked by:</strong> <a href="questions.html#q-28" class="src">Q-28</a></p>
+</article>
