+<article class="sug" id="sg-36">
+  <div class="sug-head">
+    <div>
+      <div class="tiny muted">SG-36 · Product set and scope</div>
+      <h3>Settle what the site is now that five links span two product categories</h3>
+    </div>
+    <div class="score" title="impact 5 x confidence 4 - this pass's judgement, not a measurement">
+      <strong>20</strong><span>priority</span>
+    </div>
+  </div>
+  <p><strong>The problem.</strong> The brief that supplied this repository&#x27;s products has changed the product set without saying it did. Three links submitted in November resolve to air fryers; the two submitted on 2026-11-17 resolve — as far as a fallback redirect can be said to resolve anything — to a ThermoMaven instant-read thermometer and a CHEF iQ Sense Gen3 wireless thermometer, both filed under Kitchen Utensils &amp; Gadgets &gt; Thermometers &amp; Timers &gt; Meat. The site&#x27;s masthead reads &#x27;Air-fryer field guide&#x27;, its category page is &#x27;kitchen appliances — air fryers&#x27;, and every comparison row, article and buying guide is written for that decision. This is not a data-entry task: publishing the two new items changes what the site is for, and publishing them from unconfirmed candidate destinations would build a second shallow footprint on top of the first. The register records both links with their observation fields filled and their intended-product fields blank, so the decision can be made from evidence rather than from a guess.</p>
+  <div class="evidence"><strong>Evidence.</strong> <a class="src-link" href="https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/links.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Submitted-links register, rows PI-04 and PI-05 ↗</a> <a class="src-link" href="https://kanlerxz87-cyber.github.io/Zone1/flagged.html#f76" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Flag F76 — the divergence, with the observation method ↗</a> <a class="src-link" href="https://chefiq.com/products/iq-sense" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CHEF iQ iQ Sense product page (official brand page) ↗</a> <a class="src-link" href="https://www.thermomaven.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ThermoMaven official site (its own model names) ↗</a> <a class="src-link" href="https://github.com/kanlerxz87-cyber/Zone1/blob/main/data/product-intent-confirmations.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">data/product-intent-confirmations.json ↗</a></div>
+  <p class="label">Paths you could take</p>
+  <ol class="paths"><li><strong>A. Keep the site air-fryer-only; the two links stay in the register, unpublished</strong><div class="tiny" style="margin:.15rem 0 .35rem">Effort S · risk none · reversible</div><div class="small">Nothing ships for PI-04/PI-05 beyond the register rows. The site keeps one reader intent, one internal-link graph and one set of sourced claims, and the two links remain available the moment their identity is confirmed.</div></li><li><strong>B. Stand up a second category — after the documents, not before</strong><div class="tiny" style="margin:.15rem 0 .35rem">Effort L · risk medium · reversible</div><div class="small">Read the brand pages and manuals for whichever product is confirmed, add them to the source register, then write one comparison and one guide. A second category done in this order is a publication; in the other order it is two thin sites.</div></li><li><strong>C. Follow the new links and re-plan the site around thermometers</strong><div class="tiny" style="margin:.15rem 0 .35rem">Effort L · risk high</div><div class="small">Treat the two most recent submissions as the intended direction, leave the air fryers as an archive. Cheapest to justify if the owner says so in one sentence; destructive to assume from a URL.</div></li></ol>
+  <p class="rec"><strong>This pass would pick:</strong> A. Publish nothing new from an unconfirmed identity; keep the register as the visible record and decide B or C after Q-27 is answered.</p>
+  <p class="small"><strong>Why.</strong> The site&#x27;s only durable asset is that a reader can check every row. A page about a product nobody confirmed the link points at spends that asset to gain two pages.</p>
+  <p class="small"><strong>Cost of not doing it.</strong> Medium and rising: every new article written for &#x27;three air fryers&#x27; deepens a frame the owner may have already outgrown, and re-framing later means rewriting the navigation, the category page and the internal links, not just adding pages.</p>
